
const D = {"AGENDA": [["0:00", "Welcome, purpose and how findings were reached", "10 min"], ["0:10", "Findings walk: posters by question; people add notes", "30 min"], ["0:40", "What, So What, Now What? in small groups", "40 min"], ["1:20", "1-2-4-All on the three most important recommendations", "30 min"], ["1:50", "15% Solutions: who will do what first", "20 min"], ["2:10", "Close: what changes in the report, and next steps", "10 min"]], "AIROLES": [["ToR reader", "Summarises and lists risks and gaps.", "deciding whether to bid"], ["Tree builder", "Drafts issue trees and question groupings.", "the hypothesis"], ["Matrix drafter", "Turns questions into a first matrix.", "matching methods to budget"], ["Tool drafter", "First drafts of interview guides and surveys.", "testing with people"], ["Second coder", "First-pass codes on de-identified text.", "adjudicating every code"], ["Editor", "Tightens drafts and summaries.", "every finding and number"], ["Template maker", "Contracts, trackers, guides, invoices.", "legal and tax advice"], ["Checker", "Compares drafts against the ToR and matrix.", "the final sign-off"]], "AITABS": [["good", "Good at, not good at"], ["rhythm", "The rhythm"], ["brief", "Brief it"], ["session", "Watch a session"], ["risks", "Six mistakes"], ["pact", "The pact"], ["starters", "Starters"]], "AI_EVIDENCE": ["A 2025 study tested generative models on coding news text. Without training examples, they matched human coders about as well as humans match each other (F1 around 0.70 to 0.83), but results varied by task and needed human validation.", "Use AI as a second coder and a fast drafter. Keep humans responsible for findings.", "Than and colleagues, 2025", "llmcoding"], "BLOCK5": [["Entry and contracting", [0, 2]], ["Discovery and dialogue", [3, 4]], ["Analysis and the decision to act", [4, 5]], ["Engagement and implementation", [6]], ["Extension, recycle or termination", [6]]], "BODY": [["Pick one niche and own it", "Be the first name people think of for one thing, such as MEL for state education programmes."], ["Publish on a rhythm", "A short practice note every month; one longer piece a year."], ["Speak where your clients learn", "Association events, gLOCAL, sector conferences and webinars."], ["Turn methods into tools", "Templates, checklists or platforms others can use, such as EvalCanvas."], ["Productise one service", "A retainer, a training course or MEL as a service with a fixed scope and price."], ["Give back", "Mentor, review, and contribute to your evaluation association."]], "BP7": ["Define the problem", "Disaggregate it", "Prioritise", "Build a workplan", "Analyse", "Synthesise", "Communicate"], "BP_MAP": [1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 5, 5], "BRAND": [["position", 7, "Positioning statement and brand canvas", "Positioning and niche specialisation", "One page that says who you help, with what problem, how, and what you will not do.", "The solo consultant moved from 'M&E consultant' to 'MEL systems that small education NGOs can run themselves'.", ["Fill the niche from four parts: sector, method, client and place", "List proof points you can show, not adjectives", "Write the one-sentence statement last, and test it on three people"], "Read this positioning canvas and tell me where it is vague, inflated or too broad for a client to remember: [canvas, no client names].", "position"], ["profile", 7, "Professional profile builder", "Professional-services marketing practice", "A headline, about section, featured work and keywords for your LinkedIn and one-page web profile.", "After the mid-term evaluation, the team leader rewrote the headline around livelihoods evaluation and mixed methods.", ["Write the headline from the formula: role, niche, result", "Structure the about section as problem, approach, proof, invitation", "Check every claim against your CV and your consent list"], "Review this profile as a commissioner searching for an evaluator. Which claims are unclear or unsupported, and which keywords are missing? [profile text].", "profile"], ["casestudy", 7, "Portfolio case study", "Situation, approach, result format", "A one-page, anonymised story of one assignment: context, question, approach, what changed and your role.", "The MEL set-up became a one-page case study, approved by the director, with a quote used with written permission.", ["Anonymise first, then write", "Lead with what changed, and say what you cannot claim", "Send the draft to the client and get written consent before you publish"], "Check this anonymised case study for anything that could identify the client or respondents, and for claims the evidence does not support: [case study].", "casestudy"], ["visibility", 7, "Visibility and content plan", "Visible expert research and association programmes", "Twelve months of publishing, speaking, teaching and giving back, one quarter at a time.", "The team leader planned one practice note, one session proposal, one webinar and one reviewing role in a year.", ["Pick one theme from your positioning for the year", "Plan one item per column per quarter, no more", "Review each quarter: what was read, who called, what to drop"], "Here is my 12-month visibility plan and my positioning. Is the plan realistic for someone with [days] spare days a month, and does every item support my niche? [plan].", "visibility"], ["network", 7, "Relationship map and networking tracker", "Relationship marketing and trusted-advisor practice", "A short list of the people who matter to your work, by tier, with what you can give and when you will next be in touch.", "The solo consultant listed 30 people in six tiers, and a warm introduction from the director led to a second NGO client.", ["List people in six tiers, then keep the 30 that matter most", "Note one useful thing you can give each person", "Set a light cadence and follow up within 48 hours of every meeting"], "Help me write a short, warm follow-up message to [role] after [event], offering [resource] and asking nothing in return.", "network"], ["events", 7, "Event and conference plan", "Professional association conference practice", "A one-page plan for before, during and after an event, so a conference turns into relationships and learning.", "Before a regional conference, the team leader set three goals, booked two meetings and followed up within 48 hours.", ["Before: set goals, list who to meet and submit a session if you can", "During: have conversations, not pitches, and take notes the same day", "After: follow up within 48 hours and share what you learned"], "I am attending [event]. My niche is [niche]. Suggest three realistic goals and five questions I could ask people working on [theme].", "events"]], "BRAND_CH": ["brand", "Build your name", "Build your name: be found, trusted and remembered.", "Six tools to turn good work into a name clients can find and describe: your positioning, your profile, a case study, a content plan, your relationships and the events you attend.", ["Say in one sentence who you help and how", "Show proof clients can check, with consent", "Grow relationships, not just a following"]], "BRIEF": ["The client, sector and assignment type", "The decision the findings must serve", "The ToR, or the questions, pasted in", "Your constraints: days, budget, data, politics", "What you want back, and in what format", "What the AI must not do: invent sources, quotes or numbers"], "CALCS": [["ss", "Sample size", "How many people to survey"], ["fw", "Fieldwork", "Enumerators, days and cost"], ["tl", "Timeline", "Work back from the deadline"], ["ql", "Qualitative plan", "Interviews, groups and hours"], ["inv", "Invoice", "Fee, GST and TDS"], ["cpo", "Cost per outcome", "Value for money in numbers"]], "CALC_HELP": {"ss": ["How many people do you need to survey so the results are precise enough to trust?", "A survey of a sample, not everyone, when the client needs a percentage (for example, the share of households with a bank account) or wants to compare two groups."], "fw": ["How many enumerators, field days and how much money will data collection take?", "Before you price a survey, and again when the client asks for a smaller budget."], "tl": ["Given the date the final report is due, when must each stage start?", "Before you agree a deadline, and when a client asks you to start late but finish on time."], "ql": ["How many interviews and focus groups do you need, and how many hours will they take to transcribe and analyse?", "When you plan the qualitative part of an evaluation or price it."], "inv": ["What should your invoice say, and how much will actually reach your bank?", "Every time you invoice an Indian client. Not tax advice: check rates and thresholds with a tax adviser."], "cpo": ["How much did each result cost, and is that good value?", "When a ToR asks about efficiency or value for money."]}, "CH": [["start", "Start here", "New to M&E consulting? Start here.", "What an M&E consultant does, how an assignment flows, the words you will meet first, and a 30-day plan to get from zero to your first practice assignment.", ["The decision is the product; the report is the vehicle", "Every assignment follows the same seven stages", "Learn by doing: a practice assignment is waiting"]], ["test", "The test", "Spot the risk in this ToR.", "Eight lines from realistic terms of reference. Each hides a risk that sinks assignments.", ["Most failed assignments were set up to fail before signing", "The risks hide in ordinary sentences", "Almost all of them can be fixed before you sign"]], ["kinds", "Kinds of work", "What kind of consulting is this?", "The client asks for a report. What they need may be a decision, a system or a skill. Know which role you are playing.", ["Three roles: expert, doctor, process partner", "Eight common M&E assignments", "Two examples, followed throughout"]], ["method", "The method", "Seven stages. One assignment.", "The CONSULT method takes any M&E assignment from the first ToR to findings that get used.", ["Choose before you chase", "Contract the expectations, not just the price", "Design for use from day one"]], ["science", "Why it works", "Why it works.", "It joins two traditions: how the best consultants solve problems and build trust, and the standards evaluators are held to.", ["Consulting is a relationship with phases", "Hypothesis first, then evidence", "Standards protect independence and quality"]], ["judgement", "Judgement and methods", "Judgement and methods.", "How top evaluators get from evidence to a verdict they can defend, choose a causal design that fits the question, and evaluate programmes that keep changing.", ["A judgement needs criteria and standards, not just data", "Match the causal design to the question and the budget", "Complex programmes need evaluation that adapts with them"]], ["toolkit", "Top-firm toolkit", "The top-firm toolkit.", "Twenty-one tools that strategy firms, proposal teams and evaluators rely on, explained from scratch, with a template, a worked example and an AI prompt for each.", ["Eight habits separate top firms from the rest", "Three tools for every stage of an assignment", "Each tool: what it is, a template, an example, how to use it"]], ["studio", "Engagement studio", "The engagement studio.", "Six working tools from top-firm practice. Fill them in for a live assignment and copy the result into a proposal, an inception report or a status note.", ["Frame the problem before you price it", "Make the logic visible: tree, map, storyline", "Report status the way steering committees read it"]], ["guide", "Field guide", "The field guide.", "The templates and rules to keep open on every assignment.", ["Read a ToR in ten minutes", "Proposal, budget and contract anatomy", "Inception, matrix and report structures"]], ["ai", "With AI", "Consulting with AI.", "AI makes a good consultant faster and a careless one dangerous. Client trust is the asset you protect.", ["Where AI saves real days", "Six ways it can cost you a client", "A pact you can put in a contract"]], ["workshop", "Bid desk", "The bid desk.", "Four tools for your next bid: scan a ToR, price it, set your day rate and build the evaluation matrix.", ["Flag risks in any ToR", "Estimate days and a budget", "Export an evaluation matrix"]], ["kit", "The kit", "The kit: calculators, templates and concepts.", "Everything you need to work on your own: six calculators for the numbers clients ask about, nine document templates you can download and fill, and the evaluation concepts explained in plain words.", ["Calculate samples, fieldwork, timelines and invoices yourself", "Start every document from a tested template", "Understand the concepts before a client asks"]], ["brand", "Build your name", "Build your name: be found, trusted and remembered.", "Six tools to turn good work into a name clients can find and describe: your positioning, your profile, a case study, a content plan, your relationships and the events you attend.", ["Say in one sentence who you help and how", "Show proof clients can check, with consent", "Grow relationships, not just a following"]], ["practice", "Your practice", "Build a practice, not a gig.", "Most consultants are good at the work and weak at the business around it. Both decide whether you last.", ["Position on a narrow, credible niche", "Keep a pipeline, not a hope", "Get paid on time, every time"]], ["leading", "Leading and growing", "Leading and growing.", "Top consultants lead teams, pass due diligence, negotiate well, run a room, get evidence used, and become known for one thing.", ["A firm is a promise of quality, not just more people", "Negotiate interests, not positions", "Plan for use, then build a body of work"]], ["pathway", "Your pathway", "From first assignment to global consultant.", "A five-level path from new evaluator to leading international evaluations: what you do at each level, what proves it, and the move that gets you to the next one.", ["A portfolio and referees prove competence, not a job title", "Each level is earned with evidence you can show", "Global work comes through rosters, firms and networks"]], ["launch", "Ready?", "Ready?", "Ten checks before you bid, and ten before you submit the final report.", ["Before you bid", "Before you submit", "Print it for the team"]], ["library", "Library", "The library of ideas.", "The research, standards and practices behind every stage, a glossary of every term, and a one-page cheat sheet.", ["Ideas from strategy consulting and evaluation", "A glossary in plain words", "A printable cheat sheet"]]], "CLA": [["Collaborating", "Working with the right partners and sharing what is learned."], ["Learning", "Testing the theory of change and filling knowledge gaps on purpose."], ["Adapting", "Using what is learned to change plans, budgets and activities."]], "COMP": [["Professional practice", "AEA domain", "Ethics, standards, independence, reflective practice.", "Read the UNEG ethical guidelines and the AEA principles; apply one in your next inception report.", "After each assignment, write a one-page reflection on what you would do differently."], ["Methodology", "AEA domain", "Designs, methods, sampling, analysis and judging evidence.", "Take a structured course such as IPDET or BetterEvaluation's Rainbow Framework themes.", "Co-design one evaluation with a stronger methodologist and ask for a critique."], ["Context", "AEA domain", "Sector, culture, politics, power and the people affected.", "Pick one sector and read its last five major evaluations.", "Spend time in the field with programme staff before you design tools."], ["Planning and management", "AEA domain", "Workplans, budgets, teams, risks and quality control.", "Use the RAID log and weekly status note on your next assignment.", "Price three past ToRs with the bid desk and compare with what was paid."], ["Interpersonal", "AEA domain", "Communication, facilitation, negotiation and handling conflict.", "Facilitate one validation workshop and ask two participants for feedback.", "Practise pre-wiring one hard finding with a colleague playing the client."], ["Consulting craft", "Top-firm practice", "Framing problems, issue trees, proposals, storylines, trust.", "Write a problem statement and issue tree for every new request this quarter.", "Get a red-team review on your next proposal."], ["Business of consulting", "Practice", "Niche, pipeline, pricing, contracts and getting paid.", "Write your one-sentence niche and test it on three past clients.", "Set your floor day rate with the day-rate builder."], ["Data and AI", "Practice", "Data protection, digital tools and responsible AI use.", "Add an AI and data clause to your standard contract.", "Run one AI-assisted analysis with a human second coder and compare."]], "CONCEPTS": [["Outputs, outcomes and impact", "Outputs are what a programme delivers (trainings held). Outcomes are the changes that follow (women start saving). Impact is the longer-term change (household income rises).", "A training programme reports 500 women trained (output), 300 now save monthly (outcome), and incomes rise after two years (impact).", "Reporting outputs as if they were outcomes."], ["Attribution and contribution", "Attribution asks how much of a change was caused by the programme. Contribution asks whether the programme plausibly helped cause it, alongside other factors.", "Incomes rose, but so did crop prices. Attribution needs a comparison group; contribution builds a case from evidence and rules out other explanations.", "Claiming attribution without a comparison group."], ["Counterfactual", "What would have happened without the programme. You cannot observe it directly, so you estimate it, usually with a comparison group.", "Similar villages without the programme saw incomes rise 8%; programme villages rose 20%. The counterfactual is about 8%.", "Treating 'before and after' as if it were 'with and without'."], ["Baseline, midline and endline", "Measurements at the start, middle and end of a programme, using the same indicators and methods so you can compare.", "Savings rates measured in 2024 (baseline), 2025 (midline) and 2027 (endline).", "Changing the questions between rounds, so results cannot be compared."], ["SMART indicators", "Indicators that are specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound.", "'Share of group members saving at least monthly, by district, measured annually' instead of 'improved savings'.", "Indicators no one can measure with the data available."], ["Disaggregation", "Breaking results down by groups such as gender, age, district, disability or income.", "Average income rose 15%, but for the poorest fifth it rose only 3%.", "Reporting only the average, which can hide who was left out."], ["Sampling", "Choosing who to ask. Random sampling lets you generalise; stratified sampling guarantees groups are covered; cluster sampling saves travel; purposive sampling picks information-rich cases for qualitative work.", "Randomly choose 20 villages, then 20 households in each (a two-stage cluster sample).", "Generalising from a purposive or convenience sample."], ["Margin of error and confidence", "A survey result is an estimate. 'Plus or minus 5% at 95% confidence' means that if you repeated the survey many times, 95% of the results would fall within 5 points of the true value.", "62% save monthly, plus or minus 5 points: the true value is likely between 57% and 67%.", "Comparing two groups whose difference is smaller than the margin of error."], ["Statistical and practical significance", "Statistically significant means a difference is unlikely to be chance. Practically significant means it is big enough to matter for decisions.", "A 1% income difference can be statistically significant in a huge sample but too small to matter.", "Treating 'significant' as 'important'."], ["Validity and reliability", "Validity: are you measuring what you intend to? Reliability: would you get the same answer if you measured again in the same way?", "A question about 'income' that respondents interpret as 'salary' misses farm income (a validity problem).", "Testing tools only for reliability, not for meaning."], ["Bias", "Systematic error. Common types: selection bias (who you reach), response bias (telling you what you want to hear), recall bias (forgetting), and interviewer bias.", "Programme staff collecting data may get more positive answers (courtesy bias).", "Assuming a large sample removes bias. It does not."], ["Triangulation", "Checking a finding from more than one source, method or person before you call it a finding.", "A survey shows higher savings; records confirm deposits; focus groups explain why.", "Calling a single quote a finding."], ["Saturation", "In qualitative work, the point where new interviews add no new themes. It guides how many interviews you need.", "After 14 interviews with group leaders, the last three added nothing new.", "Stopping at a fixed number without checking for new themes."], ["Theory of change and assumptions", "The steps from activities to impact, and the assumptions that must hold for each step to work.", "Training leads to saving only if women have some cash income. That is an assumption to test.", "Drawing the boxes and never testing the arrows."]], "CONTRACT": [["Scope", "The agreed questions and what is excluded."], ["Deliverables", "Each one with format, length and review rounds."], ["Payments", "Milestones tied to submission, with an advance."], ["Change process", "Extra work is agreed in writing with days and fees."], ["Independence", "The client checks facts; the evaluator decides findings."], ["Data", "Who owns, stores and deletes data, and how it is protected."], ["AI use", "What tools may be used, on which data, and how it is disclosed."], ["Client duties", "Documents, access, a focal point and feedback times."], ["Exit", "How either side can end the contract, and what is paid."]], "CREDS": [["AEA evaluator competencies", "American Evaluation Association, 2018", "Five domains to assess yourself against: professional practice, methodology, context, planning and management, and interpersonal.", "https://www.eval.org/About/Competencies-Standards/AEA-Evaluator-Competencies"], ["UNEG evaluation competency framework", "United Nations Evaluation Group, 2016", "The competencies UN evaluation offices expect of evaluators and evaluation managers.", "https://www.unevaluation.org/document/detail/1915"], ["Credentialed Evaluator (CE)", "Canadian Evaluation Society", "A professional designation based on education, experience and demonstrated competencies.", "https://ces.journals.uvic.ca/index.php/cjpe/article/view/585"], ["Voluntary Evaluator Peer Review", "European Evaluation Society and UK Evaluation Society", "A structured peer review of your practice that helps you plan your development.", "https://europeanevaluation.org/voluntary-evaluator-peer-review/"], ["IPDET", "International Program for Development Evaluation Training, Bern", "An intensive training programme in development evaluation, with core and specialised courses.", "https://ipdet.org/"], ["gLOCAL Evaluation Week", "Global Evaluation Initiative", "A yearly, worldwide week of local events: a low-cost way to present and connect.", "https://glocalevalweek.org/node/62202"], ["Your national evaluation association", "IOCE and EvalPartners", "Voluntary organisations for professional evaluation exist in most countries. Start presenting and find mentors here.", "https://evalpartners.org/evalpartners-programmes/peer-to-peer-projects/"], ["EvalYouth Global Network", "EvalPartners", "A network supporting young and emerging evaluators, including mentoring.", "https://evalpartners.org/?p=1428"]], "DEEP": {"bidgate": {"what": "A bid or no-bid gate is a short, honest check you run before you spend days writing a proposal. You score the opportunity against a few fixed criteria, such as fit with your skills, whether the budget matches the work and whether the payment terms are fair. The total tells you to go, go with conditions, or walk away. It turns a gut feeling into a decision you can explain to a partner or to yourself. Used every time, it protects your most limited resource: your own time.", "origin": "Bid decision gates come from proposal and capture management, where firms such as Shipley Associates teach a formal go or no-go decision before writing starts. The scoring sheet here is a simplified version for independent consultants and small teams.", "why": ["Writing a strong proposal can take three to ten days of unpaid work, so a poor bid has a real cost.", "A low score on one criterion, such as budget or payment terms, often becomes a clarifying question that improves the whole bid.", "Saying no to weak opportunities leaves time for the ones you are most likely to win and deliver well."], "use_when": ["A ToR or request arrives and you must decide whether to respond.", "You are tempted to bid because the client is famous or you need the work.", "A partner or firm asks you to join a consortium bid."], "skip_when": ["A trusted client asks you directly for a small piece of work with clear terms.", "You have already decided to bid for strategic reasons; then use the sheet to list conditions instead."], "principles": ["Score before you read the ToR twice; first impressions are data.", "Use the same criteria every time, so scores are comparable.", "Any criterion scored 1 is a condition to resolve, not a footnote.", "Set your go threshold in advance, not after seeing the score.", "Write down why you said no; patterns appear after a few bids."], "example": "The foundation's mid-term evaluation ToR asked 14 questions in 30 days, with 100% payment on approval and a budget covering about 60% of the work. On the gate it scored 12 of 18: strong on fit and independence, weak on budget and payment. The decision was go, with conditions. The two weak scores became clarifying questions to the foundation about scope and a staged payment schedule.", "good": ["Every criterion has a score and a one-line reason.", "The decision (go, go with conditions, no-go) is written at the bottom.", "Each low score has turned into a question, a condition or a reason to decline."], "related": ["psw", "options", "colours"]}, "psw": {"what": "A problem statement worksheet is one page that pins down the exact question your client needs answered. Clients usually describe a topic, such as 'our M&E system', rather than a question. The worksheet asks you to write one specific, time-bound question, then fill in the context, what success looks like, what is in and out of scope, who decides, the constraints and who already knows something useful. Once the client agrees the page, you have a shared definition of done. Every later choice of method, team and price can be checked against it.", "origin": "The worksheet is associated with McKinsey's structured problem-solving approach and is widely taught in consulting training. Similar one-page problem definitions are common across management consulting.", "why": ["Many disappointing assignments answered the wrong question very well; this page makes the right question visible.", "A clear scope protects you from requests that grow quietly during the work.", "Success criteria agreed at the start become the test the client uses to judge your work at the end."], "use_when": ["After the first call with a client, before you price the work.", "The request is vague, such as 'help us with M&E' or 'assess impact'.", "Different people at the client describe the need in different ways."], "skip_when": ["The ToR already states clear, agreed questions and you are only bidding on method.", "The task is a small, defined deliverable, such as cleaning one dataset."], "principles": ["Write a question, not a topic; it should end with a question mark.", "Make it specific and time-bound: who, what, by when, with what resources.", "Always fill in what is out of scope; it saves more arguments than anything else.", "Name the decision the answer will serve.", "Agree the page with the client in writing before you price."], "example": "The after-school learning NGO's director wrote: 'Set up our M&E system and train staff.' The worksheet turned this into: 'How can the board see each month whether children's reading improves, within 15 days of consultant time and existing staff?' Scope listed a framework, forms, a dashboard and training as in, and an impact evaluation as out. That one sentence shaped the three-option proposal that followed.", "good": ["The key question is one sentence, specific and time-bound.", "Scope has both an 'in' and an 'out' list.", "The client has read the page and agreed it before you sent a price."], "related": ["bidgate", "tree", "options"]}, "stakemap": {"what": "A stakeholder power and interest map lists everyone who can shape, use or block your work, then sorts them on two scales. Power means how much they can affect the assignment or its use. Interest means how much they care about it. Placing each person or group in one of four boxes tells you how to engage them: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed or monitor. It turns a vague sense of 'the stakeholders' into a concrete engagement plan.", "origin": "The power and interest grid is associated with Aubrey Mendelow's work on stakeholder mapping in strategic management in the early 1990s. It is now standard in project management and evaluation practice.", "why": ["Evaluations are used, or ignored, because of people; this map shows whose views matter for use.", "It helps you spot people with high interest but low power, such as programme participants, whose voices are easily missed.", "It guides how much time you give each group, so you do not spend equal effort on everyone."], "use_when": ["At the start of an evaluation or MEL assignment with several parties.", "Before planning interviews, validation workshops or the reporting plan.", "A finding is likely to be sensitive for some groups."], "skip_when": ["A small task with one client contact and no wider users.", "You only need a list of interviewees; use a sampling plan instead."], "principles": ["Map groups and named roles, not only organisations.", "Rate power and interest separately; they are not the same thing.", "Give every box an engagement action, not just a label.", "Check for people affected by the work who have little power.", "Revisit the map at each gate; positions shift."], "example": "In the mid-term evaluation, the foundation's board had high power and low interest, so it was kept satisfied with a one-page brief at each gate. The programme director had high power and high interest and co-designed the questions. Federation leaders and women members had low power but high interest. They were involved in validation and received a plain-language summary in the local language.", "good": ["Every stakeholder sits in one box with a reason.", "Each box has a specific engagement action and timing.", "Participants or communities appear on the map, not only funders and staff."], "related": ["rapid", "prewire", "charter"]}, "tree": {"what": "An issue tree breaks one big question into three to five smaller questions, like branches, and then into sub-questions if needed. The branches should not overlap and, together, should cover the whole question. Beside each branch you write a day-one hypothesis: your best early guess at the answer, based on what you already know. The tree organises your thinking, and the hypotheses tell you what evidence you need to confirm or reject each guess. It keeps a long list of ToR questions from turning into a long, unfocused report.", "origin": "Issue trees and hypothesis-driven problem solving are widely used in strategy consulting firms such as McKinsey and BCG. The idea of branches that do not overlap and cover everything is often called MECE: mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive.", "why": ["ToRs often list 10 to 20 questions; grouping them into a few branches makes the evaluation manageable.", "A hypothesis gives each branch a clear test, which sharpens methods and sampling.", "The branches later become the sections of the evaluation matrix and the report."], "use_when": ["A ToR has many questions that need grouping.", "You are drafting the approach section of a proposal.", "The team is collecting data without a clear sense of what it is testing."], "skip_when": ["The assignment is a single, narrow question with an obvious method.", "The client wants open exploration with no prior view, such as early learning work; hold hypotheses lightly or not at all."], "principles": ["Start from one key question at the top.", "Keep to three to five branches at each level.", "Branches should not overlap and should cover the whole question.", "Write each hypothesis as a statement that data could prove wrong.", "Be ready to drop a hypothesis; the point is to test it, not defend it."], "example": "The foundation's 14 ToR questions became a tree of five branches: relevance, income change, what explains differences, whether groups will last, and efficiency. The day-one hypothesis was 'Income gains follow strong federations.' The design compared districts and federations of different strength so that this guess could be tested. The five branches also became the five agreed evaluation questions.", "good": ["Every original ToR question sits under one branch.", "Each branch has a hypothesis that could be shown wrong.", "Each hypothesis points to the evidence and method needed to test it."], "related": ["psw", "ghost", "pareto"]}, "options": {"what": "A three-option proposal offers the client three versions of the work at three prices instead of one quote. Each option is complete in itself: a smaller essential version, a standard version and a fuller version with extra support. The client's question changes from 'yes or no?' to 'which one?'. You also make clear what each option leaves out, so the client chooses with open eyes. It is a simple way to match scope to budget without cutting your rates.", "origin": "Offering options is widely used in consulting practice and is closely associated with Alan Weiss's writing on value-based fees, which recommends giving clients a choice of options at different levels of value.", "why": ["Many M&E requests arrive with no budget; options let the client reveal what they can afford.", "When budgets are tight, you reduce scope, not your day rate or quality.", "Listing what each option excludes prevents later disputes about scope."], "use_when": ["A client request has no stated budget or timeline.", "There are clear ways to do more or less, such as adding training or coaching.", "You suspect the client's budget is lower than the full scope requires."], "skip_when": ["A formal tender asks for one technical and financial proposal in a fixed format.", "The work cannot be split without making it unsound, such as a sample that would be too small."], "principles": ["Each option must work on its own; the smallest is not a broken version of the largest.", "Price by days and value; show days so the logic is clear.", "State what each option does not include.", "Make the middle option the one you would recommend.", "Keep the descriptions short enough to compare at a glance."], "example": "For the after-school learning NGO, the consultant offered three options. Essentials covered a results framework and three forms in 9 days. Standard added a monthly dashboard and staff training in 15 days. Full added three months of coaching after handover in 21 days. The director chose standard, which became the 15-day assignment.", "good": ["Each option is complete and could be delivered on its own.", "Days, price and exclusions are shown for every option.", "The client chose an option rather than asking for a discount."], "related": ["psw", "sow", "bidgate"]}, "colours": {"what": "Colour team reviews are three planned check-ups of your proposal by people who did not write it. A pink team reviews the outline and approach early. A red team reads the full draft as if they were the evaluation panel and scores it against the criteria. A gold team checks the final version for price, forms and annexes just before you submit. Each review has a date, a reviewer and one guiding question, so it is quick and focused.", "origin": "Colour team reviews come from proposal management practice, especially as taught by Shipley Associates. Many firms adapt the colours and timing to their own process.", "why": ["Writers rarely see their own gaps; a reader acting as the panel finds them fast.", "Errors such as a budget that does not match the workplan can disqualify an otherwise strong bid.", "Planned reviews stop the last-minute rush that produces avoidable mistakes."], "use_when": ["Any competitive bid with scoring criteria.", "A proposal written by several people that must read as one.", "A high-value or strategic bid you cannot afford to lose on detail."], "skip_when": ["A short, informal quote to an existing client.", "There is no time for review at all; then do at least a gold check yourself the next morning."], "principles": ["Book reviewers and dates when you decide to bid.", "Give each review one clear question.", "Red team reviewers should score against the real criteria, as the panel would.", "Act on the three weakest points first.", "The gold team checks facts and numbers, not ideas."], "example": "For the mid-term evaluation bid, a senior colleague ran a pink review seven days before the deadline. Four days before, a colleague spent 45 minutes reading the draft as the foundation's panel. That red team caught that the budget page did not match the workplan. The gold check the day before confirmed that the price, team, forms and annexes were consistent.", "good": ["All three reviews had a date and a named reviewer before writing began.", "The red team scored the draft against the actual criteria.", "Every issue raised was fixed or consciously accepted."], "related": ["bidgate", "options", "pyramid"]}, "sow": {"what": "A statement of work is the part of a contract that says exactly what you will deliver, to what standard, and how the client will accept it. It lists deliverables with length and review rounds, what is excluded, how long the client has to comment and how payments link to milestones. Change control is the agreed way to handle new requests: a short written change request showing the effect on days, fee and timeline. Together they keep the assignment fair for both sides when things change, as they usually do.", "origin": "Statements of work and change control come from project management and contracting practice. They are standard in donor, government and private sector contracts.", "why": ["Scope creep, small extra requests that add up, is one of the main reasons consultants lose money.", "Clear acceptance terms stop reports going through endless rounds of comments.", "Linking payments to milestones protects your cash flow, especially when the ToR says 'payment on approval'."], "use_when": ["Before signing any contract, including small ones.", "The client's ToR is broad or the budget is tight.", "Several people at the client will comment on deliverables."], "skip_when": ["The client uses a fixed contract you cannot change; then use the template to list the questions to raise.", "Very short advice calls billed by the hour with no deliverable."], "principles": ["Describe each deliverable with length, format and number of review rounds.", "List exclusions as clearly as inclusions.", "Set a comment period and say what happens if it passes.", "Agree a one-page change request process before you need it.", "Spread payments across milestones, not all at the end."], "example": "The mid-term evaluation's statement of work set one review round for the inception report and two for the final report, with 10 working days for comments, and replaced the ToR's 30 days with a 14-week timeline. Payments were split 30%, 40% and 30% across signing, inception and final report. After inception, the foundation asked for two extra districts. A one-page change request added 6 days and a revised fee, and it was signed within a week.", "good": ["Every deliverable has a length, a format and a number of review rounds.", "Exclusions and the change request process are written down.", "Payments are linked to at least two milestones before the final report."], "related": ["options", "charter", "raid"]}, "rapid": {"what": "RAPID is a way of naming who plays which role in a decision. R is the person who Recommends a course of action. A is anyone who must Agree before it goes ahead. P is who will Perform, or carry it out. I is who gives Input. D is the one person who Decides. Writing these roles down for each decision your evaluation feeds makes it clear who must be involved, and when.", "origin": "RAPID was developed by Bain & Company as a tool for clarifying decision roles in organisations. It is widely used in management consulting.", "why": ["Evaluation findings are only useful if someone acts on them; RAPID shows who that is.", "It reveals hidden veto holders, such as finance, before recommendations are presented.", "It shapes who you interview, who joins validation and who receives which product."], "use_when": ["An evaluation feeds specific decisions, such as expanding or redesigning a programme.", "There is confusion about who will act on the findings.", "Several organisations share a programme, such as a funder and an implementer."], "skip_when": ["The assignment produces a learning product with no direct decision attached.", "A single person both commissions and acts on the work."], "principles": ["List the actual decisions first, then assign roles.", "Only one person holds the D for each decision.", "Keep the A role small; too many people who must agree slows everything.", "Use roles and names, not just departments.", "Confirm the roles with the client, not only your own reading."], "example": "For the mid-term evaluation's management response, the programme director recommended which recommendations to accept. The finance head had to agree, district managers would perform the actions and federation leaders gave input. The foundation's director made the final decision. Knowing this, the evaluator briefed the finance head early on the cost comparison behind the main recommendation.", "good": ["Each decision has exactly one person in the D role.", "Everyone with an A role has seen the evidence before the decision meeting.", "The roles are agreed with the client and match your engagement plan."], "related": ["stakemap", "mresponse", "prewire"]}, "charter": {"what": "An engagement charter is one page that you and the client both sign at the start of the work. It states the purpose in one line, the deliverables, who does what, the key dates, the main constraints and how you will work together, such as meeting rhythm and comment times. It is less formal than a contract and focuses on how the partnership will run day to day. When questions come up later, the charter is the shared reference both sides return to.", "origin": "The charter is adapted from the project charter used in project management practice. Consultants commonly use a shorter version for engagements.", "why": ["It turns the contract into plain language that programme staff, not only managers, can follow.", "Naming who will own the MEL system after handover makes sustainability a goal from day one.", "Agreed working norms, such as comment times, prevent friction later."], "use_when": ["At the start of any assignment, once the contract is signed.", "The client team includes people who did not see the proposal.", "The work involves building capacity or handing over a system."], "skip_when": ["A very short task of a day or two.", "The inception report already covers roles and ways of working in detail and the client prefers one document."], "principles": ["Keep it to one page.", "Write the purpose as the outcome for the client, not your activity.", "Name people, not just roles, especially future owners.", "Include how you will communicate and how fast each side responds.", "Both sides sign it."], "example": "The after-school learning NGO's charter fitted on one page. Its purpose read: 'The board can see each month whether children's reading improves.' It listed the framework, three forms, a monthly dashboard and two trained staff as deliverables. It named the two staff members who would own the system after handover, and set a weekly 30-minute call with comments within five working days.", "good": ["It fits on one page and both sides have signed it.", "Future owners of the work are named.", "Working norms, such as meetings and comment times, are specific."], "related": ["sow", "kickoff", "psw"]}, "kickoff": {"what": "The kick-off is the first working meeting with the client after signing. Its job is to confirm the questions, the approach, the timeline and who does what, so nobody starts with a different picture. The workplan is the week-by-week schedule that comes out of it. It shows activities, the milestones the client must review or approve, and what the client needs to do at each step. A good kick-off and workplan prevent most delays before they happen.", "origin": "Kick-off meetings and workplans are standard project management practice, used across consulting and evaluation.", "why": ["Evaluations often slip because of client tasks, such as sharing data or arranging field access; the workplan makes these visible.", "Review periods agreed at kick-off stop reports waiting weeks for comments.", "Gates where the client approves each stage reduce the risk of surprises at the end."], "use_when": ["At the start of every assignment, within the first week or two.", "A new team member or client contact joins midway; revisit the plan with them.", "After a major change in scope or timing."], "skip_when": ["A one-day task with a single deliverable.", "The client already runs a formal inception workshop; then bring your workplan to it rather than holding a separate meeting."], "principles": ["Send an agenda and the draft workplan before the meeting.", "Show what the client must do in each phase, not only what you will do.", "Mark gates where the client reviews and approves.", "Build in realistic review time.", "Send a short note of what was agreed within a day."], "example": "The mid-term evaluation's kick-off agreed the five evaluation questions, the sample and review rounds of 10 working days. The workplan ran over 14 weeks, from desk review and inception to the final report. Each phase listed a client task, such as introducing field teams or arranging district access. A steering group meeting was set at each gate.", "good": ["Each phase has a milestone and a client task.", "Review periods and approval gates are written into the plan.", "A summary of agreements was sent within one day of the meeting."], "related": ["charter", "raid", "status"]}, "raid": {"what": "A RAID log is one running list of four kinds of things that can affect your work. Risks are things that might go wrong. Assumptions are things you are treating as true but have not checked. Issues are problems that are already happening. Dependencies are things you cannot start until something else is done. Each item has an owner and a next step, and you review the log every week.", "origin": "RAID logs are widely used in project management practice. The four categories help teams track uncertainty in one place.", "why": ["Fieldwork depends on things outside your control, such as weather, approvals and access; the log keeps them in view.", "Writing assumptions down, such as 'records are complete', forces you to test them early.", "Sharing the log with the client makes risks a joint responsibility."], "use_when": ["Any assignment with fieldwork, several partners or a tight timeline.", "During inception, to feed the risks section of the inception report.", "In weekly status updates to the client."], "skip_when": ["A short desk-based task with no external dependencies.", "The client has its own risk register that you are asked to use instead."], "principles": ["Every item needs a named owner and a next step.", "Turn assumptions into checks with a date.", "Move a risk to issues when it actually happens.", "Review it weekly, briefly.", "Close items when they are resolved, so the log stays short."], "example": "In the mid-term evaluation, the top risk was monsoon rain cutting access to two districts, so fieldwork there was planned first with a phone survey as a fallback. The key assumption was that programme records were complete, checked with a 5% sample in week two. An out-of-date federation list was logged as an issue for district managers. Ethics approval before fieldwork was logged as a dependency.", "good": ["Every item has an owner, a next step and a date.", "Each assumption has a planned check.", "The log was reviewed and updated in the last week."], "related": ["kickoff", "status", "sow"]}, "ghost": {"what": "A ghost deck, or ghost report, is a draft of your final report's headlines written before you collect any data. Each page or section gets a placeholder headline, your best guess at the finding, and a note on the chart or table that would prove it and the data source needed. You then design your data collection tools to confirm or reject each headline. It keeps the team focused on the evidence that matters and avoids collecting data nobody will use.", "origin": "The ghost deck is associated with McKinsey and hypothesis-driven consulting practice, where teams sketch the final presentation early to guide analysis.", "why": ["It links every question in a survey or interview guide to a finding it might support.", "It reveals data gaps early, while you can still change the tools.", "It speeds up the final report, because the structure already exists."], "use_when": ["During inception, after the issue tree and before tools are finalised.", "The team is unsure what the final report should look like.", "Data collection tools are getting long."], "skip_when": ["An exploratory study where you genuinely have no prior expectations.", "The team is likely to treat placeholders as conclusions; then use questions as headlines instead."], "principles": ["Write headlines as full sentences that state a finding.", "Pair each headline with the chart and data source it needs.", "Treat every headline as a guess to test, not to prove.", "Rewrite or kill headlines when the data disagrees.", "Remove tool questions that do not feed any headline."], "example": "Before fieldwork in the mid-term evaluation, the team wrote 12 headline placeholders. One read: 'Incomes rose most where federations are strong', paired with a chart of income change by district and federation strength from the household survey and records. Another read: 'Federations cost less per member', needing budget data. The survey and focus group guides were designed to confirm or kill each one.", "good": ["Each headline has a chart idea and a named data source.", "Every data collection question links to at least one headline.", "The team has agreed in advance that headlines will change if data disagrees."], "related": ["tree", "pareto", "pyramid"]}, "pareto": {"what": "An 80/20 analysis plan ranks the analyses you could do, so the few that matter most get done first and best. The idea is that a small share of the work often produces most of the value. You list each planned analysis, judge how important it is for the key questions and estimate the days it needs. Then you set an order and push low-value analyses to 'later'. It is a guard against spending your limited analysis time evenly on everything.", "origin": "The principle is named after the economist Vilfredo Pareto, who observed uneven distributions. It was later popularised as the 80/20 rule in quality management and is used across consulting firms.", "why": ["Evaluation datasets can support dozens of analyses; time and budget support only a few done well.", "It ensures the analyses behind the main recommendations are the most careful.", "It gives you a defensible reason to say no to nice-to-have tables."], "use_when": ["After data collection, before analysis starts.", "The analysis plan in the inception report is long.", "Time is running short and you need to cut."], "skip_when": ["The ToR or donor requires a fixed set of indicators that must all be reported.", "A small dataset where every analysis is quick."], "principles": ["Rank by importance to the key questions and decisions.", "Estimate the days for each analysis honestly.", "Do the top three first, and do them well.", "Move the rest to 'later', not to 'never' without telling the client.", "The 80/20 split is a rule of thumb, not a precise law."], "example": "In the mid-term evaluation, the team listed every planned analysis and its importance. Three carried most of the evaluation: income change by district, income by federation strength and cost per active member. These were done first. Attendance trends and training satisfaction were marked low importance and moved to later.", "good": ["Every planned analysis has an importance rating and a time estimate.", "The top-ranked analyses link directly to the key questions.", "Lower-ranked analyses are clearly marked and the client knows."], "related": ["ghost", "harvey", "tree"]}, "status": {"what": "A weekly status note is a short update you send the client every week. It has one overall colour, red, amber or green, and five short lines: the headline, progress, next steps, risks and any decision you need from the client. It takes about 15 minutes to write. Regular, honest updates build trust and mean the client is never surprised by a delay.", "origin": "Weekly status reporting with a red, amber and green rating is common consulting and project management practice.", "why": ["Clients worry most when they hear nothing; a short note every week prevents anxious calls.", "Raising problems early, when they are amber, gives time to fix them together.", "Asking for a clear decision moves things forward instead of waiting."], "use_when": ["Every week during active work, especially fieldwork.", "The client has a steering group or several contacts.", "Anything is starting to slip."], "skip_when": ["Very short assignments of a week or less; one end-of-task note is enough.", "The client prefers a fortnightly call with minutes; agree the rhythm at kick-off."], "principles": ["Keep it to five lines and one colour.", "Be honest about the colour; amber early is better than red late.", "Quote progress in numbers where you can.", "End with a clear decision or 'no decision needed'.", "Send it on the same day every week."], "example": "In week six of the mid-term evaluation, the status was amber because rain delayed fieldwork in two districts. Progress was given in numbers: 210 of 400 household surveys and 8 of 12 focus groups. The note set a new target date and warned that a delay beyond two weeks would move the draft report. It asked the client to choose between a one-week later draft and a smaller sample in the delayed districts.", "good": ["The colour matches the real situation.", "Progress is stated in numbers against the plan.", "Any decision needed is clear, with options."], "related": ["raid", "kickoff", "prewire"]}, "harvey": {"what": "An evidence strength rating is a label beside each finding saying how solid its evidence is: strong, moderate or limited. You base it on the number and quality of sources, whether they agree and how well the method supports the claim. A small filled circle, called a Harvey ball, can show the rating visually. Readers then know which findings they can act on with confidence and which need more checking. It makes your judgement transparent instead of hidden.", "origin": "Rating evidence draws on evaluative rubrics, described in the work of evaluator Jane Davidson, and on evidence grading in research. Harvey balls are a common visual convention in consulting reports.", "why": ["Decision-makers often treat every finding as equally certain; ratings correct this.", "Rating forces you to check whether sources agree before you write a finding.", "It protects your credibility when a finding is later questioned."], "use_when": ["Drafting findings in any evaluation report.", "Findings rest on very different amounts of evidence.", "Findings will inform funding or expansion decisions."], "skip_when": ["Routine monitoring reports that only present indicator values.", "The client requires a formal evidence grading system; use theirs instead."], "principles": ["Define what strong, moderate and limited mean before you rate.", "Count sources and check whether they agree.", "Separate 'something changed' from 'the programme caused it'.", "Write a one-line reason for every rating.", "Do not hide limited findings; label them."], "example": "In the mid-term evaluation, 'Incomes rose in two districts' was rated strong, because the survey, programme records and focus groups all agreed. 'Federations cause the gains' was rated moderate: the pattern was clear, but other causes were possible. 'Cost per member is reasonable' was rated limited, as it relied on one incomplete source. The report showed each rating beside its finding.", "good": ["Every finding has a rating and a short reason.", "The rating scale is defined in the methodology section.", "Claims about cause are rated separately from claims about change."], "related": ["pareto", "pyramid", "ghost"]}, "pyramid": {"what": "The pyramid principle is a way of structuring a report or presentation with the answer first. At the top is one governing message: your main conclusion and what to do. Under it come three to five key lines, the reasons that support it. Under each key line sits the evidence. If you write each section title as a full sentence, called an action title, a reader can understand the whole story from the titles alone.", "origin": "The pyramid principle was developed by Barbara Minto, who worked at McKinsey, and is set out in her book on the subject. Action titles are a widely used consulting convention.", "why": ["Busy decision-makers often read only the first page and the headings.", "It forces you to decide what your evaluation actually concludes.", "Action titles make weak or unsupported points easy to spot."], "use_when": ["Writing the key messages, executive summary or a findings presentation.", "A draft report feels long and hard to follow.", "Before a validation workshop, to test the storyline."], "skip_when": ["Technical annexes and methods sections, where completeness matters more than argument.", "Participatory reporting where conclusions are developed with stakeholders; then build the pyramid after the session."], "principles": ["Start with the answer, not the background.", "Write titles as full sentences that make a point.", "Each key line must support the governing message.", "Keep three to five key lines at each level.", "Check: can someone read only the titles and get the story?"], "example": "The mid-term evaluation's governing message read: 'Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding.' Four key lines supported it. Incomes rose in two districts and were flat in two others. The gains tracked federation strength, federated groups were most likely to last, and strengthening federations cost less per member than opening new districts.", "good": ["The governing message is one sentence with a conclusion and an action.", "Every section title is a full sentence that makes a point.", "Reading the titles alone tells the whole story."], "related": ["ghost", "harvey", "prewire"]}, "prewire": {"what": "Pre-wiring means talking privately with the people most affected by a difficult finding before the big meeting where it is presented. You share the finding and the data, listen to their view and ask what would make it workable. People are much less likely to reject findings they have had time to understand. Pre-wiring is not about changing findings to please people; it is about avoiding surprise and improving accuracy. Often the person affected suggests the solution themselves.", "origin": "Pre-wiring is a common practice in McKinsey and other consulting firms. Evaluation practice has similar steps, such as sharing draft findings for factual checks.", "why": ["A hard finding presented by surprise in a group often triggers defence, not action.", "Private conversations catch factual errors before they are made public.", "People who help shape the response are more likely to own it."], "use_when": ["A finding is negative or sensitive for a specific person or team.", "A recommendation challenges a senior person's plan.", "Before a validation or findings workshop."], "skip_when": ["Independence would be compromised, for example if it invites pressure to change findings; then use a formal factual-accuracy check.", "Findings are routine and not sensitive for anyone."], "principles": ["Share the data, not only the conclusion.", "Listen more than you talk.", "Correct facts; do not soften judgements without evidence.", "Ask what would make the finding easier to act on.", "Keep a short note of each conversation."], "example": "Before the mid-term evaluation's findings workshop, the evaluator met the district manager whose district showed no income gains. The evaluator shared the data and the comparison with other districts and asked what made her district different. She explained local factors and, in the workshop, proposed the fix herself. A second meeting with the programme director covered pausing expansion and what would make that acceptable to the board.", "good": ["Everyone facing a hard finding heard it privately first.", "Factual corrections were made and noted; judgements were kept unless new evidence emerged.", "No one at the workshop was surprised by a finding about their own work."], "related": ["stakemap", "pyramid", "rapid"]}, "impactease": {"what": "Impact and ease prioritisation sorts recommendations on two scales: how much impact each would have, and how easy it is to put into practice. Recommendations with high impact and high ease are quick wins. High impact but harder ones are major projects. Low impact but easy ones are fill-ins, and low impact and hard ones are usually dropped. The result is a short, ordered list that a client can actually act on.", "origin": "The impact and ease matrix, sometimes called an impact and effort matrix, is widely used in consulting practice and in quality improvement work.", "why": ["Evaluations often end with too many recommendations of equal weight; this shows what matters most.", "It helps clients plan, with quick wins now and major projects scheduled.", "Scoring together with the client builds ownership of the recommendations."], "use_when": ["You have more than five draft recommendations.", "During a validation or recommendations workshop with the client.", "Before writing the recommendations section of the report."], "skip_when": ["There are only two or three recommendations and their order is obvious.", "A donor requires recommendations to follow a fixed structure, such as by evaluation criterion; then prioritise within each group."], "principles": ["Define the scoring scales, for example 1 to 5, before you score.", "Score impact against the key question, not general good practice.", "Ask the people who will implement to judge ease.", "Be willing to drop low-impact recommendations.", "Present quick wins first, but do not let them replace major projects."], "example": "The mid-term evaluation had 11 draft recommendations. Scored with the programme team, three were quick wins, such as tracking income every six months and sharing results with members. Four were major projects, led by strengthening federations before expanding. Two, including a new training model, were dropped as low impact.", "good": ["Every recommendation has an impact and an ease score.", "Ease was judged with the people who will implement.", "The final list is shorter and ordered, with drops explained."], "related": ["mresponse", "pyramid", "rapid"]}, "mresponse": {"what": "A management response is the client's formal reply to each recommendation in an evaluation. For each one, the client says whether it is accepted, partly accepted or not accepted. For accepted ones, it names the action, an owner and a date. The tracker then follows progress at later check-ins. It turns a report into a set of commitments and shows whether the evaluation was used.", "origin": "Management responses are part of evaluation practice in the UN system, set out in guidance from the United Nations Evaluation Group (UNEG), and are used by many donors and foundations.", "why": ["Without a response, recommendations are easily forgotten after the report is approved.", "It makes use of the evaluation visible, which matters for the evaluator's reputation and future work.", "Follow-up check-ins create a reason to stay in contact with the client."], "use_when": ["After the final evaluation report is approved.", "The client or donor asks for evidence of use.", "For any evaluation with actionable recommendations."], "skip_when": ["Monitoring reports without recommendations.", "The client already has its own response system; then offer to fill in theirs."], "principles": ["The client writes the response, not the evaluator; you can facilitate.", "Every accepted recommendation has an action, owner and date.", "'Not accepted' is allowed, with a reason.", "Agree a check-in date, such as three months later.", "Keep it to one table."], "example": "After the mid-term evaluation, the foundation accepted 9 of 11 recommendations. Strengthening federations first was accepted, with a federation support plan owned by the programme director. Pausing new districts was partly accepted, as a one-year pause. At the three-month check-in, six actions were on track.", "good": ["Every recommendation has a response and, if accepted, an owner and date.", "Reasons are given for partial or non-acceptance.", "A check-in date is set and the tracker is updated after it."], "related": ["impactease", "rapid", "aar"]}, "aar": {"what": "An after-action review is a short team conversation held after an assignment, or a major stage, to learn from it. It uses four questions: what did we expect to happen, what actually happened, why was there a difference, and what will we do next time. It takes about 30 minutes and focuses on learning, not blame. The answers go straight into your next proposal, workplan or pricing.", "origin": "The after-action review was developed by the US Army as a structured way for teams to learn after an operation. It is now used widely in organisations, including in development and humanitarian work.", "why": ["Consultants often repeat the same estimating errors; this catches them.", "It turns experience into specific changes, such as days or methods.", "It is a simple way to model learning, which is the 'L' in MEL."], "use_when": ["At the end of every assignment.", "After a major stage, such as fieldwork.", "After something went notably well or badly."], "skip_when": ["Team members cannot speak openly, for example in front of the client; hold a separate internal review.", "Immediately after a stressful event; wait a day or two."], "principles": ["Use the four questions in order.", "Focus on what happened, not who is to blame.", "Everyone speaks, including junior team members.", "End with one or two specific changes.", "Write the changes where you will see them next time."], "example": "The after-school learning NGO assignment had planned two days of staff training. The review found training took four days, because staff needed more hands-on practice with the forms on their phones. What worked was practice with real forms. The next proposal priced training at four days, not two.", "good": ["All four questions are answered.", "The difference between plan and reality is explained, not just noted.", "At least one specific change is recorded for the next assignment."], "related": ["nps", "mresponse", "options"]}, "nps": {"what": "A client feedback score is three short questions you ask every client at the end of an assignment. The first is: on a scale of 0 to 10, how likely are you to recommend us to a colleague? The second asks what was most valuable, and the third what could be better. You can also ask who else might benefit. The number is less important than the conversation it starts.", "origin": "The first question comes from the Net Promoter Score, introduced by Fred Reichheld of Bain & Company in 2003. The short version here is adapted for independent consultants.", "why": ["Most consulting work comes from referrals and repeat clients; this shows where you stand.", "A low score is a chance to fix a relationship before it is lost.", "The open answers tell you what to keep and what to change."], "use_when": ["At the end of every assignment.", "After a major milestone on a long assignment.", "You want to ask for a referral or testimonial."], "skip_when": ["The client relationship has ended badly and a call is needed first.", "A formal client satisfaction process is already required by your firm."], "principles": ["Ask the same questions every time.", "Ask soon after the final deliverable.", "Follow up any score of 6 or below with a call.", "Thank people for honest criticism.", "Track scores over time, not one at a time."], "example": "At the end of the MEL set-up, the NGO's director scored 9. The most valuable thing was that staff could run the dashboard alone; the improvement was a shorter final report. She named two NGOs in the next district who might need similar help. A score of 6 from another client led to a call that saved the relationship.", "good": ["All three questions were asked and recorded.", "Any low score was followed up personally.", "Feedback led to at least one change or a referral."], "related": ["aar", "mresponse", "bidgate"]}, "proposal": {"what": "A technical proposal is your written offer to do an assignment, usually in response to a ToR. It shows that you understand the problem, explains how you will answer the questions and why your team is right for it. It is paired with a financial proposal, which sets out the price. In competitive bids, a panel scores it against published criteria.", "readers": "An evaluation panel or the client's programme and M&E staff. They look for understanding of the context, a sound and feasible method, a realistic workplan and a qualified team, often against a scoring grid.", "why": ["It is the first sample of your thinking and writing the client sees.", "Commitments made here become the basis of the contract.", "A clear approach section makes inception much faster."], "use_when": ["Responding to a ToR or request for proposals.", "A client asks for a written offer after a discovery call.", "Joining a consortium bid led by another firm."], "length": "Usually 10 to 25 pages plus annexes, or the page limit set in the ToR. Small assignments may need only 3 to 5 pages.", "principles": ["Opens with the client's problem, not your history.", "Groups the ToR questions and shows a first hypothesis.", "Includes a draft evaluation matrix.", "Has a workplan and budget that match each other.", "Follows the ToR's order and scoring criteria."], "mistakes": ["Copying the ToR back to the client as 'understanding'.", "Listing methods without saying which question each answers.", "A workplan, team days and budget that do not add up."], "good": ["A panel member could find each scoring criterion addressed quickly.", "Every method is linked to an evaluation question.", "Days, workplan and price are consistent across the document."]}, "inception": {"what": "An inception report is written early in an evaluation, after initial reading and conversations but before data collection. It confirms the evaluation questions and sets out exactly how they will be answered: the design, methods, sample, analysis and workplan. Once the client approves it, it becomes the agreed plan for the rest of the work. It is the best moment to correct misunderstandings from the ToR.", "readers": "The client's programme, M&E and management staff, and sometimes a steering or reference group. They check that the questions are right, the methods are credible and the plan is feasible.", "why": ["It locks in the agreed questions and methods, protecting both sides from later disputes.", "It shows the evaluation matrix, which links questions to evidence.", "Approval is often a payment milestone."], "use_when": ["Two to four weeks after signing, before data collection.", "Any evaluation with more than a few days of fieldwork.", "The ToR questions need refining or reducing."], "length": "Typically 15 to 30 pages plus annexes such as tools, the evaluation matrix and the sampling plan.", "principles": ["Explains any changes from the ToR and why.", "Includes a complete evaluation matrix.", "Shows the sample size calculation and its limits.", "States the risks and what the evaluation cannot say.", "Includes a draft outline of the final report."], "mistakes": ["Repeating the proposal without new learning from the inception phase.", "Leaving ethics and data management vague.", "Promising more than the time and budget can deliver."], "good": ["Every evaluation question appears in the matrix with sources and methods.", "The client can see exactly what will happen, when and who is involved.", "Limitations are stated plainly."]}, "report": {"what": "An evaluation report presents what the evaluation found, what it concludes and what it recommends. It answers each evaluation question with evidence, makes a judgement and sets out practical recommendations. A strong report puts the key messages first, so busy readers can act on them. Annexes hold the technical detail for those who want to check it.", "readers": "Senior decision-makers read the key messages and summary; programme staff read the findings and recommendations; technical reviewers read the methods and annexes. Each looks for clear answers they can trust and use.", "why": ["It is the main product the client pays for and remembers.", "Its recommendations shape real decisions about programmes and funding.", "It is often published or shared, so it represents your work publicly."], "use_when": ["At the end of an evaluation or a major review.", "A donor requires a formal report.", "For mid-term, final or thematic evaluations."], "length": "Often 25 to 50 pages plus annexes, with a one-page key messages section and an executive summary of about three pages.", "principles": ["Leads with the answers, not the methods.", "Organises findings by evaluation question.", "Rates the strength of evidence for each finding.", "Separates findings, conclusions and recommendations.", "Makes each recommendation specific, prioritised and owned."], "mistakes": ["Burying the main messages after long background sections.", "Recommendations that do not follow from the findings.", "Hiding limitations in an annex."], "good": ["A reader of the first page knows the answers and what to do.", "Each recommendation can be traced back to a finding.", "Limitations and evidence strength are clear."]}, "invoice": {"what": "An invoice is a formal request for payment when you reach a milestone in your contract. It records who you are, who the client is, what was delivered, the amount due and how to pay. It is also a tax and accounting record for both sides. A clear, correct invoice gets paid faster.", "readers": "The client's finance team, who check it against the contract, purchase order and approved deliverable. They look for correct details, matching amounts and the right tax information.", "why": ["Late or incorrect invoices are a common cause of cash flow problems for consultants.", "Linking each invoice to a contract clause avoids queries.", "Running numbers and complete records make tax filing easier."], "use_when": ["A payment milestone in the contract is reached.", "For agreed expenses, if the contract allows reimbursement.", "A change request with a fee has been approved."], "length": "One page.", "principles": ["Uses a unique running invoice number.", "Names the milestone and the contract clause.", "Shows fee, taxes and total clearly.", "States the due date and full bank details.", "Matches the client's purchase order or contract number."], "mistakes": ["Missing the client's contract or purchase order number.", "Tax details that are wrong or missing.", "Sending the invoice before the milestone is formally accepted."], "good": ["The finance team can match it to the contract without asking anything.", "Amounts and taxes are correct.", "The due date and bank details are complete."]}, "consent": {"what": "An informed consent script is the words you say, or the form you share, before every interview, survey or focus group. It explains who you are, what the study is about, what taking part involves, and that taking part is voluntary and confidential. The person then agrees or declines, and you record their answer. It is an ethical duty, not a formality.", "readers": "Participants, who need to understand clearly what they are agreeing to. Ethics committees and clients also review it to check that it protects people.", "why": ["People have the right to decide freely whether to share their experiences.", "Clear consent protects participants, the client and you.", "Ethics approval and many data protection laws require it."], "use_when": ["Before every interview, survey or focus group.", "Before recording audio, video or photographs, which need separate consent.", "Re-contacting people for follow-up."], "length": "About one page, or one to two minutes when read aloud, in the participant's language.", "principles": ["Uses plain, local language.", "States clearly that people can refuse or stop at any time without losing services.", "Explains how answers are kept confidential.", "Asks separately for consent to recording.", "Gives a contact for questions or complaints."], "mistakes": ["Using technical or legal wording people cannot follow.", "Implying that services depend on taking part.", "Not recording whether consent was given."], "good": ["A participant could repeat back what they agreed to.", "Refusal is presented as a real, safe option.", "Consent and recording consent are recorded separately."]}, "kii": {"what": "A key informant interview guide is the list of topics and questions you use when interviewing someone with particular knowledge, such as programme staff, officials or experts. It is semi-structured: the main questions are fixed, but you follow up with probes. Each topic links to an evaluation question. The guide keeps interviews consistent across interviewers while leaving room to explore.", "readers": "Interviewers use it during the conversation. The client and reviewers check that it covers the evaluation questions and treats respondents respectfully.", "why": ["Consistent questions make it possible to compare answers across interviews.", "Linking topics to evaluation questions ensures you collect what you need.", "Good probes reveal examples and reasons, not just opinions."], "use_when": ["Interviewing staff, government officials, partners or experts.", "You need depth and explanation rather than numbers.", "To understand why results differ between places or groups."], "length": "Two to four pages, for an interview of 45 to 60 minutes.", "principles": ["Starts with easy questions about the person's role.", "Uses open questions, not yes or no.", "Includes probes such as 'Can you give an example?'.", "Links each topic to an evaluation question.", "Ends by asking what was missed and who else to speak to."], "mistakes": ["Too many questions for the time available.", "Leading questions that suggest the answer.", "Not recording the role and context of the respondent."], "good": ["Each topic maps to an evaluation question.", "The interview fits in the planned time.", "Notes record role, date and place, but not names."]}, "fgd": {"what": "A focus group discussion guide is the plan for a facilitated conversation with a small group, usually six to ten people from similar backgrounds. It sets out the opening, ground rules, warm-up, core questions and closing, and may include a participatory exercise such as ranking or mapping. A facilitator leads while a note-taker records. Focus groups reveal shared views, differences and how people talk about an issue.", "readers": "Facilitators and note-takers use it in the field. The client and ethics reviewers check that it is relevant, respectful and safe for participants.", "why": ["Groups can surface views that individual interviews miss.", "Participatory exercises make discussion concrete and inclusive.", "A clear guide keeps groups across districts comparable."], "use_when": ["Speaking with programme participants or community members.", "Exploring shared experiences, norms or perceptions.", "Validating or explaining survey findings."], "length": "Two to three pages, for a discussion of 60 to 90 minutes, plus a note-taker sheet.", "principles": ["Groups people with similar backgrounds so all feel able to speak.", "Sets ground rules, including confidentiality within the group.", "Uses four to six core questions with probes.", "Plans timing and venue around participants' needs.", "Includes a note-taker sheet for quotes and levels of agreement."], "mistakes": ["Mixing people with very different power, such as staff and participants.", "Too many questions, leaving no time for discussion.", "No note-taker, so key points are lost."], "good": ["Everyone in the group had a chance to speak.", "Core questions link to evaluation questions.", "Notes capture quotes and how widely views were shared."]}, "dmp": {"what": "A data management plan sets out how data will be collected, stored, protected, shared and eventually deleted during an evaluation. It covers consent, legal requirements, security, removing names and identifiers, use of AI tools, and who owns the data at the end. It is agreed with the client at inception. It turns good intentions about privacy into specific, checkable steps.", "readers": "The client, ethics reviewers and your own team. They look for clear, realistic protections, especially for personal or sensitive data.", "why": ["Evaluations often collect personal and sensitive information about vulnerable people.", "Data protection laws, such as India's DPDP Act, set legal duties.", "Clear rules on AI use prevent personal data being shared with tools by mistake."], "use_when": ["At inception, for every evaluation that collects data.", "Working with field teams or partner organisations.", "Planning to use AI tools for analysis."], "length": "Two to four pages, or a section of the inception report.", "principles": ["Lists each type of data and whether it is personal or sensitive.", "Names where data is stored and who has access.", "Explains how data is de-identified before analysis or sharing.", "States which AI tools may process which data.", "Sets retention and deletion dates."], "mistakes": ["Generic statements such as 'data will be kept secure' with no detail.", "No plan for deleting data at the end.", "Not addressing AI tools at all."], "good": ["A team member could follow the plan without asking questions.", "Every type of personal data has a protection step.", "Deletion and handover arrangements are dated."]}, "cv": {"what": "A key-expert CV in donor format is a structured CV used in bids for donor, UN or firm-led assignments. It follows a set layout, often provided by the donor, and focuses on experience relevant to the specific role. Panels usually score CVs against the ToR's expert requirements. It is a tailored document, not your general CV.", "readers": "Evaluation panels and procurement officers. They look for qualifications and experience that match the stated requirements, with clear dates, clients and roles.", "why": ["Team CVs can carry a large share of the technical score in many bids.", "A CV that mirrors the ToR makes it easy for a panel to award points.", "Accurate, consistent CVs build the credibility of the whole bid."], "use_when": ["Bidding for donor, UN or firm-led assignments.", "A lead firm asks you to join a team as a key expert.", "The ToR specifies a CV format."], "length": "Typically two to five pages, or the length set by the donor.", "principles": ["Follows the donor's format exactly.", "Opens with key qualifications that mirror the ToR.", "Lists relevant assignments with dates, client, role and days.", "Is honest and consistent with other team documents.", "Has references who agreed to be named."], "mistakes": ["Sending a general CV that ignores the ToR.", "Vague experience entries without dates, roles or clients.", "Overstating roles or claiming work done by others."], "good": ["Each ToR requirement can be matched to a line in the CV.", "Every assignment has dates, client, role and a short description.", "All facts are accurate and can be checked."]}, "position": {"what": "A positioning statement says, in one sentence, who you help, with what problem, how, and with what result. The brand canvas around it records the parts that make the sentence true: your niche, the problem in the client's words, your proof points, your voice and what you will not do. A niche combines four choices: sector, method, client and place. You can still take other work. The point is that when someone hears your name, they know what to call you about.", "origin": "Positioning as a marketing idea is associated with Al Ries and Jack Trout. Professional-services marketers apply it to firms and to individual experts; the Hinge Research Institute's work on 'visible experts' links a clear specialism to being known and sought out.", "why": ["Commissioners and firms shortlist people they can describe in a phrase; a vague profile is easy to pass over.", "A clear niche lets you build depth, so each assignment makes you better at the next one.", "Saying what you are not makes everything else you say more believable."], "use_when": ["You are starting out independently and your work feels scattered.", "Your profile, proposals and introductions all describe you differently.", "You keep winning work you do not enjoy or do not do well."], "skip_when": ["You work inside one firm that sets your positioning; use the canvas only for your own development.", "You are changing direction; run two small experiments before you commit."], "principles": ["Narrow enough to remember, broad enough to make a living.", "Describe the problem in the client's words, not evaluation jargon.", "Every claim needs a proof point someone could check.", "Never inflate: no results you did not achieve, no roles you did not hold.", "Review it each year; good positioning evolves with your work."], "example": "The solo consultant who set up the MEL system for the after-school learning NGO had been using the label 'M&E consultant'. The canvas narrowed this to education, MEL systems, small NGOs and India. Proof points were the 15-day set-up and staff running the dashboard alone. 'Not impact evaluations' went under what the consultant is not. The statement became: 'I help small education NGOs build MEL systems their own staff can run.'", "good": ["All four parts of the niche are filled with specific words.", "Every proof point is true, checkable and shareable.", "Three people who know your work can repeat your statement after hearing it once."], "related": ["profile", "visibility", "bidgate"]}, "profile": {"what": "Your professional profile is what people find when they look you up: a LinkedIn page and a one-page web profile. The builder gives you a headline formula (role, niche and result), an about section in four moves (problem, approach, proof, invitation), up to three featured items, two or three recommendations, and the keywords commissioners, firms and peers use when they search. Write it once from your positioning, then reuse the same words everywhere, so your CV, proposals and profile tell one story.", "origin": "The builder draws on professional-services marketing practice and on how evaluation work is commissioned: firms search networks and rosters for key experts, and commissioners check profiles against donor-format CVs. It applies the positioning canvas to the places people look.", "why": ["People usually look you up before they reply to an introduction or shortlist you for a bid.", "A clear headline and the right keywords help the right people find you, and the wrong ones pass by.", "Consistent wording across profile, CV and proposals builds trust; mismatches raise doubts."], "use_when": ["You have just written or revised your positioning.", "You have finished a major assignment you can describe.", "A firm or commissioner is about to receive your CV for a bid."], "skip_when": ["You cannot yet say what you specialise in; do the positioning canvas first.", "A client's contract forbids naming the work; describe the type of work only."], "principles": ["Write for the reader who is deciding whether to call you.", "Use the words clients and donors use, such as 'mid-term evaluation' and 'MEL system'.", "Show, then tell: featured work and recommendations do more than adjectives.", "Only name clients, share reports or quote people with their permission.", "Keep it current: review it each quarter and after every assignment."], "example": "After the mid-term evaluation of the women's livelihoods programme, the team leader's headline read only 'Independent consultant'. It became: 'Evaluation team leader | Livelihoods and women's economic empowerment | Mixed methods'. The about section opened with the problem programmes face mid-way, and featured a practice note on turning 14 questions into five, shared with the foundation's agreement. The programme director wrote a recommendation about the inception.", "good": ["Your headline tells a stranger your role, niche and result in one line.", "Every featured item and named client has permission behind it.", "Your profile, CV and proposals use the same words for what you do."], "related": ["position", "casestudy", "cv"]}, "casestudy": {"what": "A portfolio case study is a one-page story of one assignment, written so a future client can see how you think. It covers the context, the question the client needed answered, your approach, what changed, a client quote used with permission, and your exact role. It is anonymised by default: the organisation, people and places are described by type unless the client agrees in writing to be named. It is the honest, checkable version of 'I have done this before'.", "origin": "Case studies are a long-standing format in professional-services marketing, usually following a situation, approach and result pattern. This version adds the duties set out in evaluation associations' ethical guidelines: confidentiality, consent and honest reporting of limits.", "why": ["Clients want evidence that you have solved a problem like theirs; a case study shows it in one page.", "Writing it forces you to name what changed and what you cannot claim, which sharpens your next proposal.", "A well-anonymised case study respects the client, which itself builds trust."], "use_when": ["An assignment has ended well and the client is willing to review a draft.", "A proposal asks for examples of similar work.", "Your profile needs featured work that shows your niche."], "skip_when": ["The client has not agreed, or the work is confidential or sensitive.", "Results are not yet known; wait, or write about the approach only."], "principles": ["Anonymise first: describe organisations, people and places by type.", "Lead with the change, and state the limits of what you can claim.", "Name your role precisely, and credit the team and the client.", "Use a quote only in the client's approved words, with the attribution they chose.", "Keep the written consent with the case study, and respect what it covers."], "example": "The solo consultant wrote up the MEL set-up for the after-school learning NGO as 'From paper registers to a monthly reading dashboard'. The NGO appears as 'a 25-person education NGO with 40 centres'. What changed: two staff now run the dashboard alone and the board reviews it monthly. It does not claim that reading improved. The director approved the text by email for the consultant's website and proposals, with the quote attributed to 'Director, education NGO'.", "good": ["Nobody could identify the client or respondents without the client's agreement.", "The change described is observable and the limits are stated.", "Written consent is on file and covers every place you will use it."], "related": ["profile", "nps", "consent"]}, "visibility": {"what": "A visibility and content plan sets out, for the next twelve months, how you will share what you know in four ways: publish, speak, teach and give back. Publishing means short practice notes, aea365-style blog posts, contributions to BetterEvaluation and LinkedIn posts. Speaking means association events, gLOCAL Evaluation Week and conferences. Teaching means webinars and workshops. Giving back means reviewing, mentoring and task forces. One theme from your positioning runs through the year.", "origin": "The Hinge Research Institute's research on 'visible experts' describes expertise made visible through writing, speaking and teaching. The routes here are evaluation associations' own programmes, such as AEA's aea365 blog, BetterEvaluation and gLOCAL Evaluation Week.", "why": ["Being good is not enough if the people who commission evaluations never see your thinking.", "Sharing practice builds a public record of judgement that a CV cannot show.", "Giving back, through reviewing and mentoring, builds relationships and strengthens the field."], "use_when": ["You have a clear positioning and want more of the right work to find you.", "You are planning the year and want visibility to fit around paid work.", "You have learning from an assignment that others could use."], "skip_when": ["You are overloaded with paid work; keep one small item a quarter instead.", "You have not yet agreed with a client what you may share from their assignment."], "principles": ["One theme a year, taken from your positioning.", "Small and steady beats big and occasional: one item per column per quarter at most.", "Share learning and method, never respondents' data or a client's confidential findings.", "Reuse: one practice note can become a talk, a webinar and a post.", "Review each quarter what people read, who got in touch, and what to drop."], "example": "After the mid-term evaluation, the team leader chose one theme for the year: making mid-term evaluations useful. Q1: a short practice note on grouping 14 questions into five, shared with the foundation's agreement. Q2: a session proposal for a regional evaluation conference. Q3: a free webinar for a national evaluation network. Q4: reviewing conference abstracts and mentoring one emerging evaluator. Each quarter took about two days.", "good": ["Every item links to your one theme for the year.", "The plan fits the spare time you really have.", "Nothing in it shares client or respondent information without agreement."], "related": ["position", "events", "casestudy"]}, "network": {"what": "A relationship map is a short list of the people who matter most to your work, grouped in tiers: clients, past colleagues, firms who bid, commissioners in evaluation offices, peers and mentors. For each person the tracker notes your last contact, something useful you could give, and your next step. With it go a few habits: give first, keep a light cadence, follow up after every event, ask for warm introductions, and ask for referrals and testimonials at the right moment and in the right way.", "origin": "The map draws on relationship marketing and on trusted-advisor practice in professional services, such as David Maister, Charles Green and Robert Galford's The Trusted Advisor, which describes trust as built through many small, reliable interactions over time.", "why": ["Much consulting work comes through people who already know and trust you.", "A written map stops important relationships fading while you are busy delivering.", "Giving first, without asking for anything, makes later asks feel natural rather than transactional."], "use_when": ["You are setting up independently and want a steady flow of work.", "An assignment is ending and you want to keep the relationships it built.", "You have just returned from an event with new contacts."], "skip_when": ["You are tempted to use it for mass messages; it is for real relationships only.", "You cannot keep personal data safe; store roles and first names only."], "principles": ["Keep it small: around 30 people you can actually stay in touch with.", "Give first: an article, an introduction, feedback on a draft, an invitation.", "Follow up within 48 hours of every meeting or event.", "Ask for referrals and testimonials after a good result, specifically and easily declined.", "Respect privacy: store only what you need, and never share contacts without consent."], "example": "The solo consultant listed 30 people in six tiers. After the MEL set-up, the after-school learning NGO's director scored her 9 out of 10 and mentioned two other NGOs. The consultant asked whether the director would be comfortable making an introduction, with a short note the director could forward. One became the next client. The consultant thanked the director and later sent a useful dashboard template, asking nothing in return.", "good": ["Each person has a tier, a last contact and a dated next step.", "You have something useful to offer most people on the list.", "Every referral or testimonial was asked for openly and could easily be declined."], "related": ["events", "nps", "position"]}, "events": {"what": "An event and conference plan is one page in three parts. Before: set two or three goals, list who you want to meet and why, and submit a session, poster or panel if you can. During: have real conversations rather than pitches, and write notes the same day. After: follow up within 48 hours and share what you learned. It works for a local association meeting, gLOCAL Evaluation Week, Asian Evaluation Week, EvalFest of the Evaluation Community of India, or the European Evaluation Society and AEA conferences.", "origin": "The plan draws on how evaluation associations run their conferences, with open calls for sessions and volunteer roles, and on the follow-up habits taught in professional-services business development.", "why": ["Conferences take days and often money; a plan makes that time count.", "Presenting, even briefly, makes you known for a topic far more than attending.", "Most of the value comes after the event, in follow-up that most people never do."], "use_when": ["You have registered for, or are considering, an event.", "A call for sessions opens in your niche.", "You want to meet commissioners, firms or peers in a new region."], "skip_when": ["The event has nothing to do with your niche or your learning goals.", "You cannot afford the time; follow the recordings and write to one speaker instead."], "principles": ["Set goals for learning and relationships, not for sales.", "Prepare questions, not a pitch.", "Write notes the same day, while you can still remember who said what.", "Follow up within 48 hours, with something useful and specific.", "Share what you learned, crediting speakers accurately."], "example": "Before a regional evaluation conference, the team leader set three goals: learn how others group evaluation questions, meet two commissioners from evaluation offices, and test the practice note as a short session. During the conference, the notes recorded who said what. Within 48 hours, each new contact received a message with one useful link. A short post shared three lessons and credited the speakers by name.", "good": ["You arrived with goals, names and questions.", "Every new contact had a follow-up within 48 hours.", "You shared your learning and added people to your relationship map."], "related": ["network", "visibility", "profile"]}}, "DESIGNS": [["Randomised controlled trial", "Experimental", "How much difference did the programme make, on average?", "Random assignment before the programme starts; large samples; time and budget.", "Rarely possible mid-programme; says little about why.", ""], ["Difference-in-differences", "Quasi-experimental", "How did change compare with a similar group over the same time?", "Baseline and follow-up data for both groups.", "Assumes both groups would have changed in parallel.", ""], ["Matching", "Quasi-experimental", "How do participants compare with similar non-participants?", "Rich data on both groups to find good matches.", "Cannot match on what you did not measure.", ""], ["Contribution analysis", "Theory-based", "Is it reasonable to say the programme contributed, and how?", "A theory of change, evidence for each link, and rival explanations.", "Needs honest testing of other explanations.", "https://betterevaluation.org/node/2708"], ["Process tracing", "Theory-based", "Did the programme cause this specific outcome, through which steps?", "A detailed causal story and tests of how strongly evidence confirms each step.", "Demanding; best for a few important cases.", ""], ["Realist evaluation", "Theory-based", "What works, for whom, in what circumstances, and why?", "Programme theories of context, mechanism and outcome; mixed data.", "Takes skill; findings are patterns, not averages.", ""], ["Outcome harvesting", "Participatory and emergent", "What changed, including the unexpected, and how did the programme contribute?", "People who can describe and verify changes; time to substantiate.", "Finds outcomes after the fact; less suited to measuring size.", ""], ["Most Significant Change", "Participatory and emergent", "Which changes matter most to the people involved, and why?", "Stories collected and selected through structured discussion.", "A complement to other evidence, not a measure of average effect.", ""]], "DEVEVAL": [["Purpose", "Judge results at set points", "Support innovation and adaptation as it happens"], ["Timing", "Mid-term and end", "Continuous, alongside the team"], ["Evaluator's role", "Independent, at arm's length", "Part of the team, asking evaluative questions in real time"], ["Questions", "Did it work?", "What is emerging, and what should we try next?"], ["Outputs", "Reports and recommendations", "Rapid feedback, learning sessions, updated theories of change"]], "DMAP": {"comp": [1, 2], "toc": [3, 4], "why": [5], "emerge": [6, 7]}, "DOC": {"small": [["Discovery call notes", [["Asked", "\"Set up our M&E system\""], ["Real need", "Board wants to know if reading improves"], ["Readiness", "Data on paper; one keen staff member"], ["Decision", "Bid, with a tight scope"]], "go"], ["Proposal, page 1", [["Problem", "No way to see learning progress monthly"], ["Deliverables", "Framework · forms · dashboard · training"], ["Days", "15 over 8 weeks"], ["Price", "Fixed, in three payments"]], ""], ["Contract terms", [["Payments", "30% · 40% · 30%"], ["Reviews", "One round per deliverable"], ["Changes", "In writing, with days and fees"], ["Client gives", "A focal point and records"]], ""], ["Inception note", [["Users", "Director, 4 coordinators, board"], ["Indicators", "12, from 31 proposed"], ["Ghost dashboard", "Drawn before any form"], ["Signed", "Week 2"]], ""], ["Pilot log", [["Centres", "4 for two weeks"], ["Fields dropped", "2 nobody used"], ["Data checks", "Weekly completeness"], ["Status", "Dashboard live"]], ""], ["Learning note", [["Headline", "Reading levels visible monthly"], ["Pages", "4, with the dashboard"], ["Meeting", "Whole team, 90 minutes"], ["Comments", "One round, factual only"]], ""], ["Close-out", [["Handover", "Forms, dashboard, guide"], ["Check-in", "Month three: running alone"], ["Feedback", "9 of 10"], ["Next", "Two referrals"]], "go"]], "large": [["Go or no-go score", [["Fit with skills", "3 of 3"], ["Budget vs work", "1 of 3"], ["Client readiness", "2 of 3"], ["Conflicts", "None declared"]], "warn"], ["Technical proposal", [["Questions", "14 grouped into 5"], ["Hypothesis", "Stronger where federations are active"], ["Design", "Mixed methods, 4 districts"], ["Team", "Lead, researcher, 8 investigators"]], ""], ["Contract terms", [["Scope", "5 questions, agreed"], ["Findings", "The evaluator decides"], ["Payments", "On submission, not approval"], ["AI use", "Only on de-identified data"]], ""], ["Evaluation matrix, row 2", [["Question", "Did incomes change, for whom?"], ["Indicator", "Monthly income, self-reported"], ["Sources", "Survey · FGDs · records"], ["Analysis", "By district and group age"]], ""], ["Fieldwork tracker", [["Households", "400 of 400"], ["Focus groups", "12 of 12"], ["Interviews", "20 of 20"], ["Flags", "1 investigator retrained"]], ""], ["Report, 1:3:25", [["1 page", "Key messages for the board"], ["3 pages", "Summary by question"], ["25 pages", "Findings and evidence"], ["Workshop", "Findings validated"]], ""], ["Management response", [["Accepted", "9 of 11"], ["Owners", "Named, with dates"], ["Follow-up", "Six-month review"], ["Next", "Invited to bid again"]], "go"]]}, "DOCS": [["proposal", "Technical proposal", "Responding to a ToR or a client request", [["Cover letter", "One page: who you are, why this assignment, your one-line approach, and contact details."], ["Understanding of the assignment", "The problem in your words, the decision the findings will serve, and what you read between the lines of the ToR."], ["Approach and methodology", "Your first hypothesis, the grouped evaluation questions, design, methods, sampling and their limits. Include a draft evaluation matrix."], ["Ethics and data protection", "Consent, anonymity, safeguarding, data storage and deletion, and how you will use AI."], ["Workplan", "Stages, deliverables and dates, with client review time built in. A simple week-by-week chart."], ["Team", "Who does what, days per person, and why each person fits. CVs in the annex."], ["Relevant experience", "Two or three similar assignments: client, year, what you did, what changed."], ["Risks and how you will manage them", "The top five risks, each with a mitigation."], ["Financial proposal", "Days by role and stage, rates, expenses, contingency, taxes, and the payment schedule you propose."], ["Annexes", "CVs, references, sample work, registration documents."]]], ["inception", "Inception report", "Two to four weeks after signing, before data collection", [["Introduction and purpose", "Why the evaluation is happening, who will use it, and for which decisions."], ["Programme background and theory of change", "What the programme does and how it expects change to happen. Include a diagram."], ["Evaluation questions", "The agreed questions, grouped, with any changes from the ToR explained."], ["Evaluation matrix", "Each question with indicators or criteria, sources, methods and analysis."], ["Design and methods", "The design, data collection methods, sampling (with your sample size calculation) and analysis plan."], ["Ethics and data management", "Consent, protection, safeguarding, approvals, data storage and AI use."], ["Workplan and deliverables", "Dates, responsibilities, review rounds."], ["Risks and limitations", "What could go wrong, and what the evaluation will not be able to say."], ["Report outline", "The structure of the final report, ideally as draft headlines."], ["Annexes", "Draft tools, stakeholder list, documents reviewed."]]], ["report", "Evaluation report", "At the end of the evaluation", [["Key messages (1 page)", "The answer to each evaluation question, and what to do, in plain language."], ["Executive summary (3 pages)", "Purpose, methods in brief, findings by question, conclusions and recommendations."], ["Introduction", "Purpose, scope, users and the evaluation questions."], ["Programme and context", "What the programme does and what was happening around it."], ["Methodology and limitations", "Design, methods, sample, analysis, ethics and what the evaluation could not do."], ["Findings by evaluation question", "One section per question; each finding with its evidence and a strength rating."], ["Conclusions", "What the findings mean together; your evaluative judgement against the criteria."], ["Recommendations", "Specific, prioritised, feasible, each with a suggested owner and timeframe."], ["Lessons", "What others could learn beyond this programme."], ["Annexes", "ToR, evaluation matrix, tools, list of people consulted (with consent), documents reviewed, management response template."]]], ["invoice", "Invoice", "When a payment milestone is reached", [["Your details", "Name or firm name, address, PAN and GST number if registered, email and phone."], ["Client details", "Organisation name, address, GST number if any, contact person, purchase order or contract number."], ["Invoice number and date", "A running number (for example HF-2026-014) and the date of issue."], ["Description", "The milestone, for example 'Mid-term evaluation: inception report submitted on 14 November 2026', and the contract clause it refers to."], ["Amounts", "Professional fee, GST if registered, total payable. Note that the client may deduct TDS."], ["Payment terms", "Due date (for example within 15 days) and bank details: account name, number, IFSC, bank and branch."], ["Signature", "Your signature, or 'This is a computer-generated invoice'."]]], ["consent", "Informed consent script", "Before every interview, survey or focus group", [["Introduction", "Your name, who you work for, and that you are independent of the programme if true."], ["Purpose", "What the study is about, in one or two plain sentences."], ["What taking part involves", "What you will ask, how long it takes, and whether you will record or take notes."], ["Voluntary", "They can say no, skip any question or stop at any time, and it will not affect any services."], ["Confidentiality", "How their answers are protected; names are not reported; who will see the data."], ["Risks and benefits", "Any possible discomfort, and that there is no direct payment or benefit unless there is."], ["Questions and contact", "Who to contact with questions or complaints."], ["Agreement", "'Do you agree to take part?' Record verbal or written consent, and consent to recording separately."]]], ["kii", "Key informant interview guide", "Interviewing staff, officials, partners or experts", [["Opening", "Introduction, consent, and an easy first question about their role."], ["Topic 1, linked to an evaluation question", "Two or three open questions, each with probes such as 'Can you give an example?'"], ["Topic 2", "Open questions and probes."], ["Topic 3", "Open questions and probes."], ["What could be better", "What would they change, and why?"], ["Closing", "Anything we did not ask? Who else should we talk to? Thanks."], ["Interviewer notes", "Date, place, duration, role of the respondent (no name), and first impressions."]]], ["fgd", "Focus group discussion guide", "Group discussions with 6 to 10 people from similar backgrounds", [["Before the group", "Venue, timing that suits participants, a facilitator and a note-taker, consent."], ["Opening and ground rules", "Introductions, purpose, confidentiality within the group, no right or wrong answers."], ["Warm-up question", "Something everyone can answer, for example how long they have been in their group."], ["Core questions", "Four to six open questions tied to the evaluation questions, each with probes."], ["Participatory exercise", "Optional: ranking, mapping or a timeline to make discussion concrete."], ["Closing", "Summarise what you heard, ask if anything is missing, thank participants."], ["Note-taker sheet", "Seating plan by number, key quotes, levels of agreement, body language."]]], ["dmp", "Data management plan", "At inception, for every evaluation that collects data", [["Data to be collected", "Types of data, sources, and whether any is personal or sensitive."], ["Consent and legal basis", "How consent is collected and recorded; the laws that apply, such as India's DPDP Act."], ["Storage and security", "Where data is stored, who has access, passwords and encryption."], ["De-identification", "How names and identifiers are removed before analysis or sharing."], ["AI use", "Which tools may process which data, and only de-identified data where agreed."], ["Retention and deletion", "How long data is kept, and how and when it is deleted."], ["Sharing and ownership", "What is handed to the client, in what form, and who owns it."]]], ["cv", "Key-expert CV (donor format)", "Bidding for donor, UN or firm-led assignments", [["Proposed position", "The role in this bid, for example 'Team leader'."], ["Personal details", "Name, nationality, languages (reading, speaking, writing), contact."], ["Education", "Degrees, institutions and years."], ["Key qualifications", "Five or six lines that mirror the ToR's expert requirements."], ["Relevant experience", "For each assignment: dates, client, country, role, days, design and methods, short description."], ["Employment record", "Positions, organisations and dates, most recent first."], ["Publications and training", "Optional, short."], ["References", "Three people who saw you lead, with permission."], ["Certification", "A signed statement that the CV is accurate, if the donor asks for one."]]]], "DONORCV": [["Use their format", "Many donors and firms ask for a key-expert CV in a set template. Keep a master CV and adapt it in 30 minutes."], ["Lead with relevant evaluations", "For each: your role, client, country, dates, days worked, design and methods."], ["Quantify", "Team size, countries, sample sizes, budget range, number of evaluation questions."], ["Mirror the ToR", "Use the words in the expert requirements: 'team leader', 'mixed methods', 'theory-based'."], ["Be honest about languages and regions", "Overstating them is easy to check and ends relationships."], ["Three referees who saw you lead", "Ask before you list them, and tell them about each bid."]], "DQ": [["comp", "Can you build a credible comparison group, with data from before and after?"], ["toc", "Is there a clear theory of change you can test link by link?"], ["why", "Does the client need to know what works for whom, and why?"], ["emerge", "Are the important outcomes unknown in advance, or still emerging?"]], "DUEDIL": ["Registration certificate of your firm or proof of self-employment", "Tax registration (for example PAN and GST in India)", "Audited accounts or income statements for recent years", "Safeguarding policy", "Data protection policy", "Anti-fraud, anti-corruption and conflict of interest policies", "Code of conduct", "Professional indemnity insurance, if required", "CVs of the core team in donor format", "Three references and two sample reports"], "DUTY": ["Risk assessment for each field location", "Safeguarding and sexual exploitation prevention policy, signed by every team member", "Referral details for respondents in distress", "Check-in routine for field teams", "Insurance for team members where possible"], "EMERGING": [["Climate and environment", "More ToRs ask how programmes affect, and are affected by, climate and the environment. Learn the basics of environmental sustainability as an evaluation lens."], ["Evaluating AI programmes", "Clients increasingly fund AI tools in health, education and agriculture. Evaluate accuracy, fairness, safety and real use, not just adoption."], ["Data protection in India", "The Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 and its Rules, notified in November 2025 and being phased in, apply to personal data you collect. Plan consent, storage and deletion."], ["Remote and digital data", "Phone surveys, digital monitoring and administrative data are cheaper but carry bias and privacy risks. State the limits."]], "EQUITY": [["Gender-responsive evaluation", "Looks at how a programme affects women, men and gender-diverse people differently, and at the power relations behind it.", "UN Women Evaluation Handbook, 2022", "https://www.unwomen.org/en/digital-library/publications/2022/05/un-women-evaluation-handbook-2022"], ["Equity-focused evaluation", "Asks whether the worst-off groups were reached and benefited, not only the average.", "UNICEF and partners", ""], ["Participatory evaluation", "Involves the people affected in setting questions, collecting data and making sense of it.", "BetterEvaluation", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Locally led evaluation", "Puts evaluators and knowledge from the region at the centre, as in the Made in Africa Evaluation movement.", "African Evaluation Association and others", ""]], "ESSENTIALS": [["Monitoring and evaluation", "Monitoring tracks what is happening, continuously. Evaluation judges whether it worked and why, at set moments.", "Every ToR: knowing which one is being asked for changes the method, time and price.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Theory of change", "A picture of how activities lead to outputs, outcomes and impact, and the assumptions in between.", "Designing questions, and when a client has no clear logic for the programme.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Results framework and logframe", "A table of the programme's goals, indicators, targets and sources at each level.", "MEL system set-ups and checking what data already exists.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Indicators", "Specific, measurable signs of change, broken down by group where it matters (for example by gender or district).", "Building monitoring systems and evaluation matrices.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Evaluation designs", "Ways to judge cause and effect: comparison groups (experimental or quasi-experimental), theory-based designs such as contribution analysis, and participatory approaches.", "Whenever a ToR uses the word 'impact'.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Sampling", "Choosing who to ask so the answers represent the people you care about, with a size you can afford.", "Every survey, and every budget conversation.", "#hfsurveys"], ["Qualitative methods", "Interviews, focus groups and observation that explain why and how things happened.", "Most evaluations: numbers show what changed; people explain why.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Mixed methods and triangulation", "Combining numbers and stories, and checking one source against another before you call something a finding.", "Analysis, and rating the strength of evidence.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Data quality", "Checking that data is complete, accurate, timely and consistent before anyone uses it.", "Monitoring reviews and every fieldwork week.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["Ethics and consent", "Doing no harm: informed consent, privacy, safety, and reporting honestly.", "From the bid to the final report.", "https://uneval.org/document/detail/2866"]], "EST": {"small": {"name": "MEL system set-up", "rows": [["Discovery and desk review", 2, 0], ["Inception workshop and note", 2, 0], ["Framework and indicators", 3, 0], ["Forms and dashboard", 4, 0], ["Pilot and fixes", 2, 0], ["Training and handover", 2, 0]], "team": 0, "exp": 5}, "base": {"name": "Baseline study", "rows": [["Desk review and inception", 5, 2], ["Tools and testing", 4, 2], ["Training field team", 2, 2], ["Fieldwork supervision", 10, 10], ["Analysis", 6, 6], ["Report and workshop", 5, 2]], "team": 1, "exp": 15}, "mid": {"name": "Mid-term evaluation", "rows": [["Desk review and inception", 6, 3], ["Tools and ethics", 4, 3], ["Fieldwork", 12, 12], ["Analysis", 8, 8], ["Report and validation workshop", 7, 3], ["Management response support", 1, 0]], "team": 1, "exp": 20}, "final": {"name": "Final evaluation", "rows": [["Desk review and inception", 7, 4], ["Tools and ethics", 5, 3], ["Fieldwork", 15, 15], ["Analysis", 10, 10], ["Report and workshops", 8, 4], ["Learning brief", 2, 1]], "team": 1, "exp": 25}}, "ETHICS": ["Declare conflicts of interest before you bid", "Get consent, and protect respondents' identities", "Report what you found, including what nobody wanted to hear", "Do not promise results you cannot measure", "Say no to work you are not competent to do", "Credit others' work, and disclose AI use"], "EX": {"small": {"name": "The MEL system set-up", "kind": "Small assignment", "tag": "MEL SET-UP", "setup": "A 25-person NGO runs 40 after-school learning centres in one district. Its new funder asks for 'a proper M&E system'. The director emails a one-paragraph request to a solo consultant.", "start": "The request: 'Set up our M&E system and train staff. Please share your quote.' No budget, no timeline, no definition of done.", "end": "15 consultant days over eight weeks. A results framework, 12 indicators, three short forms, a monthly dashboard and two staff who can run it. Paid on time; referred to two other NGOs.", "stat": [1, 15, "consultant days"]}, "large": {"name": "The mid-term evaluation", "kind": "Large assignment", "tag": "MID-TERM EVALUATION", "setup": "A foundation funds a three-year livelihoods programme for 6,000 women in self-help groups across four districts. Halfway through, it issues a ToR for a mid-term evaluation.", "start": "The ToR: 14 questions, 'impact on income', 30 days, payment on approval, and a budget that covers about 60% of the work it describes.", "end": "Five agreed questions, a 14-week workplan in place of the ToR's 30 days, mixed methods, and a team of three plus eight field investigators. 9 of 11 recommendations accepted in the management response; invited back for the final evaluation.", "stat": [14, 5, "evaluation questions"]}}, "FACIL": [["1-2-4-All", "Validation workshops", "Everyone reflects alone, then in pairs, then fours, then the whole room. Quiet voices are heard."], ["What, So What, Now What?", "Sense-making sessions", "Look at the data together, agree what it means, then what to do."], ["15% Solutions", "Recommendations", "Each person names what they can do now, with the resources they control."], ["TRIZ", "Risk and assumption reviews", "Ask how to guarantee failure, then stop doing those things."], ["Troika Consulting", "Peer advice for your team", "One person shares a challenge; two others advise while the first listens."]], "FACIL_SRC": "From Liberating Structures, by Henri Lipmanowicz and Keith McCandless.", "FAIL": [["Scoped by hope", "Too many questions, too few days, and no change process. The work grows; the fee does not."], ["Built for the shelf", "Nobody asked who will use the findings, for which decision. The report is filed and forgotten."], ["Trust spent early", "A missed deadline, a surprise finding in the final meeting, an unpaid invoice. Relationships end on small things."]], "FEEDBACK": [["Tell them early", "No surprises in the final meeting. Pre-wire hard findings one to one."], ["Separate fact from finding", "Accept factual corrections; keep the judgement yours."], ["Name the reaction", "If someone resists, say what you notice, calmly, and listen."], ["Offer a way forward", "A finding with a feasible next step is easier to accept."]], "FIRST3": [["Second chair", "Work under an experienced lead.", "Ask for: one section to draft, a seat in client meetings, and feedback on your writing.", "Keep: the lead as a referee, and an anonymised sample of your section."], ["Small and complete", "Lead a small, tightly scoped piece from scope to handover.", "Ask for: a written scope, a change process, and an advance on signing.", "Keep: a short case study, a client reference and your first feedback score."], ["Stretch with support", "Join a bigger evaluation as deputy team leader.", "Ask for: ownership of one evaluation question and the matrix rows behind it.", "Keep: your role in the CV in the donor's words, and a second referee."]], "FLAWS": {"scope": "Open-ended scope", "impact": "Impact claim with no design", "pay": "Payment risk", "many": "Too many questions for the time", "indep": "A threat to independence", "vague": "Vague deliverable", "time": "Unrealistic timeline", "coi": "Conflict of interest"}, "G": {"bidgate": {"what": "A short scorecard you fill in before you decide to write a proposal, like checking the weather before a long trek.", "why": "Without it you spend days on bids you cannot win or should not take, and say yes to work that hurts your reputation.", "when": "The day a terms of reference (ToR) arrives, before you write anything.", "time": "20 minutes", "need": "The ToR, your calendar and your day rate", "mistakes": ["Scoring after you have already decided to bid", "Ignoring payment terms because the topic is exciting"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Criterion", "Score 1 to 3", "Why this score"], "guide": ["Keep the same six criteria every time so you can compare bids", "1 = poor fit, 2 = workable, 3 = strong", "One line of evidence from the ToR"], "rows": [["Fit with your skills and sector", "3", "Livelihoods mixed methods: our core work"], ["Budget matches the work", "1", "Covers about 60% of the questions asked"], ["Client knows how findings will be used", "2", "Mid-term decisions named, but vaguely"], ["Timeline allows a proper inception", "2", "30 days in total; tight"], ["No conflict of interest", "3", "Never worked with this programme"], ["Payment terms you can live with", "1", "100% on approval of the final report"]], "blank": 6, "foot": "Total: ____ of 18. 15 or more: go. 11 to 14: go with conditions. Below 11, or any conflict of interest: no-go."}, "psw": {"what": "One page that pins down the exact question the client needs answered, before you choose any method.", "why": "Most failed assignments answered the wrong question very well. This page makes the right question visible and agreed.", "when": "After your first call with the client, before you price the work.", "time": "45 minutes", "need": "Your notes from the discovery call", "mistakes": ["Writing a topic ('our M&E system') instead of a question", "Leaving 'what is out of scope' empty"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Key question", "One specific, time-bound question. What must be answered?", "How can the board see each month whether children's reading improves, within 15 days of consultant time?"], ["Context", "What is happening now that makes this matter?", "New funder wants evidence; data is on paper; one keen staff member."], ["Criteria for success", "How will the client know it worked?", "Board reviews a reading dashboard every month; staff run it without help."], ["Scope", "What is in, and what is out?", "In: framework, forms, dashboard, training. Out: an evaluation of impact."], ["Decision-makers and stakeholders", "Who decides, who uses, who could block?", "Director decides; board uses; centre staff collect."], ["Constraints", "Time, money, data, politics.", "15 days, eight weeks, two staff, low-end phones."], ["Sources of insight", "Who and what already knows something?", "Funder's template, staff, two similar NGOs, current registers."]]}, "stakemap": {"what": "A list of everyone who can shape, use or block the work, sorted by how much power they have and how much they care.", "why": "Evaluations get blocked, ignored or attacked by people nobody consulted early.", "when": "At inception, and again before you share findings.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "An organisation chart and the ToR", "mistakes": ["Listing organisations instead of named roles", "Forgetting the people the programme serves"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Stakeholder", "Power", "Interest", "How you will engage them"], "guide": ["A role, not just an organisation", "High or low: can they change or stop the work?", "High or low: do they care about it?", "High-high: involve closely. High-low: brief. Low-high: keep informed. Low-low: update."], "rows": [["Foundation board", "High", "Low", "One-page brief at each gate"], ["Programme director", "High", "High", "Co-design questions; fortnightly check-ins"], ["Federation leaders", "Low", "High", "Validation workshop; share results back"], ["Women members", "Low", "High", "Plain-language summary in local language"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "tree": {"what": "A diagram that breaks one big question into three to five smaller questions, like the branches of a tree, each with your best early guess at the answer.", "why": "Without it, questions overlap, gaps appear, and fieldwork days go to things nobody needed.", "when": "While you write the approach section of a proposal.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your problem statement", "mistakes": ["Branches that overlap, so the same data is collected twice", "More than five branches, which usually means some are sub-questions"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Key question (the root)", "Copy it from your problem statement.", "Is the livelihoods programme on track, and what should change?"], ["Branch 1", "A smaller question. Together, the branches must fully answer the root.", "Is the model relevant to women's needs? Hypothesis: Activities match stated needs; some groups are left out"], ["Branch 2", "Then write your hypothesis: what you expect the answer to be.", "Did incomes change, and for whom? Hypothesis: Incomes rose in two districts, not in two others"], ["Branch 3", "No overlaps with other branches.", "What explains differences between districts? Hypothesis: Strong federations drive the gains"], ["Branch 4", "No overlaps with other branches.", "Will groups last after the programme? Hypothesis: Groups with federations will keep meeting"], ["Branch 5", "No overlaps with other branches.", "Is delivery efficient? Hypothesis: Cost per active member is reasonable but uneven"]]}, "options": {"what": "Offering the client three versions of the work at three prices, instead of one take-it-or-leave-it quote.", "why": "One price invites a yes or a no. Three options invite the question 'which one?', and clients often pick the middle.", "when": "When you write the budget section of a proposal.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your day rate and the problem statement", "mistakes": ["Options that differ only in days, not in value", "Making the cheapest option too thin to be useful"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Option", "What the client gets", "Days", "Price", "What is left out"], "guide": ["Name each by its value", "The outcome, not the activities", "Lead and team days", "Including expenses", "Be honest; it builds trust"], "rows": [["Essentials", "Framework and three forms", "9", "Your price", "Dashboard, training"], ["Standard", "Plus monthly dashboard and staff training", "15", "Your price", "Coaching after handover"], ["Full", "Plus three months of coaching", "21", "Your price", "Nothing in scope"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "colours": {"what": "Three planned check-ups of your proposal by someone else: pink for the plan, red for the full draft, gold before you send.", "why": "You cannot see your own blind spots. A panel will, and it will score you down for them.", "when": "While you prepare any important proposal.", "time": "Three reviews of 30 to 60 minutes", "need": "A colleague, the ToR and its scoring criteria", "mistakes": ["Holding the red team the night before the deadline", "Reviewers who read as friends instead of as the panel"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Review", "When", "Who", "The question they answer"], "guide": ["Pink, red or gold", "Days before the deadline", "Someone who did not write it", "Pink: is the plan right? Red: would we win? Gold: is it complete?"], "rows": [["Pink", "7 days before", "A senior colleague", "Do the outline and approach answer the ToR?"], ["Red", "4 days before", "Two people acting as the panel", "Score it against the criteria: what are the three weakest points?"], ["Gold", "1 day before", "You and a second reader", "Price, team, forms and annexes all correct?"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "sow": {"what": "The part of a contract that says exactly what you will deliver, how it will be accepted, and what happens when the client asks for more.", "why": "Unwritten extras eat your profit and your quality. A clear scope turns 'one more thing' into a decision with a price.", "when": "While you negotiate the contract.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your proposal and the client's contract template", "mistakes": ["Leaving 'any other tasks as assigned' in the contract", "No limit on rounds of review"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Deliverables", "List each one with format, page limit and number of review rounds.", "Inception report (15 pages, 1 review round); final report (25 pages plus annexes, 2 rounds)"], ["Out of scope", "Write what you will not do.", "Impact estimates; data collection in new districts"], ["Acceptance", "How a deliverable is accepted, and how long the client has to comment.", "Comments within 10 working days; silence means accepted"], ["Change process", "How extra work is requested, priced and approved.", "Written change request with days, fee and timeline effect, signed by the programme director"], ["Payments", "Milestones tied to submission, not approval.", "30% on signing, 40% on inception report, 30% on final report submission"]]}, "rapid": {"what": "A table naming who plays which role in each decision the evaluation feeds: who recommends, who must agree, who carries it out, who gives input and who decides.", "why": "Good recommendations stall when nobody knows who decides.", "when": "At inception, once you know which decisions the findings will inform.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The organisation chart and the list of decisions", "mistakes": ["More than one person in the 'Decide' column", "Listing everyone under 'Agree', which gives everyone a veto"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Decision", "Recommend", "Agree", "Perform", "Input", "Decide"], "guide": ["The decision the evidence informs", "Proposes the option", "Can block it; keep this short", "Carries it out", "Is consulted", "One person only"], "rows": [["Which recommendations to accept", "Programme director", "Finance head", "District managers", "Federation leaders", "Foundation director"], ["Whether to expand to new districts", "Programme director", "Finance head", "District managers", "Federation leaders", "Foundation director"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "charter": {"what": "One page that both you and the client sign, saying what you are doing together and how you will work.", "why": "Expectations drift during long assignments. A shared page brings everyone back to what was agreed.", "when": "In the first week after the contract is signed.", "time": "45 minutes", "need": "The contract and the proposal", "mistakes": ["Writing it in consultant jargon the client will not read", "Not getting it signed"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Objective", "One sentence, in the client's words.", "The board can see each month whether children's reading improves"], ["Deliverables", "What you will hand over.", "Results framework, three forms, monthly dashboard, two trained staff"], ["Roles", "Who does what on both sides.", "Director signs off; two staff own the system; consultant designs and trains"], ["Timeline", "Key dates and gates.", "Eight weeks; framework agreed in week 3"], ["Top risks", "Two or three, with what you will do.", "Staff time is scarce: train in two short sessions"], ["Ways of working", "Check-ins, response times, reviewers.", "Weekly 30-minute call; comments within five working days"]]}, "kickoff": {"what": "The first meeting with the client, and a week-by-week plan with checkpoints where the client reviews and approves.", "why": "Clients review late when they did not know they had to. A visible plan makes their role part of the schedule.", "when": "In week one.", "time": "1 hour meeting, plus 1 hour to plan", "need": "The contract and the evaluation questions", "mistakes": ["No client review time in the plan", "Checkpoints without a named approver"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Weeks", "Activity", "Checkpoint or deliverable", "What the client must do"], "guide": ["Group by stage", "What happens", "What gets approved", "Reviews, data, access"], "rows": [["1 to 3", "Desk review and inception", "Inception report signed", "Share documents; review in 10 days"], ["4 to 5", "Tools and ethics", "Ethics approval", "Introduce field teams"], ["6 to 9", "Fieldwork", "Fieldwork complete", "Arrange access to districts"], ["10 to 11", "Analysis", "Emerging findings session", "Attend; check facts"], ["12 to 14", "Report and validation", "Final report submitted", "Comment within 10 days"]], "blank": 5, "foot": ""}, "raid": {"what": "One running list of risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies, each with an owner and a next step.", "why": "Problems surprise you when nobody wrote them down. A reviewed list turns surprises into plans.", "when": "From inception, reviewed every week.", "time": "15 minutes a week", "need": "Your workplan", "mistakes": ["Entries without an owner", "Writing it once and never updating it"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Type", "Entry", "Owner", "Next action", "By when"], "guide": ["Risk: might happen. Assumption: we believe true. Issue: happening now. Dependency: we need it first.", "Plain sentence", "A named person", "What will be done", "A date"], "rows": [["Risk", "Monsoon cuts access to two districts", "Team lead", "Plan fieldwork there first; keep a phone survey fallback", ""], ["Assumption", "Programme records are complete for 2024 and 2025", "Client M&E officer", "Check a 5% sample in week 2", ""], ["Issue", "Federation list is out of date", "District managers", "Updated list by 20 October", ""], ["Dependency", "Ethics approval before fieldwork", "Evaluator", "Submitted; decision due 25 October", ""]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "ghost": {"what": "A draft of your final report's headlines, written before you collect any data, with the chart each headline will need.", "why": "Without it you collect data you never use and miss data you needed.", "when": "At inception, after the evaluation matrix.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "The evaluation matrix", "mistakes": ["Topic headlines such as 'Income', which say nothing", "Treating the guesses as conclusions: they are there to be tested"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Page", "Headline: a finding, not a topic", "Chart or evidence it needs", "Where the data comes from"], "guide": ["In report order", "Your best guess, as a full sentence", "Bar chart, quote, table", "Survey, records, interviews"], "rows": [["1", "Incomes rose most where federations are strong", "Income change by district and federation strength", "Household survey; records"], ["2", "Two districts saw little change", "Before and after by district", "Baseline; survey"], ["3", "Federated groups are likelier to last", "Share of groups meeting regularly", "Group records; FGDs"], ["4", "Federations cost less per member", "Cost per active member", "Budget data"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "pareto": {"what": "Ranking the analyses you plan, so the few that matter most get done first and best.", "why": "Time always runs out. Without a ranking, it runs out on the analyses that mattered.", "when": "Before analysis begins.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The evaluation matrix and the data you have", "mistakes": ["Doing analyses in the order data arrives", "Treating every analysis as equally important"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Analysis", "Could it change the answer?", "Effort", "Order"], "guide": ["What you will compare or calculate", "High or low", "Days", "Do high-impact ones first"], "rows": [["Income change by district", "High", "2", "1"], ["Income by federation strength", "High", "2", "2"], ["Cost per active member", "High", "1", "3"], ["Attendance trends", "Low", "1", "Later"], ["Training satisfaction", "Low", "0.5", "Later"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "status": {"what": "A five-line update you send the client every week, with one overall colour: red, amber or green.", "why": "Clients forgive delays they hear about early. They do not forgive surprises.", "when": "The same day every week.", "time": "15 minutes", "need": "Your workplan and RAID log", "mistakes": ["Always reporting green", "Not asking for a decision when one is needed"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Overall status", "Red: off track, needs a decision. Amber: at risk, action under way. Green: on track.", "Amber"], ["Why this colour", "One sentence.", "Fieldwork in two districts is delayed by rain; analysis is on schedule elsewhere."], ["Done this week", "Facts, with numbers.", "Household survey complete in two districts (210 of 400); 8 of 12 focus groups held."], ["Next", "With dates.", "Finish two districts by 14 November; first analysis readout on 21 November."], ["Risks and issues", "What could go wrong and what you are doing.", "If the delay passes two weeks, the draft report moves by one week."], ["Decision needed", "A clear choice for the client.", "Choose: accept a one-week later draft, or a smaller sample in the delayed districts."]]}, "harvey": {"what": "A label beside each finding saying how solid its evidence is: strong, moderate or limited.", "why": "Readers treat every finding as equally certain unless you tell them. Honest ratings build trust.", "when": "During analysis and report writing.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "Your findings and their sources", "mistakes": ["Rating everything as strong", "Changing the scale halfway through"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Finding", "Sources", "Strength", "Why"], "guide": ["One sentence", "Which data supports it", "Strong, moderate or limited", "Number and agreement of sources"], "rows": [["Incomes rose in two districts", "Survey, records, FGDs", "Strong", "Three sources agree"], ["Federations cause the gains", "Survey, FGDs", "Moderate", "Pattern is clear; other causes possible"], ["Groups will last", "FGDs, records", "Moderate", "Early signs only"], ["Cost per member is reasonable", "Budget data", "Limited", "One source, incomplete"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "pyramid": {"what": "Writing the answer first, then three to five reasons, then the evidence under each, so the titles alone tell the story.", "why": "Busy readers stop after the first page. If the answer is not there, they never see it.", "when": "When you outline the report or presentation.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your findings", "mistakes": ["Section titles that are topics, such as 'Background' or 'Findings'", "More than five supporting points"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Governing message", "The one-sentence answer to the key question.", "Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding."], ["Supporting title 1", "A full-sentence finding that proves part of the message.", "Incomes rose by about a fifth in two districts and were flat in two others"], ["Supporting title 2", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "The gains track federation strength more closely than any other factor"], ["Supporting title 3", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "Groups linked to strong federations are the most likely to last"], ["Supporting title 4", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "Strengthening federations costs less per member than opening new districts"]]}, "prewire": {"what": "Talking privately to the people most affected by a hard finding before the big meeting.", "why": "People surprised in public defend themselves. People consulted in private engage, and often suggest the fix.", "when": "A week before the findings or validation workshop.", "time": "30 minutes per person", "need": "Your draft findings", "mistakes": ["Using it to negotiate the finding away", "Skipping the most senior person"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Person", "The finding that affects them", "What you will share", "What you want to learn"], "guide": ["A named role", "In one sentence", "Finding and evidence", "Their explanation and ideas"], "rows": [["District manager, District C", "Incomes did not rise in her district", "The data and the comparison", "What made her district different"], ["Programme director", "Expansion should pause", "The cost comparison", "What would make it acceptable to the board"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "impactease": {"what": "Sorting recommendations by how much they would help and how easy they are, into quick wins, major projects, fill-ins and drops.", "why": "A long unsorted list of recommendations gets ignored. A sorted one tells the client where to start.", "when": "When you draft recommendations, ideally with the client.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "Your draft recommendations", "mistakes": ["Scoring alone, without the client", "Labelling everything a major project"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Recommendation", "Impact 1 to 5", "Ease 1 to 5", "Group"], "guide": ["One action", "How much it helps", "How easy it is", "Quick win, major project, fill-in or drop"], "rows": [["Strengthen federations before expanding", "5", "3", "Major project"], ["Track income every six months", "4", "5", "Quick win"], ["Share results with members", "3", "5", "Quick win"], ["New training model", "2", "1", "Drop"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "mresponse": {"what": "A table where the client formally replies to each recommendation: accepted, partly accepted or not accepted, with an action, owner and date.", "why": "Without it, reports are filed and forgotten. With it, use can be tracked.", "when": "When the final report is delivered.", "time": "1 hour with the client", "need": "The final recommendations", "mistakes": ["Writing the client's responses for them", "No date to check back"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Recommendation", "Response", "Action", "Owner", "By when"], "guide": ["As written in the report", "Accepted, partly, not accepted", "What they will do", "A named role", "A date"], "rows": [["Strengthen federations first", "Accepted", "Federation support plan", "Programme director", "March"], ["Track income every six months", "Accepted", "Add to monitoring form", "M&E officer", "January"], ["Pause new districts", "Partly", "Pause for one year", "Board", "Next meeting"], ["New training model", "Not accepted", "None", "", ""]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "aar": {"what": "A 30-minute team conversation after the work, using four questions, to learn what to repeat and what to change.", "why": "Teams repeat the mistakes they never named.", "when": "Within a week of the final report.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The team and the original workplan", "mistakes": ["Turning it into a search for someone to blame", "Ending without one written change"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["What was supposed to happen?", "From the workplan and proposal.", "Two days of staff training"], ["What actually happened?", "Facts only.", "Training took four days"], ["Why was there a difference?", "Causes, not people.", "Staff needed more practice on phones"], ["What will we keep?", "What worked.", "Hands-on practice with real forms"], ["What will we change next time?", "One or two concrete changes.", "Price training at four days"]]}, "nps": {"what": "Three short questions you ask every client at the end of an assignment, starting with how likely they are to recommend you.", "why": "Referrals are your best source of work. This tells you whether you will get them, and why.", "when": "In the final meeting or by email the week after.", "time": "10 minutes", "need": "Nothing but the courage to ask", "mistakes": ["Only asking the clients you think are happy", "Not calling anyone who scores 6 or below"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["How likely are you to recommend us to a colleague, from 0 to 10?", "Ask as written.", "9"], ["What is the main reason for your score?", "Listen; do not defend.", "Staff can run the dashboard alone"], ["What would have made it a 10?", "Your best improvement ideas come from here.", "A shorter final report"], ["Who else might benefit from work like this?", "Ask only if the score is 9 or 10.", "Two NGOs in the next district"]]}}, "GAME": [["Scope", "The consultant will also undertake any other tasks assigned by the programme team.", "", "scope", "Every unpriced task you agree to comes out of your margin or your quality. \"Any other tasks\" means unlimited work for a fixed fee.", "Ask for: \"Additional tasks will be agreed in writing as a change request, with revised days and fees.\""], ["Design", "The evaluation will assess the impact of the programme on household income across all 12 districts.", "25 days, no baseline", "impact", "Impact means change caused by the programme. Without a baseline or comparison, and with 25 days, nobody can measure it honestly.", "Propose: \"Assess contribution to income change in four districts, using monitoring data and case studies, and state the limits.\""], ["Payment", "Payment: 100% of the fee on approval of the final report by the programme manager.", "", "pay", "You fund the whole assignment, and approval can be delayed or tied to findings the client likes.", "Ask for milestones: 30% on signing, 40% on the inception report, 30% on submission of the final report."], ["Questions", "The evaluation will answer the 14 questions listed in Annex 2.", "20 working days", "many", "Fourteen questions in 20 days is about a day and a half each, including fieldwork. All get shallow answers.", "Propose: \"We will group these into five key questions, agreed with you at inception.\""], ["Independence", "Findings will be finalised after review and approval by the programme team.", "", "indep", "Approval of findings by the people being evaluated invites pressure to soften them.", "Ask for: \"The client reviews drafts for factual accuracy. The evaluator decides the findings.\""], ["Deliverables", "Deliverable: a high-quality evaluation report.", "", "vague", "Nobody can tell when \"high quality\" is done, so review rounds never end.", "Specify: \"A report of up to 30 pages with a 3-page summary, one round of written comments, and a findings workshop.\""], ["Timeline", "Proposals due in five days. Fieldwork to begin the following week and the final report in one month.", "", "time", "No time for inception, tool testing, ethics approval or recruiting a field team. Quality is lost before you start.", "Propose a realistic plan: two weeks of inception, three of fieldwork, three of analysis and reporting."], ["Eligibility", "Preference will be given to consultants who helped design the programme.", "", "coi", "People who designed a programme are judging their own work. Funders and boards will question the findings.", "Declare any past involvement. If you designed it, offer a learning review, not an independent evaluation."]], "GAME_OPTS": [["scope", "vague", "many"], ["impact", "time", "scope"], ["pay", "indep", "vague"], ["many", "time", "impact"], ["indep", "pay", "coi"], ["vague", "scope", "many"], ["time", "many", "pay"], ["coi", "indep", "impact"]], "GIVEGET": [["Budget is cut by 20%", "Fewer questions or districts, not lower quality"], ["Deadline is moved earlier", "An interim briefing now, the full report on the original date"], ["Client wants more questions", "A change request with days and fee, or drop others"], ["Client wants payment on approval", "Payment on submission, with a fixed review window"], ["Client wants to approve findings", "Fact-checking by the client; findings stay with the evaluator"]], "GLOBAL": [["UN rosters and long-term agreements", "Evaluation offices of UN agencies such as UNDP and UNICEF regularly call for roster members and long-term agreements. Once you are on one, you are invited to bid for specific evaluations.", "Watch UNEG's vacancies page and agency job sites; apply in your niche.", "https://www.unevaluation.org"], ["UN Global Marketplace", "The common procurement portal of the UN system, where tenders are published and suppliers register.", "Register and set alerts for evaluation and monitoring tenders.", "https://www.ungm.org"], ["Development bank systems", "Multilateral development banks run their own consultant registration and procurement systems for firms and individuals.", "Register with the banks that fund your sector and region.", ""], ["Teaming with firms", "Most large donor tenders are won by firms that must name key experts. Being on a firm's bench is the fastest route to team-leader roles.", "Send three firms in your niche a short note and your donor-format CV.", ""], ["Foundations and philanthropy", "Many foundations hire through networks and referrals rather than open tenders.", "Ask past clients for one introduction each, every year.", ""], ["Job boards", "Global boards such as ReliefWeb and Devex list consultancy calls from many organisations.", "Use them to find calls, then apply selectively to the ones that fit.", "https://reliefweb.int/jobs"]], "GLOSS": [["ToR", "Short for terms of reference: the client's description of the assignment."], ["proposal", "Your written offer: how you understand the problem, your approach, team, workplan and price."], ["inception report", "The detailed plan you agree with the client before collecting data. Once approved, it is the agreed plan for the rest of the work."], ["evaluation question", "A question the work must answer, such as 'Did incomes change, and for whom?'"], ["theory of change", "How and why a programme is expected to lead to change, step by step."], ["indicator", "Something you measure to see whether change is happening, such as average monthly income."], ["baseline", "The measurement at the start, so you can see change later."], ["evaluation matrix", "A table that links each question to indicators, data sources, methods and analysis."], ["OECD DAC criteria", "Six common lenses for judging a programme: relevance, coherence, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability."], ["management response", "The client's formal reply to each recommendation, with owners and dates."], ["terms of reference", "The client's description of the assignment: purpose, questions, deliverables, timeline and budget. Often called the ToR."], ["hypothesis", "A best guess at the answer, stated early so you can test it."], ["issue tree", "A diagram that breaks a big question into smaller ones with no overlaps or gaps."], ["MECE", "Mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive: no overlaps, no gaps."], ["pyramid principle", "Writing with the answer first, then reasons, then evidence."], ["scope creep", "Work that grows beyond what was agreed, without more time or fees."], ["change request", "A written agreement to change scope, with days, fees and dates."], ["milestone payment", "A payment tied to a deliverable or stage."], ["day rate", "The fee for one day of consulting work."], ["contingency", "A small reserve in the budget for the unexpected, often around 10%."], ["deliverable", "Something you hand over: a report, a tool, a workshop, a dataset."], ["counterfactual", "What would have happened without the programme, often estimated with a comparison group."], ["contribution", "How far a programme plausibly helped cause a change, alongside other factors."], ["attribution", "How much of a change can be credited to the programme alone."], ["triangulation", "Checking a finding across different sources, methods or people."], ["mixed methods", "Using both numbers and qualitative evidence to answer questions."], ["key informant interview", "An interview with someone who knows the programme or context well."], ["focus group discussion", "A guided discussion with a small group of people."], ["validation workshop", "A meeting where stakeholders review draft findings before the report is final."], ["independence", "The evaluator's freedom to reach findings based on evidence, without pressure."], ["conflict of interest", "A personal or professional interest that could bias your work."], ["utilisation-focused evaluation", "An approach that designs evaluations around their intended users and uses."], ["logframe", "A table linking activities, outputs, outcomes and indicators."], ["pre-wiring", "Sharing difficult findings privately before a group meeting."], ["retainer", "A fixed number of consulting days bought each month."], ["utilisation rate", "The share of your working days that are billed to clients."], ["action title", "A heading written as a full-sentence finding, not a topic."], ["RAPID", "Bain's decision roles: recommend, agree, perform, input, decide."], ["RAID log", "A list of risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies, each with an owner."], ["RAG status", "Red, amber or green: a one-word signal of whether work is on track."], ["colour team review", "A planned proposal review: pink for the plan, red for the full draft, gold for sign-off."], ["engagement charter", "A one-page agreement of objective, scope, roles, timeline and ways of working."], ["evaluability assessment", "A short study of whether, and how, a programme can be evaluated."], ["rubric", "A description of what different levels of performance, such as excellent, good, adequate and poor, look like for a criterion."], ["after-action review", "A short team review of what was planned, what happened, why, and what to change."], ["statement of work", "The part of a contract that defines scope, deliverables and acceptance."], ["roster", "A list of pre-approved consultants an organisation invites to bid for or take on assignments."], ["long-term agreement", "A framework contract that lets an agency hire you for specific tasks over several years without a new tender each time. Often called an LTA."], ["key expert", "A named person in a firm's bid whose CV is scored, such as the team leader."], ["team leader", "The evaluator responsible for the design, team, quality and final report."], ["VOPE", "A voluntary organisation for professional evaluation, such as a national evaluation association."], ["value for money", "Whether a programme makes good use of resources, often judged on economy, efficiency, effectiveness and equity."], ["contribution analysis", "A theory-based way to build a credible case that a programme contributed to change."], ["realist evaluation", "An approach that asks what works, for whom, in what circumstances and why."], ["developmental evaluation", "Evaluation alongside a team, in real time, to support innovation in complex settings."], ["BATNA", "Best alternative to a negotiated agreement: what you will do if there is no deal."], ["due diligence", "Checks a client or firm makes on you before contracting: registration, accounts, policies, references."], ["design effect", "How much a cluster sample's precision is reduced compared with a simple random sample; multiply the sample by it."], ["non-response", "People who cannot be reached or refuse; plan extra sample to cover them."], ["TDS", "Tax deducted at source: the share of your fee an Indian client deducts and pays to the tax department on your behalf."], ["GST", "Goods and services tax: charged on your fee if you are registered."], ["positioning", "A clear statement of who you help, with what problem and how, so people know what to call you about."], ["niche", "The specific combination of sector, method, client and place where you do your best work."], ["warm introduction", "An introduction to a new contact made by someone who knows you both."], ["steering group", "A small group of senior client staff, and sometimes partners, who review progress and make decisions at key points. Also called a steering committee or reference group."], ["ghost deck", "An outline of the final report or presentation, with draft headlines, written before data collection. Also called a ghost report."], ["M&E", "Monitoring and evaluation: tracking a programme as it runs, and judging what it achieved and why."], ["MEL", "Monitoring, evaluation and learning: M&E with a deliberate habit of using the evidence to learn and adapt. A MEL system is the set of indicators, forms, routines and people that does this."], ["commissioner", "The person or office that hires and manages an evaluation, such as a foundation's evaluation office."], ["self-help group", "A small group of people, often women, who save and borrow together. Groups often join into larger federations."], ["results framework", "A table or diagram of the results a programme aims for and the indicators that track each one."], ["Harvey ball", "A small circle filled a quarter, half, three quarters or fully, used to show a rating at a glance."], ["Net Promoter Score", "A 0 to 10 question on how likely a client is to recommend you, introduced by Fred Reichheld of Bain & Company."], ["probe", "A follow-up prompt in an interview, such as 'Can you give an example?'."], ["de-identification", "Removing names and other details that could identify a person from data before analysis or sharing."], ["DPDP Act", "India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, which sets legal duties for handling personal data."], ["purchase order", "A client's internal approval number for a purchase, which finance teams match to your invoice."], ["PAN", "Permanent account number: the Indian tax identity number you put on invoices."], ["IFSC", "Indian Financial System Code: the bank branch code a client needs to pay you."], ["consortium bid", "A bid made jointly by several firms or consultants, usually with one lead."], ["quick win", "A recommendation with high impact that is also easy to put into practice."]], "GO": [["Fit with your skills and sector", 3], ["Budget matches the work", 3], ["Client knows how findings will be used", 3], ["Timeline allows a proper inception", 3], ["No conflict of interest", 3], ["Payment terms you can live with", 3]], "GOOD_BAD": [["Summarising a long ToR and listing its risks", "Knowing the politics behind the ToR"], ["Drafting an issue tree and proposal outline", "Choosing the hypothesis worth testing"], ["Turning questions into a first evaluation matrix", "Judging what the budget can really buy"], ["First-pass coding of de-identified transcripts", "Deciding what a pattern means for the client"], ["Tightening a draft report and summaries", "Owning a finding in a difficult meeting"], ["Drafting templates: contracts, trackers, guides", "Replacing a lawyer or tax adviser"], ["Checking a draft against the ToR and the matrix", "Seeing client data safely, unless the tool and contract allow it"]], "GROWTH": [["Solo specialist", "You, with a sharp niche.", "Low overheads, full control.", "Income caps at your own days."], ["Associate network", "You lead; trusted freelancers join per assignment.", "Bid bigger without fixed costs.", "Quality varies unless you set standards."], ["Boutique firm", "A small core team with a shared method.", "Wins larger tenders; builds a brand.", "Payroll pressure between contracts."], ["Productised practice", "Repeatable services and tools, such as retainers, training or MEL as a service.", "Income that does not depend on every bid.", "Needs investment in products and marketing."]], "GUIDE": {"bidgate": {"what": "A short scorecard you fill in before you decide to write a proposal, like checking the weather before a long trek.", "why": "Without it you spend days on bids you cannot win or should not take, and say yes to work that hurts your reputation.", "when": "The day a terms of reference (ToR) arrives, before you write anything.", "time": "20 minutes", "need": "The ToR, your calendar and your day rate", "mistakes": ["Scoring after you have already decided to bid", "Ignoring payment terms because the topic is exciting"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Criterion", "Score 1 to 3", "Why this score"], "guide": ["Keep the same six criteria every time so you can compare bids", "1 = poor fit, 2 = workable, 3 = strong", "One line of evidence from the ToR"], "rows": [["Fit with your skills and sector", "3", "Livelihoods mixed methods: our core work"], ["Budget matches the work", "1", "Covers about 60% of the questions asked"], ["Client knows how findings will be used", "2", "Mid-term decisions named, but vaguely"], ["Timeline allows a proper inception", "2", "30 days in total; tight"], ["No conflict of interest", "3", "Never worked with this programme"], ["Payment terms you can live with", "1", "100% on approval of the final report"]], "blank": 6, "foot": "Total: ____ of 18. 15 or more: go. 11 to 14: go with conditions. Below 11, or any conflict of interest: no-go."}, "psw": {"what": "One page that pins down the exact question the client needs answered, before you choose any method.", "why": "Most failed assignments answered the wrong question very well. This page makes the right question visible and agreed.", "when": "After your first call with the client, before you price the work.", "time": "45 minutes", "need": "Your notes from the discovery call", "mistakes": ["Writing a topic ('our M&E system') instead of a question", "Leaving 'what is out of scope' empty"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Key question", "One specific, time-bound question. What must be answered?", "How can the board see each month whether children's reading improves, within 15 days of consultant time?"], ["Context", "What is happening now that makes this matter?", "New funder wants evidence; data is on paper; one keen staff member."], ["Criteria for success", "How will the client know it worked?", "Board reviews a reading dashboard every month; staff run it without help."], ["Scope", "What is in, and what is out?", "In: framework, forms, dashboard, training. Out: an evaluation of impact."], ["Decision-makers and stakeholders", "Who decides, who uses, who could block?", "Director decides; board uses; centre staff collect."], ["Constraints", "Time, money, data, politics.", "15 days, eight weeks, two staff, low-end phones."], ["Sources of insight", "Who and what already knows something?", "Funder's template, staff, two similar NGOs, current registers."]]}, "stakemap": {"what": "A list of everyone who can shape, use or block the work, sorted by how much power they have and how much they care.", "why": "Evaluations get blocked, ignored or attacked by people nobody consulted early.", "when": "At inception, and again before you share findings.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "An organisation chart and the ToR", "mistakes": ["Listing organisations instead of named roles", "Forgetting the people the programme serves"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Stakeholder", "Power", "Interest", "How you will engage them"], "guide": ["A role, not just an organisation", "High or low: can they change or stop the work?", "High or low: do they care about it?", "High-high: involve closely. High-low: brief. Low-high: keep informed. Low-low: update."], "rows": [["Foundation board", "High", "Low", "One-page brief at each gate"], ["Programme director", "High", "High", "Co-design questions; fortnightly check-ins"], ["Federation leaders", "Low", "High", "Validation workshop; share results back"], ["Women members", "Low", "High", "Plain-language summary in local language"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "tree": {"what": "A diagram that breaks one big question into three to five smaller questions, like the branches of a tree, each with your best early guess at the answer.", "why": "Without it, questions overlap, gaps appear, and fieldwork days go to things nobody needed.", "when": "While you write the approach section of a proposal.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your problem statement", "mistakes": ["Branches that overlap, so the same data is collected twice", "More than five branches, which usually means some are sub-questions"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Key question (the root)", "Copy it from your problem statement.", "Is the livelihoods programme on track, and what should change?"], ["Branch 1", "A smaller question. Together, the branches must fully answer the root.", "Is the model relevant to women's needs? Hypothesis: Activities match stated needs; some groups are left out"], ["Branch 2", "Then write your hypothesis: what you expect the answer to be.", "Did incomes change, and for whom? Hypothesis: Incomes rose in two districts, not in two others"], ["Branch 3", "No overlaps with other branches.", "What explains differences between districts? Hypothesis: Strong federations drive the gains"], ["Branch 4", "No overlaps with other branches.", "Will groups last after the programme? Hypothesis: Groups with federations will keep meeting"], ["Branch 5", "No overlaps with other branches.", "Is delivery efficient? Hypothesis: Cost per active member is reasonable but uneven"]]}, "options": {"what": "Offering the client three versions of the work at three prices, instead of one take-it-or-leave-it quote.", "why": "One price invites a yes or a no. Three options invite the question 'which one?', and clients often pick the middle.", "when": "When you write the budget section of a proposal.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your day rate and the problem statement", "mistakes": ["Options that differ only in days, not in value", "Making the cheapest option too thin to be useful"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Option", "What the client gets", "Days", "Price", "What is left out"], "guide": ["Name each by its value", "The outcome, not the activities", "Lead and team days", "Including expenses", "Be honest; it builds trust"], "rows": [["Essentials", "Framework and three forms", "9", "Your price", "Dashboard, training"], ["Standard", "Plus monthly dashboard and staff training", "15", "Your price", "Coaching after handover"], ["Full", "Plus three months of coaching", "21", "Your price", "Nothing in scope"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "colours": {"what": "Three planned check-ups of your proposal by someone else: pink for the plan, red for the full draft, gold before you send.", "why": "You cannot see your own blind spots. A panel will, and it will score you down for them.", "when": "While you prepare any important proposal.", "time": "Three reviews of 30 to 60 minutes", "need": "A colleague, the ToR and its scoring criteria", "mistakes": ["Holding the red team the night before the deadline", "Reviewers who read as friends instead of as the panel"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Review", "When", "Who", "The question they answer"], "guide": ["Pink, red or gold", "Days before the deadline", "Someone who did not write it", "Pink: is the plan right? Red: would we win? Gold: is it complete?"], "rows": [["Pink", "7 days before", "A senior colleague", "Do the outline and approach answer the ToR?"], ["Red", "4 days before", "Two people acting as the panel", "Score it against the criteria: what are the three weakest points?"], ["Gold", "1 day before", "You and a second reader", "Price, team, forms and annexes all correct?"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "sow": {"what": "The part of a contract that says exactly what you will deliver, how it will be accepted, and what happens when the client asks for more.", "why": "Unwritten extras eat your profit and your quality. A clear scope turns 'one more thing' into a decision with a price.", "when": "While you negotiate the contract.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your proposal and the client's contract template", "mistakes": ["Leaving 'any other tasks as assigned' in the contract", "No limit on rounds of review"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Deliverables", "List each one with format, page limit and number of review rounds.", "Inception report (15 pages, 1 review round); final report (25 pages plus annexes, 2 rounds)"], ["Out of scope", "Write what you will not do.", "Impact estimates; data collection in new districts"], ["Acceptance", "How a deliverable is accepted, and how long the client has to comment.", "Comments within 10 working days; silence means accepted"], ["Change process", "How extra work is requested, priced and approved.", "Written change request with days, fee and timeline effect, signed by the programme director"], ["Payments", "Milestones tied to submission, not approval.", "30% on signing, 40% on inception report, 30% on final report submission"]]}, "rapid": {"what": "A table naming who plays which role in each decision the evaluation feeds: who recommends, who must agree, who carries it out, who gives input and who decides.", "why": "Good recommendations stall when nobody knows who decides.", "when": "At inception, once you know which decisions the findings will inform.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The organisation chart and the list of decisions", "mistakes": ["More than one person in the 'Decide' column", "Listing everyone under 'Agree', which gives everyone a veto"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Decision", "Recommend", "Agree", "Perform", "Input", "Decide"], "guide": ["The decision the evidence informs", "Proposes the option", "Can block it; keep this short", "Carries it out", "Is consulted", "One person only"], "rows": [["Which recommendations to accept", "Programme director", "Finance head", "District managers", "Federation leaders", "Foundation director"], ["Whether to expand to new districts", "Programme director", "Finance head", "District managers", "Federation leaders", "Foundation director"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "charter": {"what": "One page that both you and the client sign, saying what you are doing together and how you will work.", "why": "Expectations drift during long assignments. A shared page brings everyone back to what was agreed.", "when": "In the first week after the contract is signed.", "time": "45 minutes", "need": "The contract and the proposal", "mistakes": ["Writing it in consultant jargon the client will not read", "Not getting it signed"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Objective", "One sentence, in the client's words.", "The board can see each month whether children's reading improves"], ["Deliverables", "What you will hand over.", "Results framework, three forms, monthly dashboard, two trained staff"], ["Roles", "Who does what on both sides.", "Director signs off; two staff own the system; consultant designs and trains"], ["Timeline", "Key dates and gates.", "Eight weeks; framework agreed in week 3"], ["Top risks", "Two or three, with what you will do.", "Staff time is scarce: train in two short sessions"], ["Ways of working", "Check-ins, response times, reviewers.", "Weekly 30-minute call; comments within five working days"]]}, "kickoff": {"what": "The first meeting with the client, and a week-by-week plan with checkpoints where the client reviews and approves.", "why": "Clients review late when they did not know they had to. A visible plan makes their role part of the schedule.", "when": "In week one.", "time": "1 hour meeting, plus 1 hour to plan", "need": "The contract and the evaluation questions", "mistakes": ["No client review time in the plan", "Checkpoints without a named approver"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Weeks", "Activity", "Checkpoint or deliverable", "What the client must do"], "guide": ["Group by stage", "What happens", "What gets approved", "Reviews, data, access"], "rows": [["1 to 3", "Desk review and inception", "Inception report signed", "Share documents; review in 10 days"], ["4 to 5", "Tools and ethics", "Ethics approval", "Introduce field teams"], ["6 to 9", "Fieldwork", "Fieldwork complete", "Arrange access to districts"], ["10 to 11", "Analysis", "Emerging findings session", "Attend; check facts"], ["12 to 14", "Report and validation", "Final report submitted", "Comment within 10 days"]], "blank": 5, "foot": ""}, "raid": {"what": "One running list of risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies, each with an owner and a next step.", "why": "Problems surprise you when nobody wrote them down. A reviewed list turns surprises into plans.", "when": "From inception, reviewed every week.", "time": "15 minutes a week", "need": "Your workplan", "mistakes": ["Entries without an owner", "Writing it once and never updating it"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Type", "Entry", "Owner", "Next action", "By when"], "guide": ["Risk: might happen. Assumption: we believe true. Issue: happening now. Dependency: we need it first.", "Plain sentence", "A named person", "What will be done", "A date"], "rows": [["Risk", "Monsoon cuts access to two districts", "Team lead", "Plan fieldwork there first; keep a phone survey fallback", ""], ["Assumption", "Programme records are complete for 2024 and 2025", "Client M&E officer", "Check a 5% sample in week 2", ""], ["Issue", "Federation list is out of date", "District managers", "Updated list by 20 October", ""], ["Dependency", "Ethics approval before fieldwork", "Evaluator", "Submitted; decision due 25 October", ""]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "ghost": {"what": "A draft of your final report's headlines, written before you collect any data, with the chart each headline will need.", "why": "Without it you collect data you never use and miss data you needed.", "when": "At inception, after the evaluation matrix.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "The evaluation matrix", "mistakes": ["Topic headlines such as 'Income', which say nothing", "Treating the guesses as conclusions: they are there to be tested"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Page", "Headline: a finding, not a topic", "Chart or evidence it needs", "Where the data comes from"], "guide": ["In report order", "Your best guess, as a full sentence", "Bar chart, quote, table", "Survey, records, interviews"], "rows": [["1", "Incomes rose most where federations are strong", "Income change by district and federation strength", "Household survey; records"], ["2", "Two districts saw little change", "Before and after by district", "Baseline; survey"], ["3", "Federated groups are likelier to last", "Share of groups meeting regularly", "Group records; FGDs"], ["4", "Federations cost less per member", "Cost per active member", "Budget data"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "pareto": {"what": "Ranking the analyses you plan, so the few that matter most get done first and best.", "why": "Time always runs out. Without a ranking, it runs out on the analyses that mattered.", "when": "Before analysis begins.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The evaluation matrix and the data you have", "mistakes": ["Doing analyses in the order data arrives", "Treating every analysis as equally important"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Analysis", "Could it change the answer?", "Effort", "Order"], "guide": ["What you will compare or calculate", "High or low", "Days", "Do high-impact ones first"], "rows": [["Income change by district", "High", "2", "1"], ["Income by federation strength", "High", "2", "2"], ["Cost per active member", "High", "1", "3"], ["Attendance trends", "Low", "1", "Later"], ["Training satisfaction", "Low", "0.5", "Later"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "status": {"what": "A five-line update you send the client every week, with one overall colour: red, amber or green.", "why": "Clients forgive delays they hear about early. They do not forgive surprises.", "when": "The same day every week.", "time": "15 minutes", "need": "Your workplan and RAID log", "mistakes": ["Always reporting green", "Not asking for a decision when one is needed"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Overall status", "Red: off track, needs a decision. Amber: at risk, action under way. Green: on track.", "Amber"], ["Why this colour", "One sentence.", "Fieldwork in two districts is delayed by rain; analysis is on schedule elsewhere."], ["Done this week", "Facts, with numbers.", "Household survey complete in two districts (210 of 400); 8 of 12 focus groups held."], ["Next", "With dates.", "Finish two districts by 14 November; first analysis readout on 21 November."], ["Risks and issues", "What could go wrong and what you are doing.", "If the delay passes two weeks, the draft report moves by one week."], ["Decision needed", "A clear choice for the client.", "Choose: accept a one-week later draft, or a smaller sample in the delayed districts."]]}, "harvey": {"what": "A label beside each finding saying how solid its evidence is: strong, moderate or limited.", "why": "Readers treat every finding as equally certain unless you tell them. Honest ratings build trust.", "when": "During analysis and report writing.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "Your findings and their sources", "mistakes": ["Rating everything as strong", "Changing the scale halfway through"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Finding", "Sources", "Strength", "Why"], "guide": ["One sentence", "Which data supports it", "Strong, moderate or limited", "Number and agreement of sources"], "rows": [["Incomes rose in two districts", "Survey, records, FGDs", "Strong", "Three sources agree"], ["Federations cause the gains", "Survey, FGDs", "Moderate", "Pattern is clear; other causes possible"], ["Groups will last", "FGDs, records", "Moderate", "Early signs only"], ["Cost per member is reasonable", "Budget data", "Limited", "One source, incomplete"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "pyramid": {"what": "Writing the answer first, then three to five reasons, then the evidence under each, so the titles alone tell the story.", "why": "Busy readers stop after the first page. If the answer is not there, they never see it.", "when": "When you outline the report or presentation.", "time": "1 hour", "need": "Your findings", "mistakes": ["Section titles that are topics, such as 'Background' or 'Findings'", "More than five supporting points"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Governing message", "The one-sentence answer to the key question.", "Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding."], ["Supporting title 1", "A full-sentence finding that proves part of the message.", "Incomes rose by about a fifth in two districts and were flat in two others"], ["Supporting title 2", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "The gains track federation strength more closely than any other factor"], ["Supporting title 3", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "Groups linked to strong federations are the most likely to last"], ["Supporting title 4", "Read all titles in a row: do they make the argument?", "Strengthening federations costs less per member than opening new districts"]]}, "prewire": {"what": "Talking privately to the people most affected by a hard finding before the big meeting.", "why": "People surprised in public defend themselves. People consulted in private engage, and often suggest the fix.", "when": "A week before the findings or validation workshop.", "time": "30 minutes per person", "need": "Your draft findings", "mistakes": ["Using it to negotiate the finding away", "Skipping the most senior person"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Person", "The finding that affects them", "What you will share", "What you want to learn"], "guide": ["A named role", "In one sentence", "Finding and evidence", "Their explanation and ideas"], "rows": [["District manager, District C", "Incomes did not rise in her district", "The data and the comparison", "What made her district different"], ["Programme director", "Expansion should pause", "The cost comparison", "What would make it acceptable to the board"]], "blank": 3, "foot": ""}, "impactease": {"what": "Sorting recommendations by how much they would help and how easy they are, into quick wins, major projects, fill-ins and drops.", "why": "A long unsorted list of recommendations gets ignored. A sorted one tells the client where to start.", "when": "When you draft recommendations, ideally with the client.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "Your draft recommendations", "mistakes": ["Scoring alone, without the client", "Labelling everything a major project"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Recommendation", "Impact 1 to 5", "Ease 1 to 5", "Group"], "guide": ["One action", "How much it helps", "How easy it is", "Quick win, major project, fill-in or drop"], "rows": [["Strengthen federations before expanding", "5", "3", "Major project"], ["Track income every six months", "4", "5", "Quick win"], ["Share results with members", "3", "5", "Quick win"], ["New training model", "2", "1", "Drop"]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "mresponse": {"what": "A table where the client formally replies to each recommendation: accepted, partly accepted or not accepted, with an action, owner and date.", "why": "Without it, reports are filed and forgotten. With it, use can be tracked.", "when": "When the final report is delivered.", "time": "1 hour with the client", "need": "The final recommendations", "mistakes": ["Writing the client's responses for them", "No date to check back"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Recommendation", "Response", "Action", "Owner", "By when"], "guide": ["As written in the report", "Accepted, partly, not accepted", "What they will do", "A named role", "A date"], "rows": [["Strengthen federations first", "Accepted", "Federation support plan", "Programme director", "March"], ["Track income every six months", "Accepted", "Add to monitoring form", "M&E officer", "January"], ["Pause new districts", "Partly", "Pause for one year", "Board", "Next meeting"], ["New training model", "Not accepted", "None", "", ""]], "blank": 4, "foot": ""}, "aar": {"what": "A 30-minute team conversation after the work, using four questions, to learn what to repeat and what to change.", "why": "Teams repeat the mistakes they never named.", "when": "Within a week of the final report.", "time": "30 minutes", "need": "The team and the original workplan", "mistakes": ["Turning it into a search for someone to blame", "Ending without one written change"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["What was supposed to happen?", "From the workplan and proposal.", "Two days of staff training"], ["What actually happened?", "Facts only.", "Training took four days"], ["Why was there a difference?", "Causes, not people.", "Staff needed more practice on phones"], ["What will we keep?", "What worked.", "Hands-on practice with real forms"], ["What will we change next time?", "One or two concrete changes.", "Price training at four days"]]}, "nps": {"what": "Three short questions you ask every client at the end of an assignment, starting with how likely they are to recommend you.", "why": "Referrals are your best source of work. This tells you whether you will get them, and why.", "when": "In the final meeting or by email the week after.", "time": "10 minutes", "need": "Nothing but the courage to ask", "mistakes": ["Only asking the clients you think are happy", "Not calling anyone who scores 6 or below"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["How likely are you to recommend us to a colleague, from 0 to 10?", "Ask as written.", "9"], ["What is the main reason for your score?", "Listen; do not defend.", "Staff can run the dashboard alone"], ["What would have made it a 10?", "Your best improvement ideas come from here.", "A shorter final report"], ["Who else might benefit from work like this?", "Ask only if the score is 9 or 10.", "Two NGOs in the next district"]]}, "position": {"what": "One page that pins down who you help, with what problem, how you do it, and what you will not do.", "why": "Clients and firms remember specialists. 'M&E consultant' is a category; a clear niche is a reason to call you.", "when": "Once a year, and whenever your work drifts or a profile feels generic.", "time": "60 to 90 minutes", "need": "Your last five assignments and what clients said", "mistakes": ["Choosing a niche so wide it describes everyone", "Listing adjectives ('passionate', 'expert') instead of proof"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Your niche: sector x method x client x place", "One word or phrase for each. Narrow is memorable; you can still take other work.", "Education x MEL systems x small NGOs x India"], ["The problem you solve", "In the client's words, not yours. What keeps them up at night?", "'Our funder wants evidence and our data is on paper.'"], ["Who you serve best", "The client you do your best work for, and the one you should refer on.", "NGOs of 10 to 60 staff with one keen data person"], ["Proof points", "Three facts someone could check: assignments, results, published work, roles.", "Set up MEL for a 40-centre programme in 15 days; staff now run it alone"], ["Your voice", "Three words for how you work and write.", "Plain, practical, warm"], ["What you are NOT", "Work you decline or refer on. This makes the rest believable.", "Not impact evaluations; not large-scale surveys"], ["Positioning statement", "I help [client] with [problem] by [approach], so that [result].", "I help small education NGOs build MEL systems their own staff can run."]]}, "profile": {"what": "A headline, about section, featured work and keywords for your LinkedIn page and a one-page web profile.", "why": "Commissioners, firms and peers usually look you up before they call. Your profile is often your first interview.", "when": "After you finish your positioning canvas, and after every major assignment.", "time": "2 hours the first time, 20 minutes a quarter", "need": "Your positioning canvas, CV and consent list", "mistakes": ["A headline that is a job title only", "Naming clients or showing results without their agreement"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Headline", "Role + niche + result, in under 20 words. Use the words clients search for.", "Evaluation team leader | Livelihoods and women's economic empowerment | Mixed methods"], ["About: the problem you solve", "Two or three lines, in the client's language.", "Programmes need evidence they can act on mid-way, not only at the end."], ["About: how you work, and your proof", "Your approach, then two or three checkable facts.", "Utilisation-focused, mixed methods; led evaluations in four states."], ["About: invitation", "What people should contact you about, and how.", "Talk to me about mid-term and final evaluations of livelihoods programmes."], ["Featured work", "Up to three items: a case study, a practice note, a talk. Only what you may share.", "Practice note: from 14 questions to five"], ["Recommendations to ask for", "Two or three people who saw your work closely. Say what you would like them to mention.", "Programme director: inception and use of findings"], ["Keywords and one-page web profile", "Methods, sectors, donors' terms and languages; then reuse the text for your web page.", "Mid-term evaluation, contribution analysis, SHGs, Hindi, Kannada"]]}, "casestudy": {"what": "A one-page, anonymised story of one assignment: the context, the question, your approach, what changed and your role.", "why": "Clients buy evidence that you have solved a problem like theirs. A case study shows it without breaking confidentiality.", "when": "Within a month of finishing an assignment, while details are fresh and the client is warm.", "time": "90 minutes, plus client review", "need": "Your final report, the client's feedback and written consent", "mistakes": ["Publishing before the client has agreed in writing", "Claiming the whole result when you were one of a team"], "kind": "form", "fields": [["Title: the change, not the topic", "A headline someone would read.", "From paper registers to a monthly reading dashboard"], ["Context and question (anonymised)", "Type and size of organisation, sector and region, with no names unless agreed; then what the client needed to know or do.", "A 25-person education NGO with 40 after-school centres: how can the board see each month whether reading improves?"], ["Approach", "Two or three lines on what you did, and why.", "Results framework, 12 indicators, three short forms, staff training"], ["What changed", "Observable change, with honest limits. What you cannot claim.", "Staff run the dashboard alone; the board reviews it monthly"], ["Client quote (with written permission)", "Their words, approved by them, with the attribution they chose.", "'Director, education NGO', as agreed"], ["Your role", "What you did, and who else contributed.", "Solo consultant, 15 days; two staff co-designed the forms"], ["Consent and anonymisation check", "Who approved the text, when, and for which uses.", "Director approved by email for web and proposals"]]}, "visibility": {"what": "Twelve months of publishing, speaking, teaching and giving back, planned one quarter at a time.", "why": "Expertise that nobody sees does not bring work. A small, steady plan makes you findable and trusted over time.", "when": "At the start of each year, reviewed every quarter.", "time": "60 minutes a year, 20 minutes a quarter", "need": "Your positioning canvas and a calendar of association events", "mistakes": ["Planning more than you can deliver, then stopping", "Posting about everything instead of your niche"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Quarter", "Publish", "Speak", "Teach", "Give back"], "guide": ["One theme for the year, from your positioning", "Practice notes, blog posts such as aea365 or BetterEvaluation, LinkedIn", "Association events, gLOCAL, regional and global conferences", "Webinars, workshops, guest sessions", "Reviewing, mentoring, task forces"], "rows": [["Q1"], ["Q2"], ["Q3"], ["Q4"]], "label_rows": true, "rh": 21, "foot": "Each quarter, ask: what did people read or attend? Who got in touch because of it? What will I drop?"}, "network": {"what": "A short list of the people who matter to your work, by tier, with what you can give each one and when you will next be in touch.", "why": "Most consulting work comes through people who know and trust you. A light, regular habit beats a burst of networking.", "when": "Set it up once, then spend 30 minutes on it every fortnight.", "time": "90 minutes to set up", "need": "Your contacts, past clients and recent event notes", "mistakes": ["Getting in touch only when you need work", "Asking for a referral before you have given anything"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["Person (role)", "Tier", "Last contact", "Something useful to give", "Next step and date"], "guide": ["A role you will recognise; keep personal data safe", "Client, past colleague, firm, commissioner, peer or mentor", "Date and what it was about", "An article, an introduction, feedback, an event", "One action, with a date"], "rows": [], "rh": 11, "nrows": 6, "foot": "Cadence: clients and commissioners every two to three months, peers and firms every quarter, mentors twice a year. Follow up within 48 hours of every meeting."}, "events": {"what": "A one-page plan for before, during and after an event, so a conference turns into relationships and learning.", "why": "Conferences cost time and money. Without a plan you collect cards; with one you leave with conversations to continue.", "when": "Four to eight weeks before an event, or when a call for sessions opens.", "time": "45 minutes before, 30 minutes after", "need": "The programme, the speaker list and your positioning", "mistakes": ["Pitching your services in every conversation", "Waiting a week to follow up"], "kind": "table", "cols": ["When", "Step", "Your plan", "Done"], "guide": ["", "", "Specific names, sessions and dates", "Tick"], "rows": [["Before", "Three goals for the event"], ["Before", "Five people or groups to meet, and why"], ["Before", "Session, poster or panel to submit"], ["During", "Sessions to attend and questions to ask"], ["During", "Conversations and same-day notes"], ["After", "Follow up within 48 hours"], ["After", "Share what you learned"]], "label_rows": true, "rh": 13, "foot": "Within a week: add new contacts to your relationship map, and note one idea for your visibility plan."}}, "HABITS": [["Answer first", "Lead with the conclusion, then the reasons, then the evidence. Busy clients read the first line.", "Minto; every top firm"], ["Hypothesis-driven", "Write a best guess on day one and design the work to prove it wrong. It focuses scarce days.", "McKinsey, BCG"], ["No overlaps, no gaps", "Break every question into parts that are mutually exclusive and together complete.", "McKinsey (MECE)"], ["80/20", "Find the few analyses that carry most of the answer. Do those brilliantly; drop the rest.", "Pareto; all firms"], ["So what?", "Every chart and finding must say what it means for the client's decision. Facts alone are not findings.", "McKinsey"], ["Pre-wire", "No surprises in the big meeting. Walk key people through hard news one to one first.", "McKinsey"], ["Obligation to dissent", "Say when you disagree, with the client or your own team lead. Silence is a quality risk.", "Marvin Bower, McKinsey"], ["Results, not reports", "Judge the work by the decisions it changes, not the pages delivered.", "Bain; utilisation-focused evaluation"]], "HERO": [["The consultant will also undertake any other tasks assigned.", "Open scope", "Additional tasks will be agreed in writing as a change request, with days and fees.", ["Scope", "Days", "Fee", "Sign-off"]], ["Assess the impact of the programme in 20 days.", "No design", "Assess contribution to outcomes in three districts, using existing baseline data and 12 case studies.", ["Question", "Design", "Data", "Limits"]], ["100% payment on approval of the final report.", "Payment risk", "30% on signing, 40% on the inception report, 30% on the final report.", ["Advance", "Milestone", "Final", "Invoice"]]], "HF_SURVEYS": "https://claude.ai/artifact/HBV2rpvrq4igVmi1TDXzvp", "HOWTO": [["Understand it", "Read what it is and why it matters, in plain words."], ["See the template", "Look at the blank template and how to fill each part."], ["Study the example", "Switch to the filled-in version from the playbook's cases."], ["Make your own", "Download the blank template, fill it in, and use AI to check it."]], "INCEPTION": ["Purpose, users and use", "Refined evaluation questions", "Evaluation matrix", "Design, methods and sampling", "Tools, as annexes", "Ethics and data protection", "Workplan and deliverables", "Risks and how you will handle them", "Report outline", "What you need from the client"], "INFLUENCE": "Evaluations influence people in ways beyond the formal report: through taking part in the process, through ideas that spread later, and through changes long after the work ends. Plan for use, and track influence.", "JOURNEY": ["Decide whether the job is right for you.", "Write a proposal that shows you understand the problem.", "Agree the contract: scope, money and the rules.", "Plan the work in detail with the client.", "Collect and analyse the evidence.", "Share the findings so people accept and act on them.", "Hand over, follow up, and learn for next time."], "JUDGE_CH": ["judgement", "Judgement and methods", "Judgement and methods.", "How top evaluators get from evidence to a verdict they can defend, choose a causal design that fits the question, and evaluate programmes that keep changing.", ["A judgement needs criteria and standards, not just data", "Match the causal design to the question and the budget", "Complex programmes need evaluation that adapts with them"]], "KINDS": [["MEL system set-up", "Results framework, indicators, forms, dashboards and routines", "10 to 30 days", "Process partner"], ["Baseline study", "The starting values for key indicators, often with a survey", "20 to 60 days", "Expert"], ["Monitoring or data review", "Checks data quality and how well the system works", "5 to 20 days", "Doctor"], ["Mid-term evaluation", "Is it on track, and what should change?", "30 to 80 days", "Doctor"], ["Final evaluation", "Did it work, for whom, and was it worth it?", "40 to 120 days", "Expert"], ["Impact evaluation", "What changed because of the programme, against a counterfactual", "Months, with a team", "Expert"], ["Learning facilitation", "Reflection workshops, after-action reviews, learning agendas", "3 to 15 days", "Process partner"], ["Capacity strengthening", "Training and coaching staff to run M&E themselves", "5 to 30 days", "Process partner"]], "KIT_CH": ["kit", "The kit", "The kit: calculators, templates and concepts.", "Everything you need to work on your own: six calculators for the numbers clients ask about, nine document templates you can download and fill, and the evaluation concepts explained in plain words.", ["Calculate samples, fieldwork, timelines and invoices yourself", "Start every document from a tested template", "Understand the concepts before a client asks"]], "KQ": [["Does the client already know exactly what they need?", "expert"], ["Will the client run this themselves after you leave?", "process"]], "KRES": {"expert": ["You are the expert", "Agree the question tightly, deliver the answer well, and check it is still the right question at inception."], "process": ["You are a process partner", "Your job is to build the team's ownership. Plan workshops, not just deliverables, and leave tools they can run."], "both": ["Expert with a handover", "Deliver the technical work, and build in coaching so the client can keep it going."], "none": ["You are the doctor", "Start with a short diagnosis before you prescribe. Price the diagnosis separately if the scope is unclear."]}, "LEAD_CH": ["leading", "Leading and growing", "Leading and growing.", "Top consultants lead teams, pass due diligence, negotiate well, run a room, get evidence used, and become known for one thing.", ["A firm is a promise of quality, not just more people", "Negotiate interests, not positions", "Plan for use, then build a body of work"]], "LEVELS": [["Associate", "Starting out", "Collect and clean data, run interviews, review documents and draft sections under a lead.", "Clean datasets, field notes, a desk review, and a lead who will vouch for you.", "Data collection, desk reviews, transcription and coding, survey support.", "Ask to draft one full report section and present it to the client."], ["Consultant", "Often 2 to 5 years", "Lead small assignments end to end: scope, tools, analysis, report and handover.", "Two or three pieces you led, two client references, and a niche you can name in one sentence.", "MEL system set-ups, monitoring reviews, small evaluations and learning reviews.", "Join a mid-size evaluation as deputy team leader."], ["Senior consultant", "Often 5 to 10 years", "Lead mid-size evaluations and small teams. Own the method, the client relationship and quality.", "Evaluations you led as team leader, a management response that was acted on, and a method you are known for.", "Mid-term and final evaluations, baselines, theory-based and mixed-methods studies.", "Bid as team leader on a donor or UN tender, alone or with a firm."], ["Lead evaluator", "Often 10 years and more", "Lead complex, multi-site or multi-country evaluations and quality-assure other people's work.", "Team-leader roles on donor or UN evaluations, a peer review or credential, and work you have presented or published.", "Country, thematic, portfolio and strategic evaluations; evaluation quality assurance.", "Win long-term agreements and roster places; review for evaluation offices."], ["Global expert", "A reputation, not a number of years", "Shape methods and standards, advise evaluation offices and boards, and develop the next generation.", "Invited roles, reference groups, journal or panel reviews, publications, keynotes, mentees who lead.", "Methods advice, meta-evaluations, evaluation policies, expert panels.", "Give back: mentor, write, and build the evaluation ecosystem."]], "LEVEL_NOTE": "Years are indicative. People move faster with deliberate practice, mentors and a clear niche, and slower without them.", "LIB": [["block", "Flawless consulting", "Consulting craft", "Peter Block; 4th edition, 2023", "Consulting runs in five phases, and contracting is the one most people skip.", "Entry and contracting, discovery and dialogue, analysis and the decision to act, engagement and implementation, and extension or termination. Block asks consultants to state their own wants and to share responsibility with the client.", "Before your next contract, write down what you need from the client and say it out loud.", ""], ["schein", "Helping roles", "Consulting craft", "Edgar Schein, Process Consultation and Humble Consulting", "You can help as an expert, a doctor or a process partner.", "Each role suits different client situations. Problems start when consultant and client assume different roles without noticing.", "Ask early: do you want an answer, a diagnosis, or help finding your own answer?", ""], ["trust", "The trust equation", "Consulting craft", "David Maister, Charles Green and Robert Galford, The Trusted Advisor, 2000", "Trust = (credibility + reliability + intimacy) ÷ self-orientation.", "Clients trust advisers who know their stuff, keep promises and listen, and who are not visibly selling. Self-orientation divides everything else.", "In your next meeting, ask one more question before offering any solution.", ""], ["bulletproof", "Seven-step problem solving", "Consulting craft", "Charles Conn and Robert McLean, Bulletproof Problem Solving, 2019", "Define, disaggregate, prioritise, plan, analyse, synthesise, communicate.", "A method from strategy consulting that works for evaluation questions: break the problem into a tree, cut what matters least, and only then plan analysis.", "Draw the tree for your evaluation questions before writing the methodology.", ""], ["pyramid", "The pyramid principle", "Consulting craft", "Barbara Minto, The Pyramid Principle", "Put the answer first, then the reasons, then the evidence.", "Readers understand faster when ideas are grouped under one governing thought. Situation, complication and question frame the answer.", "Rewrite your report summary so its first sentence is the answer.", ""], ["hypothesis", "Hypothesis-driven work", "Consulting craft", "Strategy consulting practice; Ethan Rasiel, The McKinsey Way, 1999", "Start with a best guess at the answer, then try to prove it wrong.", "A clear early hypothesis focuses data collection. The discipline is to drop it when evidence disagrees.", "Write a one-line hypothesis for each evaluation question at inception.", ""], ["mece", "No overlaps, no gaps", "Consulting craft", "Barbara Minto; consulting practice", "Group ideas so they do not overlap and together cover everything.", "Often called MECE (mutually exclusive, collectively exhaustive). It keeps question trees and report structures clean.", "Check that your evaluation questions do not double-count and leave nothing important out.", ""], ["prewire", "Pre-wiring", "Consulting craft", "Consulting practice", "Share hard findings one to one before the big meeting.", "People react better in private, and the meeting can then focus on decisions instead of defending positions.", "Before a findings workshop, call the two people most affected by the hardest finding.", ""], ["dissent", "The obligation to dissent", "Consulting craft", "Consulting practice", "You owe the client your honest view, especially when it is unwelcome.", "In evaluation, this is also independence: findings follow evidence, not the preferences of whoever pays.", "Write down in advance how you will respond if asked to soften a finding.", ""], ["change", "Change requests", "Contracts and money", "Project management practice", "Scope changes are agreed in writing with days, fees and dates.", "Without a change process, every new idea from the client comes out of your margin or your quality.", "Add a one-paragraph change clause to every proposal.", ""], ["tor", "Terms of reference", "Contracts and money", "Evaluation practice; EvalCanvas guidance", "A good ToR states the decision, few answerable questions, deliverables and review points.", "Vague purposes and long question lists produce proposals that cannot be compared and evaluations that disappoint.", "Read every ToR first for who will use the findings, and for which decision.", "https://evalcanvas.com/?tab=learn&subTab=practical-tools"], ["coi", "Conflict of interest", "Ethics and standards", "Evaluation standards and practice", "Past involvement with a programme can compromise an evaluation's credibility.", "Funders and boards will question findings if the evaluator helped design or deliver what is being judged.", "Declare any involvement in your proposal and let the client decide.", ""], ["indep", "Independence of findings", "Ethics and standards", "UNEG norms and evaluation practice", "Clients review for facts; evaluators decide findings.", "Independence is what makes an external evaluation worth paying for. Protect it in the contract, not in the final meeting.", "Put the factual-review rule in your contract.", ""], ["aiclause", "AI terms in contracts", "AI", "UK Evaluation Society, AI in Evaluation guidelines, November 2025", "Agree permitted AI uses, data rules and verification in the contract.", "The guidelines call for transparency, data protection, proportionate verification and meaningful human oversight, and suggest contracts set out permitted uses.", "Add a short AI clause to your standard proposal.", "https://evaluation.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/UK-Evaluation-Society-AI-in-Evaluation-Good-Practice-Guidelines-for-Practitioners-V-2.0-1125.pdf"], ["unegai", "Ethical AI in UN evaluations", "AI", "UNEG, 2025", "Five ethical principles for using AI in UN evaluations.", "Approved by UNEG in 2025 as a reference document for responsible AI use across the evaluation process.", "Read it before using AI on any UN-funded assignment.", "https://www.unevaluation.org/uneg_publications/uneg-ethical-principles-harnessing-ai-united-nations-evaluations"], ["llmcoding", "AI for qualitative coding", "AI", "Nga Than and colleagues, Sociological Methods and Research, 2025", "Generative models can code text about as reliably as human coders, on some tasks.", "Without examples, models reached F1 scores of about 0.70 to 0.83 against hand coding, comparable to human agreement, but results varied by task and data and needed validation.", "Hand-code a sample first, then use AI as a second coder and compare.", "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/00491241251339188"], ["ufe", "Utilisation-focused evaluation", "Evaluation design", "Michael Quinn Patton", "Judge an evaluation by its use by intended users.", "Identify the primary intended users early, design with them, and keep them involved through findings.", "At inception, name three people who will use the findings and ask what they will decide.", ""], ["realworld", "RealWorld evaluation", "Evaluation design", "Michael Bamberger and Linda Mabry, 3rd edition, 2019", "Every evaluation works under budget, time, data and political constraints.", "The approach helps you choose the strongest design those constraints allow, and to state its limits honestly.", "List your four constraints in the inception report, with how you will work around each.", ""], ["matrix", "Evaluation matrix", "Evaluation design", "Evaluation practice", "One table that links every question to indicators, sources, methods and analysis.", "It is the backbone of an inception report and the check that every method has a purpose.", "If a method appears in no row of the matrix, cut it.", ""], ["ghost", "Ghost report", "Evaluation design", "Consulting practice, adapted", "Sketch the final report before you collect any data.", "An outline with placeholder headlines shows which evidence each section needs, and exposes questions the data cannot answer.", "Draft the report table of contents at inception, with one placeholder finding per question.", ""], ["oecd", "OECD DAC evaluation criteria", "Ethics and standards", "OECD DAC, revised 2019", "Relevance, coherence, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability.", "The revised criteria come with principles for use: apply them thoughtfully, to suit the evaluation's purpose, not as a mechanical checklist.", "Choose the two or three criteria that matter most for this client's decision.", "https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/better-criteria-for-better-evaluation_15a9c26b-en.html"], ["unegethics", "UNEG ethical guidelines", "Ethics and standards", "UNEG, 2020", "Integrity, accountability, respect and beneficence.", "The guidelines set out ethical obligations for evaluators, commissioners and managers across the evaluation process.", "Use them as the ethics section of your inception report.", "https://uneval.org/document/detail/2866"], ["aea", "AEA guiding principles", "Ethics and standards", "American Evaluation Association, 2018", "Systematic inquiry, competence, integrity, respect for people, and common good and equity.", "A short, widely used statement of what evaluators owe clients, participants and the public.", "Check your proposal against the competence principle: do you have the skills, or a team member who does?", "https://eval.org/About/Guiding-Principles"], ["rainbow", "The Rainbow Framework", "Evaluation design", "BetterEvaluation", "Seven clusters of evaluation tasks: manage, define, frame, describe, understand causes, synthesise, report and support use.", "A planning checklist so no task is forgotten, with methods and resources for each.", "Use it to check your workplan has a line for every cluster.", "https://betterevaluation.org/plan"], ["triangulation", "Triangulation", "Evaluation design", "Evaluation practice", "Check findings across different sources, methods and people.", "Agreement across sources strengthens a finding; disagreement is itself a finding to explain.", "Mark in the matrix which findings rest on one source only.", ""], ["quality", "Fieldwork quality checks", "Evaluation design", "Survey practice", "Daily checks by interviewer, back-checks and supervision.", "Problems caught on day two cost little. Problems found at analysis cost the credibility of the whole study.", "Agree the daily checks with your field team before day one.", ""], ["onethree", "1:3:25 reports", "Reporting and use", "Canadian Health Services Research Foundation; BetterEvaluation", "One page of key messages, a three-page summary, 25 pages of detail.", "Different readers need different depths. This structure serves the board, the manager and the technical reviewer in one document.", "Write the one-page messages first, then build the rest under them.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/tools-resources/reader-friendly-writing-1325"], ["mresponse", "Management response", "Reporting and use", "Evaluation practice; EvalCanvas guidance", "For each recommendation: accept, adapt, defer or decline, with an action, owner and date.", "A declined recommendation should not disappear. The response makes follow-through visible.", "Offer a management response template with your final report.", "https://evalcanvas.com/?tab=learn&subTab=practical-tools"], ["feedbackloop", "Tracking use", "Reporting and use", "EvalCanvas guidance", "Decide in advance how you will know whether findings were used.", "Build a simple follow-up into the plan, such as a check-in after six months.", "Put a three-month check-in in your close-out email.", "https://evalcanvas.com/?tab=learn&subTab=comms"], ["shelf", "The shelf report", "Reporting and use", "EvalCanvas guidance", "Technically excellent, submitted and never read.", "It happens when evaluations serve accountability only, arrive too late, or have no owner for the recommendations.", "Ask at inception when the decision is, and deliver before it.", "https://evalcanvas.com/?tab=learn&subTab=comms"], ["dayrate", "Setting a day rate", "Contracts and money", "Freelance and consulting practice", "Your rate must cover unbilled days, overheads and gaps between contracts.", "Independent consultants bill far fewer days than they work once business development, admin, learning and gaps between contracts are counted.", "Use the rate builder in the workshop with your own numbers.", ""], ["milestones", "Milestone payments", "Contracts and money", "Consulting practice", "Tie payments to submission of deliverables, with an advance.", "It shares risk fairly and keeps cash flowing during long assignments.", "Never accept 100% on approval of the final report.", ""], ["niche", "Positioning", "Your practice", "Consulting practice", "Be known for one specific thing.", "A narrow niche (sector, method, client, place) makes you easy to recommend and lets you charge for depth.", "Write your niche as one sentence a past client could repeat.", ""], ["pipeline", "A pipeline", "Your practice", "Consulting practice", "Track leads, proposals and decisions in one place.", "A steady pipeline lets you decline bad work. A thin one makes every ToR look acceptable.", "Keep a simple list: lead, stage, value, next step, date.", ""], ["winloss", "Win and loss reviews", "Your practice", "Consulting practice", "Ask why you won or lost every bid.", "Clients often tell you, if asked politely. The answers improve the next proposal faster than anything else.", "After every decision, send one short question: what made the difference?", ""], ["associates", "Associates and teams", "Your practice", "Consulting practice", "Grow capacity without fixed costs.", "A trusted bench of researchers and field coordinators lets you bid for larger work. Agree quality standards and rates in writing.", "Name three people you would bring onto a larger bid.", ""], ["psw", "Problem statement worksheet", "Consulting craft", "McKinsey problem solving", "Fix the question before you fix the method.", "A one-page worksheet: key question, context, criteria for success, scope, decision-makers and stakeholders, constraints and sources of insight.", "Fill it in after your discovery call and send it to the client to confirm.", "https://umbrex.com/resources/mckinsey-problem-solving/"], ["rapid", "RAPID decision roles", "Consulting craft", "Bain & Company; Rogers and Blenko", "Clear decision roles make findings easier to act on.", "Recommend, agree, perform, input and decide. One person holds the D for each decision.", "Name the D for each decision your evaluation must inform, at inception.", "https://www.bain.com/insights/rapid-decision-making/"], ["colourteam", "Colour team reviews", "Contracts and money", "Shipley Associates", "Review proposals at planned points, as the client would.", "Pink team checks the plan and approach, red team scores a full draft against the evaluation criteria, gold team signs off price and compliance.", "Book a 45-minute red team with a colleague before every major bid.", "https://www.shipleywins.com/blogs/color-team-reviews"], ["valuefees", "Value-based fees", "Contracts and money", "Alan Weiss, Value-Based Fees", "Price on the value to the client, and offer a choice of yes.", "Weiss argues fees should reflect the outcome's value, not hours, and that three options at different values let clients choose how much to invest.", "Put three options in your next proposal and see which one the client picks.", ""], ["mendelow", "Power and interest map", "Consulting craft", "Aubrey Mendelow, 1991", "Engage people according to their power and interest.", "A two-by-two: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, monitor.", "Map stakeholders at inception and plan one engagement for each quadrant.", "https://www.open.edu/openlearn/digital-computing/successful-it-systems/content-section-4.4"], ["raidlog", "RAID log", "Consulting craft", "Project management practice", "Track risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies in one place.", "Each entry has an owner and a next action, and the log is reviewed at every status meeting.", "Start a RAID log in your inception report with at least five entries.", "https://asana.com/resources/raid-log"], ["actiontitles", "Action titles and horizontal logic", "Reporting and use", "Consulting practice; Cole Nussbaumer Knaflic", "If the titles alone tell the story, the report works.", "Each page or slide title is a full-sentence takeaway. Read in sequence they form the argument (horizontal logic); each page's content proves its own title (vertical logic).", "Read only the headings of your next draft aloud. Fix any that are topics, not findings.", "https://www.storytellingwithdata.com/blog/2013/12/horizontal-logic"], ["rubrics", "Evaluative rubrics", "Evaluation design", "E. Jane Davidson, Evaluation Methodology Basics", "Make the standard for 'good' explicit before you judge.", "Rubrics describe what excellent, good, adequate and poor look like for each criterion, so judgements are transparent and shared.", "Co-write one rubric with the client at inception for the most contested question.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/tools-resources/rubric-revolution"], ["evaluability", "Evaluability assessment", "Evaluation design", "Rick Davies, DFID Working Paper 40, 2013", "Check whether a programme can be evaluated, and how, before you commit.", "Looks at the programme's design, the availability of data, and the interest and capacity of stakeholders to use an evaluation.", "When a ToR asks for impact with no baseline, propose a short evaluability assessment first.", "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/57a08a0d40f0b652dd000534/61141-DFIDWorkingPaper40-finalOct13.pdf"], ["aar", "After-action review", "Your practice", "US Army; widely adopted", "Learn from every assignment in 30 minutes.", "Four questions: what was supposed to happen, what happened, why there was a difference, and what to keep or change.", "Hold one with your team within a week of every final report.", "https://www.odi.org/documents/1028/6431.pdf"], ["nps", "Client feedback score", "Your practice", "Fred Reichheld, Bain; Harvard Business Review, 2003", "One question predicts referrals: would you recommend us?", "Ask how likely the client is to recommend you from 0 to 10, and why. The follow-up conversation matters more than the number.", "Ask every client at the end of the assignment, and call anyone who scores 6 or below.", ""], ["pareto", "The 80/20 rule", "Consulting craft", "Vilfredo Pareto; consulting practice", "A few analyses carry most of the answer.", "Rank work by its effect on the key question and do the top items first and best.", "Before analysis, rank your planned analyses and share the top three results early.", ""], ["statusnote", "Weekly status note", "Consulting craft", "Consulting practice", "Short, regular, honest updates build reliability.", "Overall red, amber or green; progress; next steps; risks; decisions needed. Send it on the same day every week.", "Send one every Friday, even when nothing has changed.", ""], ["aeacomp", "AEA evaluator competencies", "Your practice", "American Evaluation Association, 2018", "Assess yourself against five domains.", "Professional practice, methodology, context, planning and management, and interpersonal. AEA offers a self-assessment.", "Rate yourself on each domain and pick two to grow this year.", "https://www.eval.org/About/Competencies-Standards/AEA-Evaluator-Competencies"], ["unegcomp", "UNEG evaluation competency framework", "Your practice", "United Nations Evaluation Group, 2016", "Know what UN evaluation offices expect.", "Competencies for evaluators, evaluation managers and heads of evaluation in the UN system.", "Before a UN roster call, map your evidence to the framework.", "https://www.unevaluation.org/document/detail/1915"], ["ce", "Credentialed Evaluator", "Your practice", "Canadian Evaluation Society", "A professional designation for evaluators.", "Based on education, experience and demonstrated competencies, with ongoing professional development.", "Check whether a credential would help in the markets you want to work in.", "https://ces.journals.uvic.ca/index.php/cjpe/article/view/585"], ["vepr", "Voluntary Evaluator Peer Review", "Your practice", "European Evaluation Society; UK Evaluation Society", "Peers review your practice and your development plan.", "A structured review against a capabilities framework, designed to support professional growth.", "Use its framework as a checklist even before you apply.", "https://europeanevaluation.org/voluntary-evaluator-peer-review/"], ["ipdet", "IPDET", "Your practice", "International Program for Development Evaluation Training", "Intensive training in development evaluation.", "Core and specialised courses held in Bern, for evaluators and commissioners worldwide.", "Plan one structured course in the next 12 months, then apply it at once.", "https://ipdet.org/"], ["glocal", "gLOCAL Evaluation Week", "Your practice", "Global Evaluation Initiative", "Present to a global audience at low cost.", "A yearly week of evaluation events organised locally around the world.", "Propose one session with a colleague or your association.", "https://glocalevalweek.org/node/62202"], ["vope", "Evaluation associations and EvalYouth", "Your practice", "IOCE; EvalPartners; EvalYouth Global Network", "Your network is your first market.", "National and regional voluntary organisations for professional evaluation, and EvalYouth for young and emerging evaluators, offer events, mentoring and visibility.", "Join one, volunteer for one task, and present within a year.", "https://evalpartners.org/?p=1428"], ["evalreason", "Evaluative reasoning", "Evaluation design", "Michael Scriven; E. Jane Davidson; Julian King", "A judgement needs criteria, standards and evidence, combined transparently.", "The logic of evaluation: decide criteria, set standards, gather evidence, and synthesise into a judgement others can follow.", "For your next evaluation, agree a rubric with the client before data collection.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/tools-resources/rubric-revolution"], ["vfi", "Value for money with rubrics", "Evaluation design", "Julian King; Oxford Policy Management", "Judge value for money across economy, efficiency, effectiveness and equity.", "An approach that combines explicit criteria, rubrics and mixed methods instead of a single ratio.", "Add a value-for-money question and rubric to your next inception report.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/tools-resources/value-for-investment"], ["contrib", "Contribution analysis", "Evaluation design", "John Mayne", "Build a credible case that a programme contributed to change.", "Test each link in the theory of change against evidence and rival explanations, and tell a contribution story.", "Use it when there is no comparison group but a clear theory of change.", "https://betterevaluation.org/node/2708"], ["process", "Process tracing", "Evaluation design", "Political science and evaluation practice", "Test whether specific steps caused a specific outcome.", "Sets out the causal chain and applies tests of how strongly evidence confirms or rules out each step.", "Use it for one or two high-stakes outcomes.", ""], ["realist", "Realist evaluation", "Evaluation design", "Ray Pawson and Nick Tilley, 1997", "What works, for whom, in what circumstances, and why?", "Explains outcomes through context and mechanism, and builds programme theories from mixed evidence.", "Ask 'for whom and in what circumstances' of every finding.", ""], ["harvest", "Outcome harvesting", "Evaluation design", "Ricardo Wilson-Grau and colleagues", "Collect changes first, then work back to the contribution.", "Identifies outcomes, including unexpected ones, verifies them with informed people, and analyses how the programme contributed.", "Use it for advocacy and networks where outcomes are hard to predict.", ""], ["msc", "Most Significant Change", "Evaluation design", "Rick Davies and Jess Dart", "Let people say which changes matter most.", "Stories of change are collected and selected through structured discussion at several levels.", "Add it alongside a survey to explain what the numbers mean to people.", ""], ["develeval", "Developmental evaluation", "Evaluation design", "Michael Quinn Patton, 2010", "Evaluation for innovation in complex settings.", "The evaluator works alongside the team, feeding back evidence in real time as the programme adapts.", "Offer it when a client is designing something new, not judging something finished.", ""], ["cla", "Collaborating, learning and adapting", "Reporting and use", "USAID Learning Lab", "Build learning into how a programme is managed.", "Collaborating with partners, learning on purpose and adapting plans, supported by culture, processes and resources.", "Propose one pause-and-reflect session each quarter.", "https://usaidlearninglab.org/node/29036"], ["dacquality", "DAC Quality Standards", "Ethics and standards", "OECD DAC, 2010", "The standards commissioners use to judge evaluation quality.", "Covers overarching considerations, purpose and scope, context, questions, methods, reporting, and follow-up.", "Check your report against them before you submit.", "https://www.betterevaluation.org/tools-resources/quality-standards-for-development-evaluation-dac-guidelines-reference-series"], ["unwomen", "Gender-responsive evaluation", "Ethics and standards", "UN Women Evaluation Handbook, 2022", "Evaluate how programmes affect gender equality and power.", "Guidance on integrating gender equality and human rights into every stage of an evaluation.", "Add a gender question to your next evaluation matrix.", "https://www.unwomen.org/en/digital-library/publications/2022/05/un-women-evaluation-handbook-2022"], ["influence", "Evaluation influence", "Reporting and use", "Karen Kirkhart, 2000", "Evaluations influence people beyond the report.", "Influence can come from the process or the results, can be intended or not, and can happen during, soon after, or long after the work.", "Plan products for each audience, and check back on influence later.", ""], ["yes", "Principled negotiation", "Contracts and money", "Roger Fisher and William Ury, Getting to Yes", "Negotiate interests, not positions.", "Separate people from the problem, focus on interests, invent options and use objective criteria; know your best alternative to a deal.", "Before your next fee discussion, write your walk-away point.", ""], ["ls", "Liberating Structures", "Reporting and use", "Henri Lipmanowicz and Keith McCandless", "Simple ways to involve everyone in a room.", "A set of facilitation methods such as 1-2-4-All and What, So What, Now What?", "Use one in your next validation workshop.", "https://www.liberatingstructures.com/history-of-ls"], ["dpdp", "India's data protection law", "Ethics and standards", "Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023; Rules 2025", "Personal data collected in India now has legal rules.", "Consent, purpose limits, security and deletion duties apply, with the Rules notified in November 2025 and phased in.", "Add consent, storage and deletion to your data management plan, and check with a legal adviser.", "https://static.pib.gov.in/WriteReadData/specificdocs/documents/2025/nov/doc20251117695301.pdf"], ["sampsize", "Sample size for surveys", "Evaluation design", "Survey statistics", "Precision depends on sample size, not on population size, for large populations.", "For a proportion: n = z² × p(1 − p) ÷ e², then adjust for design effect, small populations and non-response.", "Calculate before you price fieldwork, and state the margin of error in the inception report.", ""]], "LVL4": ["Aware", "Practising", "Leading", "Teaching"], "MATRIX_COLS": ["Evaluation question", "Sub-questions", "Indicators or judgement criteria", "Data sources", "Methods", "Analysis"], "MONEY": ["An advance on signing", "Payments on submission, not approval", "Invoice the day a milestone is met", "Check registration, GST and tax deduction rules with an adviser", "Keep three months of costs in reserve", "Never start fieldwork unpaid"], "MX_EX": [["Is the model relevant to women's needs and priorities?", "Do activities match what women say they need? Who is left out?", "Share of women rating activities as useful; groups excluded", "Survey; FGDs; programme records", "Household survey; 12 FGDs", "Compare by district and group age"], ["Did incomes change, and for whom?", "How much, in which districts, for which groups?", "Change in monthly income, self-reported, against baseline", "Baseline data; survey; FGDs; records", "Survey of 400 households; case studies", "Before and after by district; triangulate with FGDs"], ["What explains differences between districts?", "Role of federations, markets and staff?", "Federation strength score; market access; staff turnover", "KIIs; records; FGDs", "20 KIIs; document review", "Compare strong and weak federation areas"], ["Are groups likely to last after the programme?", "Can groups run without staff? What would they need?", "Groups meeting and saving without staff support", "Group records; FGDs; KIIs", "Group record review; FGDs", "Score groups on a sustainability rubric"], ["Is delivery efficient?", "Cost per active member; delays?", "Cost per active member; share of activities on time", "Budget and expenditure data; KIIs", "Cost analysis; KIIs with finance staff", "Compare costs across districts"]], "NEGO": [["Separate the people from the problem", "Be warm with the person and firm about the issue."], ["Focus on interests, not positions", "Ask why the client wants 30 days: a board meeting? Then solve for that."], ["Invent options for mutual gain", "Offer a short early briefing for the board and a full report later."], ["Use objective criteria", "Refer to day rates in comparable tenders, or the days the questions really need."], ["Know your walk-away point", "Decide before the call the minimum fee and scope you will accept, and your best alternative if there is no deal."]], "NICHE": [["Sector", "Education, health, livelihoods, climate..."], ["Method", "Surveys, qualitative, MEL systems, impact, learning..."], ["Client", "NGOs, foundations, CSR, government, multilaterals"], ["Place", "A state, a country, a region"]], "P": {"Choose": [["Read the ToR", "Summarise this ToR in ten lines: purpose, users, questions, deliverables, timeline, budget, payment terms. Then list every risk and gap: [paste ToR, no names].", "Which of these risks would make an experienced evaluator decline?"], ["Clarifying questions", "Draft five polite clarifying questions for the pre-bid window on these gaps: [gaps].", "Which question is most likely to change the budget?"], ["Go or no-go", "Score this assignment for fit, budget, client readiness and conflict of interest, from 1 to 3 each, with reasons: [summary].", "What would need to be true to move from no-go to go?"]], "Offer": [["Issue tree", "Turn these evaluation questions into an issue tree with no overlaps and no gaps, at most five branches: [questions].", "Which branch would a sceptical funder push on first?"], ["First hypothesis", "Given this programme summary, list three plausible hypotheses about what the evaluation will find, and the evidence that would disprove each: [summary].", "Which hypothesis would be most useful to the client if true?"], ["Proposal outline", "Draft a proposal outline with headings and one line each for: [assignment]. Keep it to five pages.", "What would make this proposal stand out from five others?"]], "Negotiate": [["Gap check", "Compare this draft contract with our proposal and list differences in scope, deliverables, payment and review rounds: [both, no names].", "Which difference costs us the most days?"], ["Change clause", "Draft a short, plain change-request clause for a consulting contract.", "How could the client read this clause against us?"], ["AI clause", "Draft a plain clause on permitted AI use, data protection and disclosure for an evaluation contract.", "What would a cautious client add to this?"]], "Set up": [["Evaluation matrix", "Build an evaluation matrix for these questions with columns: question, sub-questions, indicators or judgement criteria, sources, methods, analysis: [questions].", "Which row is weakest given [days] days of fieldwork?"], ["Ghost report", "Draft a report outline with a headline finding placeholder for each question, before any data: [questions].", "Which placeholder can our data not fill?"], ["Risk log", "List ten risks for this evaluation with likelihood, impact and response: [design summary].", "Which risk would we be embarrassed to have missed?"]], "Uncover": [["Interview guide", "Draft a 45-minute key informant interview guide for [role], covering these questions: [questions].", "Which question leads the respondent?"], ["Daily checks", "List daily quality checks for a household survey of [n] interviews with [k] investigators.", "Which check catches invented interviews?"], ["Second coder", "Act as a second coder. Using this codebook, code these de-identified excerpts and flag disagreements with the human codes: [codebook, excerpts].", "Where is the codebook definition too loose?"]], "Land": [["Pyramid", "Rewrite these findings as a pyramid: one governing message, three supporting points, evidence under each: [approved findings].", "Which supporting point is weakest?"], ["One-page summary", "Draft a one-page summary for a board from these approved findings and recommendations: [text].", "What would a board member ask first?"], ["Recommendations", "Make these recommendations specific, prioritised, with an owner and timeframe: [recommendations].", "Which recommendation is not feasible in the budget?"]], "Transfer": [["Management response", "Draft a management response table: recommendation, response (accept, adapt, defer, decline), action, owner, date: [recommendations].", "Which recommendation is likely to be quietly dropped?"], ["Handover guide", "Draft a two-page guide for staff to keep this monitoring system running: [system summary].", "What will break first when I leave?"], ["Feedback request", "Draft a short, warm email asking the client for feedback and a reference.", "How can I make it easy to say yes?"]]}, "PATHS3": [["I am completely new", "Follow the 30-day plan below, then do the practice assignment.", "#plan30", "Start the 30-day plan"], ["I have done some M&E work", "Go straight to the seven-stage method and the top-firm toolkit.", "#method", "Open the method"], ["I already consult", "Use the toolkit, the engagement studio and the bid desk on a live assignment.", "#toolkit", "Open the toolkit"]], "PIGS": ["#ff5a5a", "#ffde59", "#7ed957", "#cb6ce6", "#ff66c4", "#5fb7f0", "#1f1d22"], "PIPE": [["Repeat and referral", "Your best source. Ask every satisfied client for a referral and keep in touch."], ["Networks", "Evaluation associations, alumni and past colleagues. Be known for one thing."], ["Job boards and tenders", "Useful, crowded and price-driven. Bid selectively."], ["Content", "Short notes and tools that show how you think. They bring clients to you."]], "PLAN12": [["Months 1 to 3", "Foundations", ["Take the competency self-assessment and pick two gaps", "Join your national evaluation association and EvalYouth if you are early career", "Read the UNEG norms, the OECD DAC criteria and one evaluation a week", "Write your one-sentence niche"]], ["Months 4 to 6", "First proof", ["Take a second-chair role with a lead you respect", "Lead one small, well-scoped piece end to end", "Write a one-page case study of it, with permission", "Ask for two references while the work is fresh"]], ["Months 7 to 9", "Visible expertise", ["Publish one short practice note in your niche", "Present at an association event or during gLOCAL Evaluation Week", "Build a master CV in the key-expert format donors use", "Find a mentor one or two levels above you"]], ["Months 10 to 12", "Global entry", ["Register on UNGM and the consultant systems of two agencies or banks", "Apply to two roster calls or long-term agreements in your niche", "Join a firm's bid as a named expert", "Retake the self-assessment and set next year's two gaps"]]], "PLAN30": [["Week 1", "Learn the language", [["Read this Start here chapter and the ten words", "#start"], ["Take the test: spot the risk in eight ToR lines", "#test"], ["Read what kind of consulting this is, and meet the two examples", "#kinds"], ["Read the M&E essentials and open one link a day", "#essentials"]]], ["Week 2", "Learn the method", [["Walk through the seven stages with both examples", "#method"], ["Read why it works: trust, problem solving, standards", "#science"], ["Study three tools: bid gate, problem statement, issue tree", "#toolkit"], ["Fill in a problem statement in the studio", "#studio"]]], ["Week 3", "Practise on a real-looking ToR", [["Do practice tasks 1 to 4 below", "#drill"], ["Compare each answer with the model answer", "#drill"], ["Use the ToR scanner on the practice ToR", "#workshop"], ["Write down three things you would ask the client", "#drill"]]], ["Week 4", "Get ready for real work", [["Do practice tasks 5 to 7", "#drill"], ["Rate yourself on the eight competencies", "#pathway"], ["Write your one-sentence niche", "#practice"], ["Start a master CV in the donor format", "#pathway"]]]], "POWERQ": ["Who chose the evaluation questions?", "Whose data is collected, and who owns it?", "Are respondents paid for their time, or only asked to give it?", "Who will see the findings first, and who last?", "Is the team from the region it evaluates?"], "PRACTICE": [["Decide whether to bid", "bidgate", "20 minutes", "Read the practice ToR. Score it on the six bid criteria and decide: go, go with conditions, or no-go.", "About 11 or 12 of 18: go with conditions. The conditions: fewer questions, a design that fits 'impact', milestone payments, independent enumerators, and a written change process instead of 'any other tasks'."], ["List the risks", "workshop", "15 minutes", "Paste the practice ToR into the ToR scanner. Then find any risk it missed.", "Open-ended scope ('any other tasks'), an impact claim with no comparison or baseline, payment of 100% on approval, approval of findings by the programme team, a vague deliverable, staff collecting data, all districts and beneficiaries, 30 days, and 10 questions."], ["Write three clarifying questions", "psw", "20 minutes", "Write three polite questions to send before the bid deadline. Each should change your design or price.", "1. Which decisions will the findings inform, and when? 2. Is there baseline or monitoring data on household income, and can we access it? 3. Could payment follow milestones (inception and final report submission), as is common practice?"], ["Fix the key question", "psw", "20 minutes", "Turn the ToR's purpose into one specific key question for a problem statement.", "Is the livelihoods programme on track to raise household income across the four districts, what explains differences between them, and what should change in the second half?"], ["Group the 10 questions", "tree", "30 minutes", "Group the ToR's 10 questions into no more than five branches with no overlaps, and write a hypothesis for each.", "Relevance (Q1); Income and savings change (Q2, Q3, Q7); What explains differences: groups, federations, training, markets (Q4, Q5, Q6, Q8); Sustainability (Q10); Efficiency (Q9). Hypothesis for branch 3: strong federations explain most of the difference."], ["Price it and propose payments", "workshop", "30 minutes", "Use the days and budget estimator with the mid-term preset. Adjust days for 30 calendar days, then set milestone payments.", "About 38 lead days and 29 team days do not fit into 30 calendar days with reviews: propose 14 weeks, or a smaller scope. Payments: 30% on signing, 40% on the inception report, 30% on submission of the final report."], ["Draft the first page of the report", "pyramid", "30 minutes", "Write a governing message and three supporting action titles, as if your hypotheses were confirmed.", "Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding. Supporting: incomes rose in two districts and were flat in two; the gains track federation strength; federated groups are likelier to last."]], "PREV": {"bidgate": ["rows", "Mid-term evaluation: bid score", [["Fit with skills and sector", "3"], ["Budget matches the work", "1"], ["Client knows how findings will be used", "2"], ["Timeline allows an inception", "2"], ["No conflict of interest", "3"], ["Payment terms you can live with", "1"], ["Total: 12 of 18", "Go, with conditions"]]], "psw": ["cols", "MEL set-up: problem statement", [["Key question", "Can the board see monthly whether reading improves, in 15 days?"], ["Success", "Board reviews a dashboard every month"], ["Scope", "In: forms, dashboard. Out: impact study"], ["Decides", "The director"], ["Constraints", "15 days, two staff"], ["Sources", "Registers, funder template"]]], "stakemap": ["grid2", "Mid-term evaluation: stakeholders", [["Keep satisfied", "Foundation board, Finance"], ["Manage closely", "Programme director, District managers"], ["Monitor", "None"], ["Keep informed", "Federation leaders, Women members"]]], "tree": ["tree", "Is the programme on track, and what should change?", ["Relevant to women's needs?", "Did incomes change, for whom?", "What explains district differences?", "Will groups last?", "Is delivery efficient?"]], "options": ["cols", "MEL set-up: three options", [["Essentials", "Framework and forms · 9 days"], ["Standard (chosen)", "Plus dashboard and training · 15 days"], ["Full", "Plus 3 months of coaching · 21 days"]]], "colours": ["cols", "Mid-term bid: review plan", [["Pink · day 3", "Outline, win themes, approach"], ["Red · day 8", "Full draft scored as the panel"], ["Gold · day 10", "Price, team, compliance"]]], "sow": ["rows", "Change request no. 1", [["Request", "Add two districts"], ["Extra days", "+6 (lead 2, team 4)"], ["Fee change", "+ revised budget attached"], ["Timeline effect", "Draft report one week later"], ["Approval", "Signed by programme director"]]], "rapid": ["rows", "Decision: which recommendations to accept", [["Recommend", "Programme director"], ["Agree", "Finance head"], ["Perform", "District managers"], ["Input", "Federation leaders"], ["Decide", "Foundation director"]]], "charter": ["cols", "MEL set-up: engagement charter", [["Objective", "Board sees reading progress monthly"], ["Deliverables", "Framework, 3 forms, dashboard, training"], ["Roles", "Director signs off; two staff own it"], ["Ways of working", "Weekly check-in; 5-day reviews"]]], "kickoff": ["rows", "Mid-term evaluation: workplan", [["Weeks 1 to 3", "Inception · gate: report signed"], ["Weeks 4 to 5", "Tools and ethics · gate: approval"], ["Weeks 6 to 9", "Fieldwork"], ["Weeks 10 to 11", "Analysis · gate: emerging findings"], ["Weeks 12 to 14", "Report and validation"]]], "raid": ["rows", "Mid-term evaluation: RAID log", [["Risk", "Monsoon cuts access to two districts"], ["Assumption", "Programme records are complete"], ["Issue", "Federation list out of date"], ["Dependency", "Ethics approval before fieldwork"]]], "ghost": ["cols", "Ghost report: first four headlines", [["Page 1", "Incomes rose most where federations are strong"], ["Page 2", "Two districts saw little change"], ["Page 3", "Federated groups are likelier to last"], ["Page 4", "Federations cost less per member"]]], "pareto": ["rows", "Analyses ranked by effect on the answer", [["1. Income by district", "High"], ["2. Income by federation strength", "High"], ["3. Cost per active member", "High"], ["4. Attendance trends", "Low"], ["5. Training satisfaction", "Low"]]], "status": ["rows", "Week 6 status: Amber", [["Why", "Rain delayed two districts"], ["Done", "210 of 400 surveys; 8 of 12 FGDs"], ["Next", "Finish by 14 November"], ["Decision needed", "Later draft, or smaller sample?"]]], "harvey": ["rows", "Findings and strength of evidence", [["Incomes rose in two districts", "Strong"], ["Federations cause the gains", "Moderate"], ["Groups will last", "Moderate"], ["Cost per member is reasonable", "Limited"]]], "pyramid": ["tree", "Results follow strong federations: invest there before expanding.", ["Gains track federation strength", "Federated groups are likelier to last", "Federations cost less per member"]], "prewire": ["cols", "Before the findings workshop", [["Who", "District manager, area with no gains"], ["Share", "The finding and its evidence, privately"], ["Listen", "Her explanation and ideas"], ["Then", "She proposes the fix in the workshop"]]], "impactease": ["grid2", "11 recommendations sorted", [["Major projects", "4 · plan with owners"], ["Quick wins", "3 · start now"], ["Drop", "2 · low impact, hard"], ["Fill-ins", "2 · do if time allows"]]], "mresponse": ["rows", "Management response", [["Strengthen federations first", "Accepted · by March"], ["Revise income tracking", "Accepted · by January"], ["Pause new districts", "Partly accepted"], ["New training model", "Not accepted"]]], "aar": ["cols", "MEL set-up: after-action review", [["Planned", "2 days of training"], ["Happened", "Training took 4 days"], ["Why", "Staff needed practice on phones"], ["Next time", "Price training at 4 days"]]], "nps": ["rows", "End-of-assignment feedback", [["How likely to recommend us, 0 to 10?", "9"], ["Why?", "Staff can run the dashboard alone"], ["What would make it a 10?", "A shorter final report"], ["Referral", "Two NGOs introduced"]]]}, "PRICING": [["Day rate", "Simple and fair for uncertain scope. Clients see every day."], ["Fixed price per deliverable", "Rewards efficiency; needs a tight scope and a change process."], ["Retainer", "A set number of days a month for ongoing support. Smooths income."]], "PRINCIPLES": [["Contract the wants on both sides", "Say what you need from the client as clearly as what you will deliver. Responsibility is shared.", "Peter Block, Flawless Consulting", "block"], ["Trust is the product", "Credibility, reliability and intimacy build trust; putting yourself first destroys it.", "Maister, Green and Galford, 2000", "trust"], ["Answer first", "Start with the governing thought, then the reasons, then the evidence.", "Barbara Minto, The Pyramid Principle", "pyramid"], ["Design within real constraints", "Budget, time, data and politics always limit an evaluation. Name them and design around them.", "Bamberger and Mabry, 2019", "realworld"]], "PROMISES": [["Client data stays where agreed", "Only approved tools, only de-identified data, as the contract says."], ["Every source is checked", "No reference, quote or number goes in unopened."], ["AI use is disclosed", "The method note says what AI did and who checked it."], ["Humans own the findings", "AI drafts and suggests; the evaluator decides and signs."], ["AI terms are in the contract", "Permitted uses, data rules and disclosure, agreed up front."], ["Saved time goes to the client", "More listening, sharper synthesis, or a lower price."]], "PROPOSAL": [["Understanding", "The problem in your words, the decision it serves, and what you heard between the lines."], ["Approach", "A first hypothesis, the question tree, design and methods, and their limits."], ["Workplan", "Stages, deliverables and dates, with client review time built in."], ["Team", "Who does what, with days, and why they fit."], ["Budget", "Days by role and stage, expenses, contingency and taxes, and the payment schedule."], ["Risks", "What could go wrong and what you will do about it."], ["Credentials", "Two or three similar assignments, with what changed because of them."]], "PSW": [["key", "Key question", "One specific, time-bound question. What must be answered?", "How can the board see each month whether children's reading improves, within 15 days of consultant time?"], ["ctx", "Context", "What is happening now that makes this matter?", "New funder wants evidence; data is on paper; one keen staff member."], ["succ", "Criteria for success", "How will the client know it worked?", "Board reviews a reading dashboard every month; staff run it without help."], ["scope", "Scope", "What is in, and what is out?", "In: framework, forms, dashboard, training. Out: an evaluation of impact."], ["who", "Decision-makers and stakeholders", "Who decides, who uses, who could block?", "Director decides; board uses; centre staff collect."], ["con", "Constraints", "Time, money, data, politics.", "15 days, eight weeks, two staff, low-end phones."], ["src", "Sources of insight", "Who and what already knows something?", "Funder's template, staff, two similar NGOs, current registers."]], "QA": [["Before fieldwork", "Tools pre-tested; enumerators trained; consent and safety plans in place."], ["During fieldwork", "Daily data checks by interviewer; back-checks on a sample; issues logged."], ["During analysis", "Second coder on a sample; findings traced to evidence; peer review of the matrix."], ["Before submission", "Red-team read; quality checklist; facts checked; sources opened."]], "QC": {"kinds": ["A client says \"Our data is always late. Fix our M&E.\" Which role are you probably playing?", ["Expert", "Doctor", "Process partner"], 1, "They know the symptom, not the cause. Diagnose before you prescribe."], "method": ["The client wants to add three questions after the inception report is signed. What do you do?", ["Add them quietly", "Use the change process: days, fees, sign-off", "Refuse outright"], 1, "Changes are normal. Unpriced changes are not."], "science": ["In the trust equation, what most quickly destroys trust?", ["Low credibility", "High self-orientation", "Low intimacy"], 1, "Clients forgive gaps in knowledge sooner than they forgive being sold to."], "guide": ["What belongs on the first page of an evaluation report?", ["The methodology", "Key messages: answers and actions", "The ToR"], 1, "Answer first. Methods go later, for the readers who need them."], "ai": ["A client's contract says nothing about AI. You want to code transcripts with it. What first?", ["Go ahead with de-identified data", "Agree it with the client in writing", "Use it and disclose later"], 1, "Silence in a contract is not permission. Agree it first."], "practice": ["Which source of work usually wins at the best price?", ["Open tenders", "Repeat clients and referrals", "Cold emails"], 1, "Trust already earned shortens the sale and reduces price pressure."], "toolkit": ["Your draft report's section titles are 'Background', 'Findings' and 'Recommendations'. What would a top firm change?", ["Add more charts", "Make each title a full-sentence finding", "Shorten the report"], 1, "Action titles let a reader get the whole story from the headings alone."], "studio": ["Fieldwork is two weeks late and the report date is at risk. What goes in this week's status note?", ["Nothing until it is fixed", "Amber, the cause, and a decision the client must make", "Green, to avoid alarm"], 1, "Reliability is part of trust. Flag it early and ask for the decision."], "pathway": ["You want team-leader roles on donor evaluations but have none yet. What is the fastest route?", ["Apply alone to large tenders", "Join a firm's bids as a named expert, then deputy team leader", "Wait until you have 10 years of experience"], 1, "Firms need named experts. Each bid builds the record that later makes you a team leader."], "start": ["A client asks for 'an evaluation report'. What are they really buying?", ["A long document", "Evidence that helps them make a decision", "Charts and tables"], 1, "The decision is the product; the report is only the vehicle."], "judgement": ["A ToR asks for 'impact' mid-way through a programme, with no baseline. Which design is most realistic?", ["A randomised trial", "Contribution analysis, testing the theory of change", "A satisfaction survey"], 1, "With no baseline or comparison group, a theory-based design is honest and feasible."], "leading": ["The client cuts the budget by 20% but keeps all 10 questions. What do you offer?", ["Do it all for less", "Fewer questions or districts, keeping quality", "Walk away immediately"], 1, "Trade scope, not quality. Give something, get something."], "kit": ["A client wants results by district for 4 districts, each with a margin of error of 5 points. What happens to the sample?", ["It stays the same", "It is roughly four times larger, because each district needs its own sample", "It halves"], 1, "Each subgroup you report on needs enough respondents on its own."], "brand": ["Which is the strongest proof point on a professional profile?", ["'Passionate and results-driven evaluator'", "'Set up MEL for a 40-centre programme in 15 days; staff now run it alone'", "'Over many years of varied experience'"], 1, "Proof is a fact someone could check. Adjectives are claims, and anyone can write them."]}, "QUAD": [["Keep satisfied", "High power, low interest: short briefs at key gates."], ["Manage closely", "High power, high interest: co-design, regular check-ins."], ["Monitor", "Low power, low interest: light updates."], ["Keep informed", "Low power, high interest: involve in validation and share results back."]], "QUALITY": ["The purpose, users and use are stated", "Questions match the ToR and the inception report", "The design and methods fit the questions, with limits stated", "Every finding traces to evidence", "Conclusions follow from findings", "Recommendations are specific, feasible and prioritised", "Gender, equity and human rights are addressed", "Ethics, consent and data protection are described", "Stakeholder views are represented fairly", "The executive summary stands alone"], "QUALITY_SRC": "Based on the OECD DAC Quality Standards for Development Evaluation (2010) and UNEG guidance on report quality.", "RAID_EX": [["Risk", "Monsoon cuts access to two districts", "Team lead", "Plan fieldwork there first; keep a phone survey fallback"], ["Assumption", "Programme records are complete for 2024 and 2025", "Client M&E officer", "Check a 5% sample in week 2"], ["Issue", "Federation list is out of date", "District managers", "Updated list by 20 October"], ["Dependency", "Ethics approval before fieldwork", "Evaluator", "Submitted; decision due 25 October"]], "RAPID_EX": {"dec": ["Which recommendations to accept", "Whether to expand to new districts"], "who": ["Programme director", "Finance head", "District managers", "Federation leaders", "Foundation director"], "grid": [["R", "A", "P", "I", "D"], ["R", "A", "P", "I", "D"]]}, "RAPID_ROLES": [["R", "Recommend", "Makes the proposal, gathers input"], ["A", "Agree", "Must sign off; can block"], ["P", "Perform", "Carries out the decision"], ["I", "Input", "Consulted for facts and views"], ["D", "Decide", "Makes the final call, one person"]], "READY_EVAL": ["Every question answered, in order", "Findings trace to evidence in the matrix", "Limits and constraints stated plainly", "A one-page summary on top", "Recommendations with owners and timeframes", "Factual comments addressed, findings kept", "Respondents cannot be identified", "Every source and quote checked", "AI use disclosed in the method note", "Data and tools ready to hand over"], "READY_MON": ["The decision behind the ToR is clear", "Your skills fit the sector and methods", "Budget and days match the questions", "Conflicts of interest declared or ruled out", "Clarifying questions asked", "A first hypothesis and question tree", "Days by role and stage, with contingency", "Payment milestones you can live with", "Change process and review rounds proposed", "AI and data use stated"], "REASON": [["Criteria", "Decide what matters: the aspects of performance you will judge.", "Income change, sustainability of groups, value for money."], ["Standards", "Describe what excellent, good, adequate and poor look like for each criterion, before you see the data.", "A rubric agreed with the client at inception."], ["Evidence", "Gather and weigh evidence against each standard, from more than one source.", "Survey, records, focus groups, cost data."], ["Synthesis", "Combine the ratings into an overall judgement, and show your reasoning.", "'Good overall: strong where federations exist, weak elsewhere.'"]], "REPORT": [["1 page", "Key messages: the answer to each question, and what to do."], ["3 pages", "An executive summary by question, with the main evidence."], ["25 pages", "Findings by question, methods, limits and recommendations. Annexes hold the rest."]], "RISKS": [["Client data in the wrong tool", "Pasting transcripts, names or unpublished reports into a tool the contract does not allow.", "Agree AI use in the contract. Use approved tools and de-identified data only."], ["Invented sources", "Confident references to reports, standards or statistics that do not exist.", "Open every source yourself. No citation goes in unchecked."], ["Proposals that read like everyone's", "Generic, polished text that shows no understanding of this client.", "Write the problem statement and hypothesis yourself, from the discovery call."], ["Over-trusted coding", "AI themes accepted without reading the transcripts.", "Code a sample by hand, compare, and adjudicate every disagreement."], ["Undisclosed use", "The client finds out later that AI drafted parts of the report.", "Say in the method note what AI did and who checked it."], ["Hours saved, value lost", "Racing to deliver faster and skipping the thinking the client paid for.", "Spend the time AI saves on more stakeholder contact and sharper synthesis."]], "ROLES3": [["Expert", "The client knows the problem and buys your skill to solve it.", "\"Design a sampling plan for our survey.\"", "Risk: you solve the wrong problem very well."], ["Doctor", "The client knows the symptoms and wants a diagnosis and a prescription.", "\"Our data is always late. Fix our M&E.\"", "Risk: the client never owns the cure."], ["Process partner", "You help the client find and solve the problem themselves.", "\"Help our team agree what success looks like.\"", "Risk: it feels slow to clients who wanted an answer."]], "RUBRIC": [["Income change", [["Excellent", "Average incomes rose by more than 20% in all districts, including the poorest members."], ["Good", "Rose by more than 15% in at least half the districts, with no group worse off."], ["Adequate", "Rose in some districts; some groups unchanged."], ["Poor", "No clear rise, or some groups worse off."]], "About 22% and 19% in two districts; flat in two; the poorest members gained least.", 2], ["Sustainability of groups", [["Excellent", "Most groups meet, save and lend without programme staff."], ["Good", "Most groups linked to federations do so; others still need support."], ["Adequate", "Only a few groups show signs of independence."], ["Poor", "Groups depend on staff visits to function."]], "Federated groups meet regularly; groups without federations decline when visits stop.", 1], ["Value for money", [["Excellent", "Cost per active member below comparable programmes, and falling."], ["Good", "Comparable to similar programmes, and stable."], ["Adequate", "Higher, but justified by reaching poorer women."], ["Poor", "Higher, with no clear reason."]], "Close to comparable programmes; higher in two remote districts that reach poorer women.", 1]], "SCQ": [["Situation", "The programme reached 6,000 women in two years."], ["Complication", "Incomes rose in two districts but not in the other two."], ["Question", "What explains the difference, and what should change?"], ["Answer", "Results follow strong group federations. Invest in federations before expanding."]], "SCRIPTS": [["Discovery call: ten questions", "Your first call with a client", "1. What decision will this work help you make, and when?\n2. Who will use the findings?\n3. What made you decide to do this now?\n4. What data do you already have?\n5. What would make this a success for you?\n6. What would make it a failure?\n7. Who else should I speak to?\n8. What is your budget range and timeline?\n9. How do you prefer to review drafts?\n10. Is there anything that would make you hesitate to hire an outside consultant?"], ["Clarifying questions email", "Before a bid deadline", "Subject: Clarifying questions on the [assignment] ToR\n\nDear [name],\n\nThank you for sharing the terms of reference. To prepare a strong proposal, could you help with three questions?\n\n1. [Question that changes the design]\n2. [Question on existing data]\n3. [Question on payment or timeline]\n\nWe would be grateful for a reply by [date]. Thank you.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your name]"], ["Polite decline", "When the bid gate says no", "Subject: [Assignment] proposal\n\nDear [name],\n\nThank you for inviting us to bid. After careful review, we have decided not to submit a proposal this time, as [the timeline does not allow the quality of work this evaluation deserves].\n\nWe would be glad to be considered for future work, and happy to share thoughts on [scope or timeline] if that would help.\n\nWith best wishes,\n[Your name]"], ["Change request email", "When the client asks for more", "Subject: Change request no. [1]: [short title]\n\nDear [name],\n\nThank you for the request to [add two districts]. We are happy to do this. It would mean:\n\n- Extra days: [6]\n- Fee change: [amount]\n- Timeline: [draft report one week later]\n\nIf you agree, please confirm by reply and we will proceed.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your name]"], ["Invoice reminder", "When a payment is late", "Subject: Invoice [number] for [milestone]\n\nDear [name],\n\nI hope you are well. A gentle reminder that invoice [number] for [the inception report], sent on [date], was due on [date]. I have attached it again for convenience.\n\nPlease let me know if anything is needed from my side to process it.\n\nThank you,\n[Your name]"], ["Feedback request", "In the week after the final report", "Subject: Two minutes of feedback?\n\nDear [name],\n\nThank you for the chance to work on [assignment]. Could you answer three quick questions?\n\n1. How likely are you to recommend us to a colleague, from 0 to 10?\n2. What is the main reason for your score?\n3. What would have made it a 10?\n\nThank you; your answers help us improve.\n\nBest regards,\n[Your name]"]], "SESS": {"small": [["you", "Here is a one-paragraph request from an NGO: \"Set up our M&E system and train staff.\" List what I need to know before I can scope this, as questions for a 30-minute discovery call."], ["ai", "Ten questions, grouped: who will use the data and for which decisions; what is collected now and how; who will run the system after you leave; budget and timeline; funder requirements. Ask first: \"What would be different in a year if this worked?\""], ["you", "Good. Which three answers would make me decline or rescope the work?"]], "large": [["you", "Here are 14 evaluation questions from a mid-term evaluation ToR (pasted, no names). Group them into no more than five key questions, mutually exclusive, and show which originals each covers."], ["ai", "Five groups: relevance of the model; changes in income and assets; what drives differences between districts; sustainability of groups; efficiency of delivery. Two originals overlap, and one asks for impact, which needs a comparison the ToR does not fund."], ["you", "Draft the clarifying question I should send about that impact question, politely."]]}, "ST": [["C", "Choose the work", "var(--kumkum)", "#ff5a5a"], ["O", "Offer", "var(--haldi)", "#ffde59"], ["N", "Negotiate the contract", "var(--patta)", "#7ed957"], ["S", "Set up the inception", "var(--jamun)", "#cb6ce6"], ["U", "Uncover the evidence", "var(--gulal)", "#ff66c4"], ["L", "Land the findings", "var(--aakash)", "#5fb7f0"], ["T", "Transfer and track use", "#ff9f43", "#ff9f43"]], "STAGES": [{"t": "Choose the work", "q": "Should we bid, and on what terms?", "one": "Choose before you chase.", "p": "Read the ToR for its real purpose, users and risks. Score the fit: your skills, the client's readiness, the budget against the work, and any conflict of interest. Ask clarifying questions before you bid. Saying no to a badly framed assignment protects your reputation.", "small": "A 30-minute discovery call showed the real need: the board wants to know whether children read better. The consultant scoped that, and declined to take on \"and any other tasks\".", "large": "The ToR scored 6 of 10. The consultant sent four questions in the pre-bid window and learned the budget could stretch by 20%. They decided to bid with a team.", "ai": "Summarise the ToR, list its risks and draft clarifying questions. Check every risk yourself.", "ids": ["block", "trust", "tor", "coi"]}, {"t": "Offer", "q": "Does the proposal show we understand the problem?", "one": "Lead with the answer you expect, then show how you will test it.", "p": "A strong proposal restates the client's problem better than they did, offers a first hypothesis, breaks the questions into a tree, and shows a workplan and budget that match. Price the work, not your hope of winning it.", "small": "A two-page proposal: the problem in one sentence, three deliverables, 15 days, a fixed price and a payment schedule.", "large": "The technical proposal grouped 14 questions into five, with a first hypothesis: \"Results are strongest where group federations are active.\" The budget showed days by role and stage.", "ai": "Draft an issue tree and a first proposal structure. Write the hypothesis and the price yourself.", "ids": ["bulletproof", "pyramid", "hypothesis", "mece"]}, {"t": "Negotiate the contract", "q": "Have we agreed what done looks like, and what happens when things change?", "one": "Contract the expectations, not just the price.", "p": "Agree the questions, deliverables, review rounds, payment milestones, data ownership, independence of findings and a change process, in writing. Say what you need from the client too: documents, access and timely feedback.", "small": "Three payment milestones (30, 40, 30), one review round per deliverable, and a one-line change process. The director agreed to name one staff member as the focal point.", "large": "The scope came down to five questions. The contract separated factual comments from findings, set payments on submission rather than approval, and agreed how AI may be used with data.", "ai": "Compare the draft contract with your proposal and list gaps. A person reads every clause before signing.", "ids": ["block", "change", "indep", "aiclause", "yes"]}, {"t": "Set up the inception", "q": "Does everyone agree on exactly what will be done?", "one": "Design for use from day one.", "p": "Meet the people who will use the findings. Turn questions into an evaluation matrix, sketch the final report before you collect anything, and settle sampling, tools, ethics, risks and the workplan. The signed inception report becomes your working contract.", "small": "A half-day workshop with staff agreed 12 indicators. The consultant drew the monthly dashboard on paper before any form was built.", "large": "The inception report had an evaluation matrix, a sample of 400 households, 12 focus groups and 20 interviews, ethics approval, a risk log and a \"ghost\" report outline. Signed in week three.", "ai": "Draft the evaluation matrix and a first report outline from the agreed questions. Check every method against the budget.", "ids": ["ufe", "matrix", "ghost", "realworld", "evalreason", "unwomen"]}, {"t": "Uncover the evidence", "q": "Will the evidence stand up when someone challenges it?", "one": "Collect only what answers a question, and check it every day.", "p": "Pilot tools, train the team, run daily quality checks and triangulate across sources. Keep a decision log. Share emerging findings with the client before the final meeting, so nobody is surprised.", "small": "Forms were piloted in four centres for two weeks. Two fields were dropped; the dashboard filled itself from the first month of data.", "large": "Daily checks caught one investigator rushing interviews. A mid-fieldwork call shared three emerging findings, so the foundation was ready for the hard one.", "ai": "Flag odd patterns in de-identified data and suggest first-pass codes. Humans adjudicate every code and finding.", "ids": ["triangulation", "prewire", "llmcoding", "quality", "contrib", "realist"]}, {"t": "Land the findings", "q": "Will the people who decide understand and accept the findings?", "one": "Answer first, then the evidence, then the detail.", "p": "Synthesise by question: what we found, so what, and what to do. Write in a pyramid, with a one-page summary on top. Validate findings with stakeholders, accept factual corrections, and keep the findings your own.", "small": "A four-page learning note and a working dashboard, walked through with the whole team in one meeting.", "large": "A 1:3:25 report and a validation workshop. One finding was challenged; the evidence held, and it stayed, with the programme's view noted.", "ai": "Draft summaries from your approved findings and tighten the language. Never let AI invent a finding or a quote.", "ids": ["pyramid", "onethree", "dissent", "oecd", "influence", "ls"]}, {"t": "Transfer and track use", "q": "Did anything change because of our work?", "one": "The assignment ends when the findings are used, not when the invoice is paid.", "p": "Help the client write a management response with owners and dates. Hand over data, tools and a short guide. Close the contract cleanly, ask for feedback, and check back in a few months.", "small": "A three-month check-in found staff running the dashboard alone. The director referred the consultant to two other NGOs.", "large": "The management response accepted 9 of 11 recommendations, each with an owner. The foundation invited the team to bid for the final evaluation.", "ai": "Draft the management response template and a handover guide from the final report.", "ids": ["mresponse", "block", "trust", "feedbackloop", "cla"]}], "STAKE_EX": [["Foundation board", 88, 30], ["Programme director", 82, 85], ["District managers", 55, 75], ["Federation leaders", 32, 90], ["Finance team", 62, 35], ["Women members", 20, 70]], "STANDARDS": [["OECD DAC criteria", "Relevance, coherence, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability, revised in 2019. Use them as a menu, not a checklist.", "oecd"], ["UNEG ethical guidelines", "Integrity, accountability, respect and beneficence, set out in 2020 for anyone evaluating with the UN.", "unegethics"], ["AEA guiding principles", "Systematic inquiry, competence, integrity, respect for people, and common good and equity, updated in 2018.", "aea"], ["Utilisation-focused evaluation", "Judge an evaluation by its use by intended users. Find them first and design with them.", "ufe"]], "STATUS_EX": {"rag": "amber", "why": "Fieldwork in two districts is delayed by rain; analysis is on schedule elsewhere.", "done": "Household survey complete in two districts (210 of 400); 8 of 12 focus groups held.", "next": "Finish two districts by 14 November; first analysis readout on 21 November.", "risk": "If the delay passes two weeks, the draft report moves by one week.", "dec": "Choose: accept a one-week later draft, or a smaller sample in the delayed districts."}, "STORY_EX": {"gov": "Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding.", "s": ["Incomes rose by about a fifth in two districts and were flat in two others", "The gains track federation strength more closely than any other factor", "Groups linked to strong federations are the most likely to last", "Strengthening federations costs less per member than opening new districts"]}, "SYSQ": [["Boundaries", "What is inside and outside the evaluation, and who decided?"], ["Perspectives", "Whose view of success counts, and whose is missing?"], ["Relationships", "Which connections between actors make change happen or block it?"]], "TARGETS": {"Consultant": [2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2], "Senior consultant": [3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2], "Lead evaluator": [4, 4, 3, 3, 4, 3, 3, 3], "Global expert": [4, 4, 4, 3, 4, 4, 3, 3]}, "TEAM": [["Team leader", "Owns design, quality, client and final report."], ["Specialists", "Methods, sector or gender experts for parts of the work."], ["Field coordinator", "Plans fieldwork, logistics, safety and daily checks."], ["Enumerators and facilitators", "Collect data to the protocol, with training and supervision."], ["Quality reviewer", "Reads drafts as the commissioner would, before the client does."]], "THEMES": {"Consulting craft": "var(--jamun)", "Contracts and money": "var(--haldi)", "Evaluation design": "var(--aakash)", "Reporting and use": "var(--patta)", "Ethics and standards": "var(--kumkum)", "AI": "var(--muted)", "Your practice": "var(--gulal)"}, "TITLE": "Consulting in Monitoring and Evaluation with AI", "TOOLKIT": [["bidgate", 0, "Bid or no-bid gate", "Shipley capture management", "A short scored review before anyone writes a proposal.", "The mid-term evaluation scored 12 of 18: go, with conditions. The conditions became the clarifying questions.", ["Score fit, client readiness, budget, timeline, conflicts and payment", "Set a threshold before you look at the ToR", "Write down why you said no, for next time"], "Score this ToR against six bid criteria from 1 to 3 and explain each score in one line: [ToR, no names].", "gate"], ["psw", 0, "Problem statement worksheet", "McKinsey", "One page that fixes the key question, the decision it serves and what success looks like, before any method is chosen.", "The MEL set-up's request was 'set up our M&E'. The worksheet turned it into: 'How can the board see each month whether children's reading improves, within 15 days and existing staff?'", ["Write the key question as one specific, time-bound sentence", "Fill in context, success criteria, scope, decision-makers, constraints and sources", "Agree it with the client before you price the work"], "Turn this request into a problem statement: one specific question, the decision it serves, success criteria, scope and constraints: [request].", "psw"], ["stakemap", 0, "Stakeholder power and interest map", "Mendelow, 1991", "A two-by-two that places people by their power over the work and their interest in it.", "In the mid-term evaluation, the foundation's board had high power and low interest: keep satisfied with a one-page brief. Federation leaders had high interest: involve them in validation.", ["List everyone who can shape, use or block the work", "Place each one on power and interest", "Choose an engagement for each quadrant: manage closely, keep satisfied, keep informed, monitor"], "From this ToR, list likely stakeholders and suggest where each sits on power and interest, with a reason: [ToR].", "grid"], ["tree", 1, "Issue tree and day-one hypothesis", "McKinsey and BCG", "Break the key question into sub-questions with no overlaps or gaps, and write your best early guess at the answer.", "The 14 ToR questions became a tree of five branches. Day-one hypothesis: 'Income gains follow strong federations.' The design was built to test it.", ["Put the key question at the root", "Split into three to five branches, each mutually exclusive", "Write a hypothesis for each branch and the evidence that would disprove it"], "Draft an issue tree for this key question with no more than five branches, then a testable hypothesis for each: [question].", "tree"], ["options", 1, "Three-option proposal", "Alan Weiss, Value-Based Fees", "Offer the client a choice of yes: three scopes at three prices, each tied to the value it delivers.", "The MEL set-up offered: essentials (framework and forms), standard (plus dashboard and training) and full (plus three months of coaching). The client chose standard.", ["Define the outcome the client values most", "Build three options that rise in value, not just in days", "Price each option, and say what the client gets and gives up"], "Suggest three proposal options for this assignment, each with scope, value to the client and what is left out: [summary].", "options"], ["colours", 1, "Colour team reviews", "Shipley Associates", "Planned reviews of a proposal at set points: pink for the plan, red for a full draft read as the client, gold for final sign-off.", "For the mid-term evaluation bid, a colleague did a 45-minute red team as the foundation's panel and caught that the budget page did not match the workplan.", ["Pink: check the outline, win themes and approach", "Red: score the full draft against the ToR's criteria, as the evaluator would", "Gold: confirm price, team and compliance before you send"], "Act as the evaluation panel. Score this draft proposal against the ToR's criteria and list the three weakest points: [proposal and ToR].", "colours"], ["sow", 2, "Statement of work and change control", "Project management practice", "A written scope with deliverables, acceptance rules and a change process, so that extra work becomes a decision, not a favour.", "When the foundation asked for two extra districts after inception, a one-page change request added 6 days and a revised fee. It was signed in a week.", ["List each deliverable with format, length and review rounds", "Say what is out of scope", "Agree how changes are requested, priced and approved"], "Draft a one-page change request for this new ask, with days, fee, timeline effect and the approval needed: [ask].", "sow"], ["rapid", 2, "RAPID decision roles", "Bain & Company", "Name who recommends, who must agree, who performs, who gives input and who decides, for each key decision.", "For the management response, the programme head recommends, finance must agree, district teams perform, federations give input and the foundation's director decides.", ["List the two or three decisions the evaluation must inform", "Assign one D per decision, and only a few As", "Put the roles in the inception report"], "For these decisions, propose RAPID roles (recommend, agree, perform, input, decide) from this organisation chart: [decisions and roles].", "rapid"], ["charter", 2, "Engagement charter", "Project management practice", "One page that both sides sign: objective, scope, deliverables, roles, timeline, risks and how you will work together.", "The MEL set-up's charter fitted on one page and named the two staff who would own the system after handover.", ["Write the objective in the client's words", "Add scope, deliverables, roles, timeline and top risks", "Agree ways of working: check-ins, response times, reviewers"], "Turn this proposal and contract into a one-page engagement charter: [proposal summary].", "charter"], ["kickoff", 3, "Kick-off and workplan", "Project management practice", "A first meeting that confirms the plan, and a workplan that shows stages, gates and client review time.", "The mid-term evaluation's kick-off agreed the five questions, the sample, review rounds of 10 working days and a steering group meeting at each gate.", ["Confirm questions, users, timeline and reviewers", "Show the workplan with gates and client review time", "Agree what the client must provide, and by when"], "Build a kick-off agenda and a week-by-week workplan with gates for this assignment: [summary].", "gantt"], ["raid", 3, "RAID log", "Project management practice", "One living list of risks, assumptions, issues and dependencies, each with an owner and a date.", "Top risk: monsoon access to two districts. Assumption: programme records are complete. Dependency: ethics approval before fieldwork.", ["Start it at inception with at least five entries", "Give every entry an owner and a next action", "Review it in every status meeting"], "From this inception plan, draft a RAID log with five risks, three assumptions, two issues and two dependencies: [plan].", "raid"], ["ghost", 3, "Ghost deck and report", "McKinsey", "Sketch the final report's headlines and charts before you collect data, then fill them in.", "Before fieldwork, the team wrote 12 headline placeholders, such as 'Incomes rose most where federations are strong', and designed tools to confirm or kill each one.", ["Write the report outline as action titles", "Sketch the chart each finding will need", "Check every tool question feeds a headline"], "From this evaluation matrix, draft a ghost report: ten action titles and the chart each needs: [matrix].", "ghost"], ["pareto", 4, "80/20 analysis plan", "Pareto principle; all firms", "Rank your analyses by how much each could change the answer, and do the top few first.", "Three analyses carried most of the mid-term evaluation: income by district, income by federation strength, and cost per active member.", ["List every analysis you could run", "Score each by its effect on the key question", "Do the top three first and share them early"], "Rank these planned analyses by how much each could change the answer to the key question: [list].", "pareto"], ["status", 4, "Weekly status note", "Consulting practice", "A five-part note each week: overall status in red, amber or green, progress, next steps, risks, and decisions needed.", "Week 6 of the mid-term evaluation was amber: two districts delayed by rain. The note asked the client to decide between a later report and a smaller sample.", ["Give one overall colour and say why", "List what was done and what is next", "Ask for any decision the client must make"], "Turn these notes into a weekly status note: overall red, amber or green, progress, next steps, risks, decisions needed: [notes].", "rag"], ["harvey", 4, "Evidence strength rating", "Evaluative rubrics (Davidson); consulting Harvey balls", "Rate each finding by how strong its evidence is, so readers can see what is solid and what is tentative.", "'Incomes rose in two districts' was rated strong: survey plus records plus focus groups. 'Federations cause the gains' was rated moderate.", ["Agree the rating scale at inception", "Rate each finding on sources, quality and consistency", "Show the rating next to the finding"], "For each finding below, rate the strength of evidence (strong, moderate, limited) and explain why in one line: [findings with sources].", "harvey"], ["pyramid", 5, "Storyline and action titles", "Barbara Minto; McKinsey", "A storyline where every page title is a full-sentence finding. Read only the titles and the whole argument still makes sense.", "The mid-term evaluation's title read: 'Results follow strong federations; invest in them before expanding.' Each supporting title proved one part of it.", ["Write the governing message in one sentence", "Write three to five supporting titles that prove it", "Test: read only the titles aloud"], "Rewrite these slide or section titles as full-sentence findings, so the titles alone tell the story: [titles and findings].", "pyramid"], ["prewire", 5, "Pre-wiring and validation", "McKinsey", "Walk the people most affected through hard findings one to one, then hold a validation workshop focused on decisions.", "Before the findings workshop, the evaluator met the district manager whose area showed no gains. In the workshop, she proposed the fix herself.", ["Find who is most affected by each hard finding", "Meet them privately first and listen", "Use the workshop to test findings and agree actions"], "Draft talking points for a one-to-one pre-wire on this difficult finding, including likely objections: [finding].", "prewire"], ["impactease", 5, "Impact and ease prioritisation", "Consulting practice", "A two-by-two that sorts recommendations by their impact and how easy they are to do.", "Of 11 recommendations, three were quick wins, four were major projects and two were dropped as low impact.", ["Score each recommendation on impact and ease", "Place them on the grid", "Lead with quick wins and plan major projects"], "Score these recommendations from 1 to 5 on impact and ease, and say which are quick wins: [recommendations].", "grid"], ["mresponse", 6, "Management response tracker", "UNEG; evaluation practice", "A table where the client accepts, partly accepts or rejects each recommendation, with an owner, action and date.", "The foundation accepted 9 of 11 recommendations. At the three-month check-in, six actions were on track.", ["Draft the template with the final report", "Help the client fill it in, without writing it for them", "Check back at three and six months"], "Draft a management response template for these recommendations, with columns for response, action, owner and date: [recommendations].", "mr"], ["aar", 6, "After-action review", "US Army", "A short team review of four questions: what was planned, what happened, why, and what to do next time.", "The MEL set-up's review found training took twice as long as planned. The next proposal priced it at four days, not two.", ["What was supposed to happen?", "What actually happened, and why?", "What will we keep, and change, next time?"], "Turn these notes into an after-action review: planned, actual, why, keep, change: [notes].", "aar"], ["nps", 6, "Client feedback score", "Bain (Net Promoter, Reichheld 2003)", "Ask one question at the end of each assignment: how likely is the client to recommend you, from 0 to 10, and why?", "The MEL set-up's director scored 9 and named two NGOs to refer. A 6 from another client led to a call that saved the relationship.", ["Ask every client, every time", "Always ask why, and listen", "Call anyone who scores 6 or below"], "Draft a three-question feedback form for the end of an assignment, including the 0 to 10 recommendation question: [assignment].", "nps"]], "TOOLS_BY_STAGE": [["bidgate", "psw", "stakemap"], ["tree", "options", "colours"], ["sow", "rapid", "charter"], ["kickoff", "raid", "ghost"], ["pareto", "status", "harvey"], ["pyramid", "prewire", "impactease"], ["mresponse", "aar", "nps"]], "TOOL_OUT": {"bidgate": "A go, no-go or go-with-conditions decision, with the reasons written down", "psw": "A one-page problem statement the client has agreed", "stakemap": "A stakeholder grid and an engagement plan for each quadrant", "tree": "An issue tree of three to five branches, each with a hypothesis", "options": "A proposal with three priced options", "colours": "A proposal reviewed and scored the way the client will score it", "sow": "A signed scope and a one-page change request form", "rapid": "A decision-roles table for the inception report", "charter": "A one-page charter both sides sign", "kickoff": "An agreed workplan with gates and client review time", "raid": "A living log with an owner and next action for every entry", "ghost": "A report outline written as headlines, with the chart each one needs", "pareto": "A ranked analysis plan with the top three analyses first", "status": "A five-line weekly note with one overall colour", "harvey": "A strength-of-evidence rating beside every finding", "pyramid": "A storyline whose titles alone carry the argument", "prewire": "Talking points, and a list of who to meet before the workshop", "impactease": "Recommendations sorted into quick wins, major projects and drops", "mresponse": "A response table with an owner and date for every recommendation", "aar": "A one-page review: what to keep and what to change next time", "nps": "A score, the reasons behind it, and a call to anyone who scored 6 or below"}, "TOOL_STUDIO": {"psw": "psw", "tree": "tree", "stakemap": "map", "rapid": "rapid", "pyramid": "story", "status": "status", "raid": "status", "bidgate": "workshop"}, "TORQ": ["Who will use the findings, for which decision?", "Which questions matter most, and can they be answered in the time?", "Is it monitoring, review, evaluation or impact?", "What data already exists: baseline, monitoring, reports?", "Is the budget stated, and does it match the work?", "What are the deliverables, page limits and review rounds?", "How is payment scheduled?", "Who owns the data and the report?", "Who approves what, and is independence protected?", "Are ethics approval or government permission needed?", "Any past involvement that could be a conflict?", "What happens if scope changes?"], "TOR_EX": "Terms of Reference: Mid-term evaluation of a women's livelihoods programme\n\nBackground: The programme supports 6,000 women in self-help groups across four districts.\n\nPurpose: To assess the impact of the programme on household income and to recommend improvements.\n\nThe evaluation will answer the following questions:\n1. Is the programme relevant to women's needs?\n2. Has household income increased?\n3. Have savings increased?\n4. Are groups functioning well?\n5. Are federations effective?\n6. Is the training of good quality?\n7. Has women's decision-making changed?\n8. Are market linkages working?\n9. Is the programme cost-efficient?\n10. Will groups be sustainable?\n\nScope: All districts and all beneficiaries.\nProgramme field staff will support data collection.\nThe consultant will also undertake any other tasks assigned by the programme team.\nDeliverable: a high-quality evaluation report.\nTimeline: 30 days from signing of contract.\nPayment: 100% of the fee on approval of the final report.\nFindings will be finalised after approval by the programme team.", "TOR_FLAGS": [["any other (task|dut|work)", "Open-ended scope", "Ask for extra tasks to be agreed in writing with days and fees."], ["\\bimpact\\b", "Impact claim", "Check there is a baseline or comparison, and budget for it. Otherwise propose contribution."], ["(100 ?%|full payment|entire fee).{0,60}(approv|final)", "Payment on approval", "Propose milestones: advance, inception, final submission."], ["approv\\w* .{0,40}(findings|report)|findings .{0,40}approv", "Approval of findings", "Separate factual review from the evaluator's judgement."], ["high[- ]quality report|comprehensive (evaluation|assessment|report)", "Vague deliverable", "Ask for page limits, structure and review rounds."], ["(programme|project|field) staff .{0,40}(collect|enumerat|interview)", "Staff collect the data", "Independent enumerators, or a clear plan to limit bias."], ["(designed|design of) (the|this) (programme|project)", "Possible conflict of interest", "Declare any past involvement."], ["all (districts|states|sites|regions|locations|beneficiaries)", "Very wide coverage", "Check days and budget allow it, or propose a sample."], ["within (one|1|two|2) weeks?|(\\d+|five|ten) days? (of|after|from) (signing|contract)", "Tight timeline", "Show a realistic plan with inception and review time."], ["intellectual property|all (rights|data) .{0,30}(vest|belong|property)", "Ownership terms", "Agree data ownership, storage and deletion; keep the right to use anonymised learning."]], "TREE_EX": {"q": "Is the livelihoods programme on track, and what should change?", "b": [["Is the model relevant to women's needs?", "Activities match stated needs; some groups are left out"], ["Did incomes change, and for whom?", "Incomes rose in two districts, not in two others"], ["What explains differences between districts?", "Strong federations drive the gains"], ["Will groups last after the programme?", "Groups with federations will keep meeting"], ["Is delivery efficient?", "Cost per active member is reasonable but uneven"]]}, "TRUST": ["Credibility", "Reliability", "Intimacy", "Self-orientation"], "TURN": [["Find the real decision behind the ToR", "Score fit, readiness, budget and conflicts", "Ask clarifying questions before you bid", "Walk away from work set up to fail"], ["Restate the problem in one sentence", "Offer a first hypothesis", "Break questions into a tree", "Show days by role and stage"], ["Deliverables with page limits and review rounds", "Payments on milestones, not approval", "A written change process", "What you need from the client"], ["Meet the people who will use the findings", "An evaluation matrix for every question", "Sketch the report before collecting data", "Sign off the inception report"], ["Pilot every tool", "Daily quality checks", "Triangulate across sources", "Share emerging findings early"], ["Answer first, then evidence", "A one-page summary on top", "Validate with stakeholders", "Keep factual and findings comments apart"], ["A management response with owners and dates", "Hand over data and tools", "Ask for feedback and a reference", "Check back in three months"]], "USEPLAN": [["Foundation board", "Decide on expansion", "One-page brief and a 15-minute presentation", "Before the board meeting"], ["Programme team", "Change delivery", "Validation workshop and full report", "Within two weeks"], ["Federation leaders", "Strengthen federations", "Local-language summary and a meeting", "Within a month"], ["Women members", "Know what was found", "Short audio or poster summary", "Within a month"], ["Wider sector", "Learn from it", "Practice note or webinar, with permission", "Within three months"]], "VFM": [["Economy", "Buying the right inputs at the right price.", "Were trainers and materials procured at fair cost?"], ["Efficiency", "Turning inputs into outputs well.", "Cost per group formed, per training delivered."], ["Effectiveness", "Turning outputs into outcomes.", "Did trained groups raise incomes?"], ["Equity", "Reaching those who need it most.", "Did the poorest women benefit, even if it cost more?"]], "VFM_NOTE": "Judge value for money with explicit criteria and a rubric across the four Es, using numbers and stories together. A single ratio rarely survives scrutiny.", "WHAT3": [["Who hires you", "NGOs, foundations, company CSR teams, governments, UN agencies and development banks, and the consulting firms that serve them."], ["What they buy", "An answer to a question they must decide on, a monitoring system that works, or skills their team keeps after you leave."], ["What you deliver", "Plans, tools, data, analysis, and a report or workshop that changes a decision. The decision is the product; the report is only the vehicle."]], "WORDS": [["ToR", "Terms of reference: the client's description of the job, its questions, deliverables, timeline and budget."], ["Proposal", "Your written offer: how you understand the problem, your approach, team, workplan and price."], ["Inception report", "The detailed plan you agree with the client before collecting data. It becomes the working contract."], ["Evaluation question", "A question the work must answer, such as 'Did incomes change, and for whom?'"], ["Theory of change", "How and why a programme is expected to lead to change, step by step."], ["Indicator", "Something you measure to see whether change is happening, such as average monthly income."], ["Baseline", "The measurement at the start, so you can see change later."], ["Evaluation matrix", "A table that links each question to indicators, data sources, methods and analysis."], ["OECD DAC criteria", "Six common lenses for judging a programme: relevance, coherence, effectiveness, efficiency, impact and sustainability."], ["Management response", "The client's formal reply to each recommendation, with owners and dates."]], "WORKFLOW": [["You frame", "The question, the client and the limits"], ["AI drafts", "Trees, matrices, outlines or summaries"], ["You judge", "Against the evidence and the client's reality"], ["AI revises", "And lists its assumptions"], ["Client reviews", "And you decide the findings"]]};
